[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb46238-0858-5e29-93f5-3b1abf6e8453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512264} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc4c62d-f986-5b1e-8753-4ec4676a3b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512311} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d76ec8-e500-538c-8442-85b23edc88a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512535}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512535} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6a7300-844d-5db4-8005-d85823b210da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9336c13e-c8cb-5577-898b-73fc979337d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{45(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512448} {:answer/id #uuid "9e473653-b9e6-5e7c-a302-54a5b439449d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512469} {:answer/id #uuid "dadc4114-78f9-5557-a713-9afe7cf481ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512469} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5322b59-ac05-57c5-bebd-3ff95532c124" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b98de4ab-a079-5d39-b165-ba7998caa236" :question/math-academy-id "q-343834" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{{field-2}}\\cdot \\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & \\,+5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n})\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n})\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n} &=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the third equation, \n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15}\\right)\\frac{n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${b}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{n\\pi }{15}\\cdot \\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot \\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot \\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05de7114-c6d2-5aa0-af39-01c4eb3c61be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512117} {:answer/id #uuid "71142195-0b10-524d-8d89-cc3312aea868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.29)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512368} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfe4040-0a28-5e2a-beae-ec384a40584e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.78)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512429} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d038d4-8c45-51ea-a946-61383d3a330e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.93)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512562} {:answer/id #uuid "e08790cd-6ba8-55b1-8612-1e73f81cd371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.34)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512117} :answer-field/id #uuid "99799375-0cad-5fd7-aafe-9d47d5e34722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ba83e244-060a-5dec-904e-55d3bc3fab0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-146974" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=88$ from a population where $44\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $52\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=88,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.44.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=88(0.44)=38.72>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=88(1-0.44)=49.28>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=88$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,\\frac{0.44(1-0.44)}{88}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,0.0028).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.52).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}>0.52) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.52-0.44}{\\sqrt{0.0028}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.51) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.51) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.51)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec85ad5-7ebb-57fe-b54b-9f5f21beff93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7673" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512127} {:answer/id #uuid "10502504-65e5-552d-8583-e710377ff51e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2266" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512132} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9307e2-adf7-5f81-ab85-5654801c0e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2327" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512355} {:answer/id #uuid "be94cbff-4f1f-52e7-8aca-b344e58fdc42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7995" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512557} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b89101-b002-5379-99aa-15ff76a8e86e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7734" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512127} :answer-field/id #uuid "160c9e56-fbfc-5c34-9ac0-b74ec90271f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c6ec9582-211a-54f7-b78a-6175feef090c" :question/math-academy-id "q-110256" :question/problem "A seller delivers bricks on pallets. One pallet contains $500$ bricks. The weight of a brick is normally distributed with a mean of $3\\,\\text{lb}$ and a standard deviation of $0.25\\,\\text{lb},$ while the weight of a pallet is normally distributed with a mean of $40\\,\\text{lb}$ and a standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{lb}.$ If one pallet with bricks is randomly selected, what is the probability that it weighs less than $1\\,545\\,\\text{lb}?$ You may assume that the weights are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th brick, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a pallet. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(3,{0.25}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 500,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY∼N(40,{4}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected pallet with bricks. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{500}+Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =500\\cdot 3+40 \\\\  & =1\\,540, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =500\\cdot {0.25}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =47.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(1\\,540,47.25).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W<1\\,545),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<1\\,545) & =P(Z<\\frac{1\\,545-1\\,540}{\\sqrt{47.25}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.73) \\\\  & =Φ(0.73)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.73)≈0.7673.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<1\\,545) & ≈Φ(0.73) \\\\  & ≈0.7673.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6edd5f67-1149-5905-9dfd-2531aac7d778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512362} {:answer/id #uuid "8efe1823-faf5-5f48-bcfa-08d5910e2be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512436} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4df0cb-ec84-5087-bbab-1841e915272d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512468} {:answer/id #uuid "caf882ed-b1ef-5c87-ab85-e0fb21914627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{96}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512588} {:answer/id #uuid "fb927e68-a451-56b0-abb4-b479e7119a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512706}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512468} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d1682f-7eb6-5691-9d8f-0cd7e762540d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf31f09e-0f29-56f8-9f97-abccd7a0062d" :question/math-academy-id "q-147844" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{7}{12}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{7}{12},$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right)\\left(\\frac{7}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822ed65c-479c-5147-9dc8-b41dd3fd0810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513210} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2f7804-b38c-5fdc-9e4a-2633c4a5d087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513210} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5a5f4e4-cfa4-5892-ad1b-880a9fe20d4d" :answer-field/key "$x\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35724131-51b1-5650-8379-cd697029ec72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513197} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ec01b4-8855-5eed-8d35-2e0b3a9270d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513197} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd93f924-7b0e-587d-a3b9-eb939063d9a7" :answer-field/key "$x\\in (0,1]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513223} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1fed61-97ad-5ade-aae2-213abe68defb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513187} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a584cd-4a11-57a0-97eb-a24cd1c40205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513236} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3a0f93f-1a9a-5ff5-9861-3ecab65a85cd" :answer-field/key "$x\\in [-1,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "64c7ad19-8343-5b7b-a647-af679630a146" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{2})^{2},$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval $x\\in [-1,0)$, $[-1,0)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $0\\le \\frac{1}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval $x\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{1}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval $x\\in (0,1]$, $(0,1]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{3}{4}$, both with output $\\frac{1}{16}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x\\in [-1,0)$; $x\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9e7e29d-dc45-5fb6-bcfc-e630b22d851e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513214} {:answer/id #uuid "d6fdd4d7-c2bc-5448-a362-fdc0305229e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/