86513463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dae8c946-1cc2-5ed8-b9a6-f478059f9a1a" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y={\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}^{x}-\\frac{9}{4}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/74/743dbb86aa003de162949be163c9e95347c8003907896f033a75dd759755eca1.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y={\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}^{x}-\\frac{9}{4},\n$$ we take the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}^{x}$ and shift it $\\frac{9}{4}$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,- \\frac{5}{4}),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=- \\frac{9}{4}.$\n\n![](images/11/1137ee2e0adbdc21f9fbb1a08a77c8977a2f8e85e561cb4f267ef1654eda4c8e.png)"} {:answer/id #uuid "89f23f80-b8ac-5ae5-88f7-06c168735fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(\\frac{8{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022f1a03-c389-5417-9840-269af635a05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sech}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513530} {:answer/id #uuid "755e0dee-1ad3-5455-a72b-95356420ea2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513841} {:answer/id #uuid "a72f2c7a-f375-5c68-a7ef-897ca237a31f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513963} {:answer/id #uuid "af7c9ea1-288c-5839-b4c5-25fe0687c0f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513993} {:answer/id #uuid "f04916b4-6e99-5195-a44d-8f7bbbffacb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514158}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513993} :answer-field/id #uuid "29223fdb-fde4-56b6-bbbe-c49644ecd755" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "16fea3d9-04d8-53ba-a81e-4ab7949c02d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-95671" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)-4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fed9afb-d84e-5a5c-9427-67c85f80b53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513617} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac49a99-6c9e-556a-a07b-1d7dbcc6149f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513819} {:answer/id #uuid "978426ac-e8fc-5307-a926-b42029a81429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513924} {:answer/id #uuid "bcbb33a9-826e-5e2e-b378-e4b369c0ac56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514034} {:answer/id #uuid "c2fb5695-2b16-5a62-9ddc-880edd81d5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514047}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513924} :answer-field/id #uuid "273b91a1-bbe7-504f-a91e-d8be18aa8ef2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "196ecf31-b7aa-5c38-b760-a54c4f34d305" :question/math-academy-id "q-96531" :question/problem "$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{csch}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{-2}}-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{2}^{-2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1}{4}-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-\\frac{15}{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f23f80-b8ac-5ae5-88f7-06c168735fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(\\frac{8{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513891} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb950154-2aa3-5ddf-9f65-38fa15780a80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "26db2697-717d-50e6-ae97-51a48de209b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-341279" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=8{x}^{2}+3$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=8{x}^{2}+3$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(8{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}+3x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{8{\\pi }^{3}}{3}+3\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{8{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06eef846-65a9-5990-acd0-b045e13d1cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{x}^{3/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513548} {:answer/id #uuid "393f1a37-77c6-5bbd-a580-db9adc219b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{-1/3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513680} {:answer/id #uuid "68568d39-43ec-5afd-b396-0468db1f01fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{x}^{-1/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513812} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ca349d-23a9-53ce-9e6b-be88f7c8ec44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{1/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513996} {:answer/id #uuid "c14ac74e-1927-56c7-a26e-5732f3c912ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{-3/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514042}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513812} :answer-field/id #uuid "eae9e030-db6a-548d-840a-748c2245c7ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "28f4ccc6-88e6-5461-934d-fc36db9c8ad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-29993" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e343d0-9e3d-57b4-a7bc-fc9b2686c5b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513556} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2855e0-fb0a-5301-b062-4ac8a822906c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513749} {:answer/id #uuid "81cf656b-bd3c-5484-968b-6e7bc93c98ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513870} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fe22da-80f5-5f8c-9080-2147a668ed55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{1/2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514005} {:answer/id #uuid "dec7850d-5fd9-5c84-b294-33569ce40c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{1/2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513556} :answer-field/id #uuid "42f6efb1-acc1-594f-9863-9843b604f60e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "34621fcb-1a0d-5d3e-9880-09e09830a08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-2024" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/2}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "877168ac-4c34-57ba-a846-5362369f5757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{5}{x}^{3/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513885} {:answer/id #uuid "943d70e7-e52b-597e-9871-44857a8a7ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}{x}^{5/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513915} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b24cd8-5808-5ff4-8dd5-6d0e70ccaa9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{5}{x}^{5/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514052} {:answer/id #uuid "e25dd75d-4458-58ea-a630-3ccbc3fecb7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}{x}^{3/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514125} {:answer/id #uuid "e355f008-e331-5495-83d3-a7df50fe8e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514130} :answer-field/id #uuid "15bc8d9e-bde6-57af-844a-0b82646dbe35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "37cc1eff-0240-5120-ba5e-14ada8bc6fca" :question/math-academy-id "q-29665" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{(\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/2}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25425771-e7b7-59e7-a21c-34da79a147e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{1/2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513636} {:answer/id #uuid "46f4c2a1-30c4-58bc-a872-acf707c7f214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{x}^{5/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513723} {:answer/id #uuid "bf5da9ff-937c-53a4-b1fa-cc6b757fbfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{1/2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514037} {:answer/id #uuid "dae23439-686a-536f-8883-85442f6a6ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{1/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514104} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7a9df8-4ded-54ff-9dd7-df5316a89579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2{x}^{5/2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513636} :answer-field/id #uuid "a255f30f-2d4d-5a18-8010-7066f9da2d12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3ce88619-593c-543d-be7b-78867655bb11" :question/math-academy-id "q-113703" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{1/2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041ce06a-3f86-5e30-a3fa-868512983162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2z}{1-{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513540} {:answer/id #uuid "30f00be7-84e1-5894-80e6-57a208b88c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513662} {:answer/id #uuid "69fcfaa8-1f10-5a22-a6a0-edbaf0ccf489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513817} {:answer/id #uuid "cf67f338-3bbd-5b2b-b73c-d6f9874a475c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{z}{{z}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514075} {:answer/id #uuid "d881232c-3fdd-51aa-bc26-114e5f59ead6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{z}{{z}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514097}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513662} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fdec476-5453-533f-ba1c-e55425c49aeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "558ac263-8cd5-56f3-9f79-c76511bfad24" :question/math-academy-id "q-125700" :question/problem "Given that $z>0,$ which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)?$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{z+(z)^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(z+\\frac{1}{z})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{{z}^{2}+1}{z})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75bfff0a-4c65-5fd2-b1ef-35a173aa2b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513842}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513842} :answer-field/id #uuid "826eda45-40bd-506b-a5bb-0ddbb36d06dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "60d8ee2b-aed9-5a87-bc1d-99d095c196bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-166072" :question/problem "${g}^{″}(x)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =8-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the second derivative, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514411 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f2a22da-8ee7-5c03-9406-a381bafad62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513698} {:answer/id #uuid "67813379-2e23-5017-8ad9-a1a5d968c331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513810} {:answer/id #uuid "82b05498-2c78-5f43-a343-c6dea0a978f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513873} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c794a4-ad11-517b-b863-482edd911ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514085} {:answer/id #uuid "f908b992-6ecb-5dd9-87da-ddd849714903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514183}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513810} :answer-field/id #uuid "63dec131-ea37-5a89-8bfa-e5223fe88c95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "65fa3d9f-f374-57d7-bfef-bf8f42da2e6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109732" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1}∼N(5,3)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(8,4)$ are independent and $Y=4{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2},$ calculate $P(Y<14).$ Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ | $\\,0.8830\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=4{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2} & ∼N(4{\\mu }_{1}-2{\\mu }_{2},16{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+4{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(20-16,48+16) \\\\  & ∼N(4,64).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =4$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{64}=8.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(Y<14).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<14) & =P(Z<\\frac{14-4}{8}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.25)≈0.8944.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y<14)≈0.89.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2011937d-5a96-5735-baa1-c9d00e438e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4292" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513618} {:answer/id #uuid "912af71c-ec6c-5acf-a218-4c537f9cb75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3944" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513908} {:answer/id #uuid "ac32782d-5d6e-552b-9ecc-ae443cf9c2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4015" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513984} {:answer/id #uuid "bca9eba0-cb3e-5af7-8eb5-bc98388caaf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514033} {:answer/id #uuid "edf7bc56-279f-5326-8543-b3671134dc41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4131" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513908} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb070c91-e2b2-5f1b-b37c-ab7d04905344" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "666db65e-d2dd-5df6-aa61-3e935a25f26e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109755" :question/problem "A factory produces shovels, each consisting of a handle and a blade.\nThe length of a handle is normally distributed with a mean of $120$ centimeters and a variance of $0.14,$  while the length of a blade is normally distributed with a mean of $30$ centimeters and a variance of $0.02.$ If one shovel is randomly selected, what is the probability that its length is more than $150$ centimeters, but less than $150.5$ centimeters? You may assume that the lengths are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ | $\\,0.9279\\,$ | $\\,0.9292\\,$ | $\\,0.9306\\,$ | $\\,0.9319\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the length of a handle, and let $Y$ denote the length of a blade. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(150<X+Y<150.5).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(120,0.14)$ and $Y∼N(30,0.02)$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & ∼N(120+30,0.14+0.02) \\\\  & ∼N(150,0.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =150$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.16}=0.4.$\n\nIn order to find $P(150<X+Y<150.5),$ we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(150<X+Y<150.5) & =P(\\frac{150-150}{0.4}<Z<\\frac{150.5-150}{0.4}) \\\\  & ≈P(0<Z<1.25) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.25)-P(Z\\le 0) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25)-Φ(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.25) & ≈0.8944,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0) & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(150<X+Y<150.5) & ≈Φ(1.25)-Φ(0) \\\\  & ≈0.8944-0.5 \\\\  & ≈0.3944.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that the length of a randomly selected shovel is more than $150$ centimeters, but less than $150.5$ centimeters is $0.3944.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f4e9c3e-c075-5ee8-b58c-ef68c609d2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e^x(\\cos x-\\sin x)}{8\\sin^2 x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513862}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513862} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba412a72-8abe-5840-811a-70be04c017e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "709bef3c-1deb-5177-94a0-6a0cd6bca192" :question/math-academy-id "q-166075" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right)\\,=\n$${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{16{\\sin }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{16{\\sin }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{8{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "569b842d-17a2-53d4-9b00-9b5178c0a749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e^x+12x^2-\\frac{x^4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513767} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a994c66-aac8-5b19-bc23-3cf37a49b2cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7323da16-6dcc-5329-81f1-5b6cb91bd332" :question/math-academy-id "q-166079" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\n$${{field-1}}\n$+C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "We combine the sum and the constant multiple rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+3(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{x}+24x-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+24\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+24\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ddf04c2-71d3-54b1-9f4b-d9699e9cc552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{12}}{13}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513693} {:answer/id #uuid "69f7fd71-6066-58ff-973d-5a24b0715dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513816} {:answer/id #uuid "a01e6e97-6d87-5937-8e57-076aaa390207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{12}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513942} {:answer/id #uuid "a8adc543-25c4-55be-8081-e8857819cce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{14}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513971} {:answer/id #uuid "f56d5cb2-21a5-51fe-8738-17f8affe9a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13{x}^{14}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513816} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8fbc5ea-5d9e-52eb-88d3-b4d6971c6c74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7c1117fb-96ed-5032-8c50-48772b44cf5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-113668" :question/problem "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{13}$ and ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=13$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{13+1}}{13+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{14}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{14})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{14}\\cdot 14\\,{x}^{13} \\\\ &= {x}^{13}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5576b01a-9d1c-5313-b50a-e30808c3d5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513764} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2af0f0-a818-54d9-b56d-5228585449fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513860} {:answer/id #uuid "80281663-e94f-539d-9b79-950c750311f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sech}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513866} {:answer/id #uuid "e6717f7c-2154-5695-905e-b3451522c467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514138} {:answer/id #uuid "fe09c074-c5d7-5c62-88d6-9836961da553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sech}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513860} :answer-field/id #uuid "14d5db5c-02d3-5b2c-b27c-ac3d6f3cb6ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "925db400-88a9-5795-a4b0-aad7734a8104" :question/math-academy-id "q-95674" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the results for the derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given function using these results, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7ed843-f01b-5ff0-9400-1f575ae46bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4817" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513604} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7b928a-dbf0-5c53-8e9c-221cc73604b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4925" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513688} {:answer/id #uuid "953e7d4e-e884-5c5e-bebe-5d6c15fc8f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4639" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513919} {:answer/id #uuid "a7650dd9-8eb4-5963-be58-a9d76c3de7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5153" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513965} {:answer/id #uuid "c072162b-92cd-5039-9d7e-cf458cf7fc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513688} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f73640d-d4b8-5379-a9d2-f83257d2b179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9dd9198d-6665-5e5e-bf17-48bb8a7b15be" :question/math-academy-id "q-109748" :question/problem "The weights of some chocolate cookies are normally distributed with a mean of $10\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $1\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}.$ The weights of some oatcakes are normally distributed with a mean of $15\\,\\text{g}$ and a variance of $2\\,{\\text{g}}^{2}.$  If one chocolate cookie and one oatcake are randomly selected, what is the probability that $6$ times the weight of the cookie plus $4$ times the weight of oatcake is less than $120\\,\\text{g}$, but more than $100\\,\\text{g}?$ You may assume that the weights of the cookies and oatcakes are independent. \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0062\\,$ | $\\,0.0060\\,$ | $\\,0.0059\\,$ | $\\,0.0057\\,$ | $\\,0.0055\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0082\\,$ | $\\,0.0080\\,$ | $\\,0.0078\\,$ | $\\,0.0075\\,$ | $\\,0.0073\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0107\\,$ | $\\,0.0104\\,$ | $\\,0.0102\\,$ | $\\,0.0099\\,$ | $\\,0.0096\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the weight of a chocolate cookie, and let $Y$ denote the weight of an oatcake. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(100<6X+4Y<120).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are  independent random variables such that $X∼N(10,1)$ and $Y∼N(15,2),$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6X+4Y & ∼N(6\\cdot 10+4\\cdot 15,36\\cdot 1+16\\cdot 2) \\\\  & ∼N(120,68).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $6X+4Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =120$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{68}.$\n\nTo find $P(100<6X+4Y<120),$ we convert $6X+4Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(100<6X+4Y<120) & =P(\\frac{100-120}{\\sqrt{68}}<Z<\\frac{120-120}{\\sqrt{68}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.43<Z<0) \\\\  & =P(Z<0)-P(Z\\le -2.43) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0)-P(Z\\le -2.43) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.43)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-2.43) & ≈0.0075,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0) & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(100<6X+4Y<120) & ≈Φ(0)-Φ(-2.43) \\\\  & =0.5-0.0075 \\\\  & ≈0.4925.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0387228b-625c-57a2-b9a5-65db0be7f989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513537} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8e57bc-7acc-5bac-a92c-82592c8e49c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513823} {:answer/id #uuid "78df9ad8-f292-595e-872f-5f8ef18126cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{x}^{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513850} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf0e947-1093-5e50-a47b-29c991bd99cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513977} {:answer/id #uuid "b224165e-2200-5c30-be67-560f8a090e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513998}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513977} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee20556c-e250-51aa-b85c-fc28797d2901" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a4e1eabf-8b25-5ba2-8660-9b7cb6a5610c" :question/math-academy-id "q-29994" :question/problem "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{4}$ and ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=4$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4+1}}{4+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} \\\\ &= {x}^{4}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3d08d87-5e72-5cee-a7d3-5556366a9ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514131} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fea91a9-b09a-5a02-bb27-07b1b9e3e60e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a717dc62-f0d0-579e-9ded-2923a5f96442" :question/math-academy-id "q-300677" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n\n$\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{5})^{-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}+(\\sqrt{5})^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{5}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "972c2fc3-94db-5577-8544-208982267693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2e}{{e}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513922} :answer-field/id #uuid "414b1934-a60c-5b00-b690-7baace00611d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa7fb149-1626-56db-aafd-3693bf4f703f" :question/math-academy-id "q-340820" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\operatorname{sech}⁡1.$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n\n$\\operatorname{sech}⁡1=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡1 & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{1}+{e}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(e+\\frac{1}{e})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{{e}^{2}+1}{e})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2e}{{e}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e707ecd-c178-55dc-854b-4e79cc7e337f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{t}}{\\sqrt{t}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513785} {:answer/id #uuid "95c44a7e-df43-50bc-a5cd-70761f467a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{t}}{t+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513920} {:answer/id #uuid "ea931382-e509-5b41-b3c4-5c291f5e42e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{t}}{t+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514145} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb5e7fd-8833-595e-8978-65b4198dc5b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{\\sqrt{t}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514149} {:answer/id #uuid "fc20631b-64e7-56b3-b072-c5a52e914f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{t+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513920} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ad40e26-871d-5735-a53a-4f67d90f045a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bc92dd53-1396-50af-a160-bf5a5b938587" :question/math-academy-id "q-125698" :question/problem "Given that $t>0,$ which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t})?$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t}) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t}}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{t}}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{t})^{-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}+(\\sqrt{t})^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{t+1}{\\sqrt{t}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{t}}{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "201fe908-79f1-5c96-8882-603760dea14f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513619} {:answer/id #uuid "3f49c5b4-7cf8-5c1b-9bb1-15cfaacad0b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513700} {:answer/id #uuid "45b310bb-a17f-5c57-88ef-88eaeb7ca1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513719} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb3285b-1658-5c85-a2b4-99659e2beceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513831} {:answer/id #uuid "92bbd547-69c8-54a5-adf2-467fb7d18b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513913}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513913} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e47ae0-7d67-5cf0-9030-1e73b5fe057a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cc4d5cf2-890e-5aaa-adb5-f60e0888df55" :question/math-academy-id "q-71405" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz)$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(yz))\\,k \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz))\\text{ }j+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(yz)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i+(-(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0))\\,j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2f634bf-1fab-5c5c-9faf-cca696e95edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e^{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514126}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514126} :answer-field/id #uuid "f49d4659-1c96-5a65-a285-84ed00e2cd31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d1eaed8d-b238-536c-b3a6-a6e7f9e94a12" :question/math-academy-id "q-166073" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\nSlope $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}+h\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2{e}^{\\pi }.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffcd5731-2069-5efb-ac00-2f8ff3414dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514208}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514208} :answer-field/id #uuid "cad4afc6-9656-5283-837f-a8416585d0c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f714102a-964b-532c-b041-8149ac6f2160" :question/math-academy-id "q-340828" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\operatorname{coth}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n\n$\\operatorname{coth}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2})=$\n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{coth}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}+1}{{e}^{2x}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{e}^{-2x}}{1-{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fourth definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1+{e}^{-2(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2})}}{1-{e}^{-2(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2}}}{1-{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{1}{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}}{1-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+\\frac{1}{4})}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}÷\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c3822e0-7f7b-5338-85bf-9c5070788c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513825}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513825} :answer-field/id #uuid "43939460-e1b0-596e-a7b7-ed6180d6c370" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fbda5c07-3c3f-5393-8bd3-32f9b514a1ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-166070" :question/problem "The function $p(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\np(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}k+10, & \\,x\\le 3, \\\\ g(x), & \\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFind the value of the constant $k$ that makes $p(x)$ continuous at $x=3.$\n\n$k\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the piecewise function\n\n$$\np(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}k+10, & \\,x\\le 3, \\\\ 8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}, & \\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nAt $x=3,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}p(x) & =p(3) \\\\  & =k+10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}p(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8(3)-\\frac{2}{3}(3)^{3} \\\\  & =24-18 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $p(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}p(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}p(x) \\\\ k+10 & =6 \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ when $k=-4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f84a058-98da-5a43-85a6-c9443bc774a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513657} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b6dda4-c668-5dd7-ba0c-f66c9657a070" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ff24eb5c-4023-53e8-863a-4c063695bc62" :question/math-academy-id "q-340819" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\operatorname{csch}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n\n$\\operatorname{csch}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{csch}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}}-{e}^{-(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}}-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(\\sqrt{5})}^{-1}}-{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{5}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}-\\sqrt{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1-5}{\\sqrt{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}})} \\\\  & =2\\times (-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f0cd083-b85f-5b73-be46-8de81ad9529e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514903} {:answer/id #uuid "3f987373-c5cf-510d-a27d-0b648d09125c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515010} {:answer/id #uuid "93e4a872-764c-5c44-8abe-38d12305d515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515184} {:answer/id #uuid "abb63ab6-3015-5a1d-98ac-a93a26fa894d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515233} {:answer/id #uuid "be75ab74-ecad-5cde-bccf-db980f3b0d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515276}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514903} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b988816-8ed1-5b88-adb7-733d9244bffa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07669a6a-a7d4-5af3-bbb0-d0ed7db93b62" :question/math-academy-id "q-14364" :question/problem "If the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(-5,3)$ and $(a,b)$ is $(1,4),$\nthen $a+b=$" :question/worked-solution "The given points are $\\underbrace{(-5,3)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ $\\underbrace{(a,b)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and $\\underbrace{(1,4)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (1,4) & =(\\frac{-5+a}{2},\\frac{3+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\frac{-5+a}{2} \\\\ 4 &=\\frac{3+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a &=7 \\\\ b &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(7,5)$ and $a+b=12.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0618c56b-92f8-5743-9e32-328933507a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}+{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514887} {:answer/id #uuid "42831ed2-f44c-506e-a16d-0e06ead786c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}-{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515014} {:answer/id #uuid "581a6ccb-d929-5a4c-abd5-314717c764d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}+t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515068} {:answer/id #uuid "b182ff13-9dbc-5c38-8d5b-05e109460201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515243} {:answer/id #uuid "b6bd0e4e-b413-5783-9897-cec63d3177bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515258}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515068} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb54adf-64fe-5825-9ef0-62336c5f858a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2becf284-e142-5ae3-80f6-d208696ea202" :question/math-academy-id "q-95068" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)={e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}+t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320777af-ae37-523c-bacf-e28e66480cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514983} {:answer/id #uuid "7852e317-6684-5e8f-b211-c3d2fd1b2920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515122} {:answer/id #uuid "9cdb3a18-70b7-5be8-85e6-7d754574d6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515204} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2ebd72-45b1-5c05-82b9-a266f54fe224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515274} {:answer/id #uuid "d551241c-84d6-5150-94ac-c0e6751dcdf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515322}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515274} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3819eb9-9cd0-5d3c-852e-6d38178d3c62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "57c74959-06e0-5008-b9fc-e618f65747e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-143584" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{23}{15},$ find $\\text{E}[3X-Y].$" :question/worked-solution "We're given $\\text{E}[Y].$ So, we first need to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nThe marginal density function for $X$ when $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{9}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{[{x}^{3}y+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}(2{x}^{3}+\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}({x}^{3}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot \\frac{4}{9}({x}^{3}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{[\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}(\\frac{1}{5}+1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[3X-Y] & =3\\,\\text{E}[X]+(-1)\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =3\\,\\left(\\frac{8}{15}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{23}{15}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047ee7a4-18a8-58d6-b450-3ca4a9d4d7e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514881} {:answer/id #uuid "10c4fde8-8a2c-5044-81db-2ef8e8bc14ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514909} {:answer/id #uuid "3a95cbe7-f451-5320-9372-bdc4d36f1770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515000} {:answer/id #uuid "bc2b2632-3760-5a09-ae52-b564a7addc6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515271} {:answer/id #uuid "c562738c-9074-56f9-991a-6abc27300f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515294}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514881} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee53ea70-06f7-5a83-81c3-442de950c369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6141f906-6242-52a2-92a1-6fa0f7b0342a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159839" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=3,\\,{x}_{3}=2,\\,{x}_{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =1!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 2!=1\\cdot 6\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=24, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1+3+2+2=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-4\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+8+24=28.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b2db6b-e4a2-59ce-aec1-ef7d698cc557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514912} {:answer/id #uuid "8592fd8a-a60b-5222-be37-08c4d12b699e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515149} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3abf38-e119-56f0-b466-68f90093ef89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515236} {:answer/id #uuid "e77a7ff1-b917-56d9-9450-beaa5bf0254b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515359} {:answer/id #uuid "fe364573-b5d5-5e45-b831-a1dc4fd75f72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515408}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515408} :answer-field/id #uuid "50b0880a-d63a-547c-ac9e-9646cff1bb56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "871ccc09-e09c-5033-9027-58208490bfda" :question/math-academy-id "q-159842" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=4,\\,{x}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =1!\\cdot 4!\\cdot 2!=1\\cdot 24\\cdot 2=48, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1+4+2=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-3\\theta +7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-3+7+48=52.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10528982-819d-558d-b7b9-c606e71872b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=3x+1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514906} {:answer/id #uuid "7d86ddc1-2c7d-548c-9b05-047b8457b098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=x-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515131} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e89105-6501-5255-9bbc-617aef43299e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=x-\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515228} {:answer/id #uuid "becf689a-2251-5072-95a8-008d1c2e4200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=2x+\\pi\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515277} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b5a29-3a47-5678-8b2a-9278853cd35b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=2x\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515373} :answer-field/id #uuid "37d670d1-3bff-5409-be4d-e79488473006" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ae3534f1-44d3-55be-bcdb-7634e16c97c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132594" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Given that the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2x{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi .$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & ={e}^{0}+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1/2,\\pi )}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\pi \\\\ z &=2x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b7b9791-af25-5e90-861e-446399a6a146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515128} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b2a52c3-ee42-535e-8b88-9e9ef0d1b7d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515446} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8c1496a-1064-5406-b667-1268a2be4ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515264} :answer-field/id #uuid "daca740b-bc95-5aa8-be58-811d21d0982f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c5f766d1-90d4-56be-969c-e0d6ece7eafd" :question/math-academy-id "q-216948" :question/problem "![](images/75/75ea79c4a66a187d5973375c40f49d373cee14f9d15848841616e93a420c6cbd.png)\n\nGiven that $(-3,3)$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ above, find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The given points are $\\underbrace{(a,b)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$, $\\underbrace{(0,1)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and $\\underbrace{(-3,3)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (-3,3) & =(\\frac{a+0}{2},\\frac{b+1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3 &=\\frac{a}{2} \\\\ 3 &=\\frac{b+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a &=-6 \\\\ b &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $a=-6$ and $b=5.$"} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0216de2e-4a0f-57e3-9b57-0ff0f473bd35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515915} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9acb2e-307a-5bff-84df-1d99817452ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515986}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515986} :answer-field/id #uuid "11def7b4-86ae-5083-b3a6-95ac3f063630" :answer-field/key "The range of the function is $(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75a178e2-b8a0-5e23-95d8-57e0b22e66e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515948} {:answer/id #uuid "cea54101-9dc1-513c-8325-1c76ddfeb073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515989}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515989} :answer-field/id #uuid "131a63aa-afd8-5dd6-98ab-74699ace8718" :answer-field/key "The domain is all real numbers." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515920} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f0a2436-d2b8-5ada-bc74-03b482439509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515917} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3d707f-8861-51cf-bd71-44eb3c3678ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515924}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515924} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b59ef31-8c85-53fa-af26-f5a8104dc0e2" :answer-field/key "The least positive period is $\\pi.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515960} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37f2a0ca-c13a-53b6-8fde-b6f03e923b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515927} {:answer/id #uuid "93abb7ed-08f3-5fe3-b5e8-61618de9def4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515961} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c77fec-21b9-5241-91cb-3a7932d621ee" :answer-field/key "The function is negative throughout $(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi,n\\pi).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "34e68721-06df-5e4c-ae2f-a9024e61fe8c" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\tan x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\tan x.$\n\n![](images/e6/e65a2728d8bdad40af736ced1ec8303bf10fb4d11fc66b33784663512093a26b.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The domain is all real numbers” is false. The function is defined everywhere except where $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi.$\n\n- Statement “The function is negative throughout $(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi,n\\pi).$” is true. The function is positive on $(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2})$ and negative on $(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},0).$ The period is $\\pi,$ so these signs repeat after each integer multiple of $\\pi.$\n\n- Statement “The least positive period is $\\pi.$” is true. The graph repeats after a horizontal shift of $\\pi,$ and no smaller positive shift repeats the whole graph. Therefore, the least positive period is $\\pi.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The function is negative throughout $(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi,n\\pi).$”; “The least positive period is $\\pi.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0216de2e-4a0f-57e3-9b57-0ff0f473bd35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515915} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9acb2e-307a-5bff-84df-1d99817452ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515986}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515986} :answer-field/id #uuid "11def7b4-86ae-5083-b3a6-95ac3f063630" :answer-field/key "The range of the function is $(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515919} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "579b7bf4-e674-5a6e-b50f-733059831e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515936} {:answer/id #uuid "f1aa9208-25fb-5388-854e-ae4d8c510856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516004} :answer-field/id #uuid "8931d3a2-66cf-56b8-bac8-575ecfa645ca" :answer-field/key "The vertical asymptotes are exactly $x=2n\\pi.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515958} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a9e56e4-bf34-5382-b3b1-43439c58246b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515966} {:answer/id #uuid "be607b27-6eca-5b4a-9d6d-c11c7c093414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515966} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5857270-a22c-56e6-97bf-70aed0be91ec" :answer-field/key "The zeros of the function are exactly $x=n\\pi.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "599ca534-9c31-5bf4-83d8-146c8a000e49" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\tan x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\tan x.$\n\n![](images/e6/e65a2728d8bdad40af736ced1ec8303bf10fb4d11fc66b33784663512093a26b.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The zeros of the function are exactly $x=n\\pi.$” is true. The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm\\pi,\\pm2\\pi,\\ldots,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi.$\n\n- Statement “The vertical asymptotes are exactly $x=2n\\pi.$” is false. The function has an asymptote at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and the period is $\\pi.$ Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi.$\n\n- Statement “The range of the function is $(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}).$” is false. The function takes values on all the real numbers, hence the range is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The zeros of the function are exactly $x=n\\pi.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9606e487-0713-51cd-8d7b-86344299b977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515964} {:answer/id #uuid "bd35cfd0-28c3-566f-b8e3-4d292de9e5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515979}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515979} :answer-field/id #uuid "442f3a19-905b-523f-b1f9-f30240a29310" :answer-field/key "The domain is all real numbers except $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515930} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c26246b2-ee13-51b7-9e93-524ec615af29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515982} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f03165-7735-5013-924c-72f36254f7c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516003} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a03890b-f8e9-5a96-8bd7-d6ef8a3efdee" :answer-field/key "The function has no positive period." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515952} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76f3739f-3d95-5f10-9e90-1ba5246e8957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515949} {:answer/id #uuid "a792c3b8-b6dd-5cfb-9674-2e56d346de23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515971} :answer-field/id #uuid "b695a7a6-9766-58f8-a0a0-616c0947a9ef" :answer-field/key "The function is negative throughout $(n\\pi,\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9792d956-ea77-5200-b68a-06624704ecdd" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\tan x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\tan x.$\n\n![](images/e6/e65a2728d8bdad40af736ced1ec8303bf10fb4d11fc66b33784663512093a26b.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The domain is all real numbers except $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi.$” is true. The function is defined everywhere except where $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi.$\n\n- Statement “The function is negative throughout $(n\\pi,\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi).$” is false. The function is positive on $(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2})$ and negative on $(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},0).$ The period is $\\pi,$ so these signs repeat after each integer multiple of $\\pi.$\n\n- Statement “The function has no positive period” is false. The graph repeats after a horizontal shift of $\\pi,$ and no smaller positive shift repeats the whole graph. Therefore, the least positive period is $\\pi.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The domain is all real numbers except $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi.$” only."} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5544273f-0dc3-52d8-9606-4ee35a47e671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 175921