ment “The least positive period is $2\\pi.$” is false. The graph repeats after a horizontal shift of $\\pi,$ and no smaller positive shift repeats the whole graph. Therefore, the least positive period is $\\pi.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The domain is all real numbers except $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi.$”; “The function is positive throughout $(n\\pi,\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi).$” only."} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9a685b-e7bf-53bd-99ed-2a928b6f1ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516142} {:answer/id #uuid "532771d5-519d-50af-94e8-f4b6987d74f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516288} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6ebad4-4e5c-5893-89b1-194838c5792a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69b3c7ed-0ad2-5549-acf8-acdbd9e0e4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3e^{-4(x-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516341} :answer-field/id #uuid "600c0667-9438-5a96-b5a3-42ddde81d0e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "07505f7b-3ca5-545d-9207-d1e1cfa4ebbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-343946" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=f(x),\\,y(1)=-3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $3$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+4y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+4y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=-3.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =A{e}^{-4(1)}+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ -3 & =A{e}^{-4}+0 \\\\ A & =-3{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=(-3{e}^{4}){e}^{-4x}=-3{e}^{4-4x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-3{e}^{4-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fce1eb7-6882-55e9-9a78-19b8b9ec12de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0565" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516439} {:answer/id #uuid "a11c1dd4-4d83-5f50-ab22-450ede146cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0428" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516443} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ee7691-eaaa-5a44-8e4b-9c9a62cb1212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0615" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516455} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf18dde-67fe-5d37-ba3e-bc04e8232dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0784" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516579} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a58d8e-66ae-563e-a563-1b7fc88e1a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0516" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516615} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4643a15-31bb-5228-8c72-0d5dcdaebd79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "08394bc0-c2d2-5c3c-828d-b5a2969449ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-147070" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=96$ from a population where $36\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $28\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0537\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ | $\\,0.0516\\,$ | $\\,0.0505\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0655\\,$ | $\\,0.0643\\,$ | $\\,0.0630\\,$ | $\\,0.0618\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0793\\,$ | $\\,0.0778\\,$ | $\\,0.0764\\,$ | $\\,0.0749\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=96,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.36.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=96(0.36)=34.56>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=96(1-0.36)=61.44>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=96$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.36,\\frac{0.36(1-0.36)}{96}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.36,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.28).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.28) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.28-0.36}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.63) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.63).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.63) & =0.0516.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\widehat{\\,p}<0.28) & =Φ(-1.63) \\\\  & =0.0516.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "917086dd-8ad2-56fb-8eb0-55b0aeef4f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "97\\,200\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516417} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff5a488-9441-5023-926c-ce3a0b335fed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "97.2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516441} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f79363-79cd-5503-9bee-b923c291154b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.972\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516485} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bd8d2d-544f-5f51-b969-f8bb0251dbd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,720\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516616} {:answer/id #uuid "f638ad08-eeb3-554a-98df-a3d74479967d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9.72\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516626} :answer-field/id #uuid "4da99ea6-5345-5314-8a2c-a1800552e160" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0d475207-225c-5dcb-85b4-165f52b26314" :question/math-academy-id "q-105551" :question/problem "A cruise ship on the high seas travels at a speed of $972$ centimeters per second. What is the speed of the ship in meters per second?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{cm}$ with $\\text{m}.$ To get rid of $\\text{cm},$ we can put $\\text{cm}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{m},$ we can put $\\text{m}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The speed given in the problem statement is $972\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}.$\n\n- There are $100$ centimeters per meter. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\frac{1}{100}$ meters per centimeter.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}972\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}} & =972\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}} \\\\  & =972\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}) \\\\  & =9.72\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the speed of the cruise is $9.72\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a8235a-b96e-5fa1-8108-e0e6dd4ab2d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516146} {:answer/id #uuid "4dccfb7c-69d9-5e39-bdc6-b9c1f9d8666a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516272} {:answer/id #uuid "4e878814-1cec-558c-9ad5-c9d1d1636938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516273} {:answer/id #uuid "5923c2ed-ea1c-5b9b-918c-6ab0788b0527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516302} {:answer/id #uuid "70ba8ee7-e027-558d-ba3b-6caf25bcdd99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516366}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516273} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a80460b-81a6-5762-8a66-e56c03dc6782" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1a1059d5-573e-59f8-8d92-01104e325d82" :question/math-academy-id "q-132444" :question/problem "A player's height in a particular professional basketball league is normally distributed with a mean of $77$ inches and a standard deviation of $3.57$ inches.\nWhat is the probability that the height of a randomly selected player is more than $76$ inches but less than $78$ inches?\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.1\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5636\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5675\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5714\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6026\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6064\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6103\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.3\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6406\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6443\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6480\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3594\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3557\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3520\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3974\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3936\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3897\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4364\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4325\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4286\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the height (in inches) of a randomly selected player. Then we have $X∼N(77,{3.57}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(76<X<78).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(76<X<78) & =P(\\frac{76-77}{3.57}<Z<\\frac{78-77}{3.57}) \\\\  & =P(-0.28<Z<0.28)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.28<Z<0.28) & =P(Z<0.28)-P(Z\\le -0.28) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.28)-P(Z\\le -0.28) \\\\  & =Φ(0.28)-Φ(-0.28)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.28) & =0.6103 \\\\ Φ(-0.28) & =0.3897.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.28<Z<0.28) & =Φ(0.28)-Φ(-0.28) \\\\  & =0.6103-0.3897 \\\\  & =0.2206 \\\\  & ≈0.22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the height of a randomly selected player is more than $76$ inches but less than $78$ inches is approximately $0.22.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "292e7bbd-999c-520e-bb6b-1e42da3fa632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{4}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516197} {:answer/id #uuid "795d6440-8fe5-57dc-ae9c-d5da35732f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516375} {:answer/id #uuid "a924941b-e8c9-5ed5-80b8-ad7a06f41304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{4}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516465} {:answer/id #uuid "d46dfd71-e586-55eb-8c97-07a1e7452b66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{4}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516560} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1af559-472b-5b48-b982-a14b98c054ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516635}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516465} :answer-field/id #uuid "de980059-9619-56fd-bc0c-0fa09f73dc66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2c958fbe-a6a3-5e35-b0a4-3549e7aa33c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-95419" :question/problem "If $y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2{x}^{2},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(2{x}^{2})}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {(2{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{4}+1}}\\cdot 4x \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{4}+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a99c937-0618-51b0-9ee8-a05c39b743bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(3-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{b}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516132} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9a685b-e7bf-53bd-99ed-2a928b6f1ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516142} {:answer/id #uuid "532771d5-519d-50af-94e8-f4b6987d74f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516288} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6ebad4-4e5c-5893-89b1-194838c5792a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516376}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516142} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b3e1afe-7ade-5f8c-9104-fa8e0533e940" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "465baa29-a84f-575d-bfbc-3ca094c0808a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343950" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $2\\pi $-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+2=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{b}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=3$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =B\\sqrt{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{b}_{n} \\\\ B\\sqrt{2} & =3-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{b}_{n} \\\\ B & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n{b}_{n}}{\\sqrt{2}})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2526bd24-622c-5111-ad94-fbb56ead3d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516183} {:answer/id #uuid "49972733-8d9d-5321-b836-cf1a178c9f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516261} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec01a81-fb61-582d-ae8d-2125920edf15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516316} {:answer/id #uuid "b523d36c-1bf0-53cc-978b-b71319d7a12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516489} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e8dd65-936c-5ce5-b9fd-88eb040a28a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516527}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516489} :answer-field/id #uuid "45a8bede-a863-52ca-9cb6-ae8ed026f9dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5082eabf-27a1-5373-b336-872133f4c0e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-105556" :question/problem "A racecar can reach a speed of $360$ kilometers per hour. What is this speed in kilometers per second?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{h}$ with $\\text{s}.$ To get rid of $\\text{h},$ we can put $\\text{h}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{s},$ we can put $\\text{s}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The speed given in the problem statement is $360\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}.$\n\n- There are $3\\,600$ seconds per hour. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\frac{1}{3\\,600}$ hours per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}360\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}} & =360\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\,600}\\,\\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =(360\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\,600})\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.1\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the race car reach a speed  of $0.1\\,\\text{km}/\\text{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311a3504-e36b-5f10-892e-bdeb105186c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516216} {:answer/id #uuid "462d5be7-6aa9-513b-9a51-6ade4b44dd82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516255} {:answer/id #uuid "7f967064-3dce-5f42-b291-550af58a18c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516382} {:answer/id #uuid "ae284407-e445-5a54-b789-f40aae94b7e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516474} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e752a9-62d5-5350-8ec9-a86a3355e5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516610}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516382} :answer-field/id #uuid "16da39d2-fdd0-5aa5-84cd-d52ae5dab2c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "642f1676-b974-5c20-b33e-f45029a01f5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-46697" :question/problem "A car travels at a speed of $2.5$ kilometers per minute. What is this in kilometers per hour?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{min}$ with $\\text{hour}.$ To get rid of $\\text{min},$ we can put $\\text{min}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{hour},$ we can put $\\text{hour}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{hour}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{hour}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $2.5\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ minutes in one hour.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.5\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}} & =2.5\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot 60\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{hour}} \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 60(\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{hour}}) \\\\  & =150\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d8cfe9-4b93-5f75-80a5-535b163dfe29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "85.3\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516214} {:answer/id #uuid "34d6d3c7-1689-5248-895a-b81d7cf970d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "87.5\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516223} {:answer/id #uuid "6466cbf3-e0e7-5e28-a7cc-3d9c12cae870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "89.8\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516331} {:answer/id #uuid "66956326-a3a4-5914-80b0-99ab80e8cc5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "87.9\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516334} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cebdaf-ad45-5124-8538-17b343686fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "89.4\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516484}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516484} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7a92c6e-2892-5988-ae79-425c23feccca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7012a871-ceb2-5bf5-9d51-cddf37c78344" :question/math-academy-id "q-133054" :question/problem "The average volume of soda in cans is $12$ ounces. Additionally, in $3\\%$ of cans, the soda volume is less than $11.7$ ounces. Assuming the volume of soda is normally distributed, find the percentage of cans containing less than $12.2$ ounces of soda, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the volume of soda in a randomly selected can. Then we have $X∼N(12,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<12.2).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nWe know that $P(X<11.7)=0.03.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<11.7) & =0.03 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{11.7-12}{\\sigma }) & =0.03 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{-0.3}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.03 \\\\ \\frac{-0.3}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.03)≈-1.8808.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-0.3}{\\sigma } & =-1.8808 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =6.26933 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{6.26933} \\\\  & ≈0.1595.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<12.2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<12.2) & =P(X\\le 12.2) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{12.2-12}{0.1595}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le 1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(1.25) \\\\  & ≈89.4\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $89.4\\%$  of soda cans contain less than $12.2$ ounces of soda."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06171ae9-aab5-5555-8742-22098a666ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.19)-Φ(-1.19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516124} {:answer/id #uuid "076508d1-5160-580c-894f-c6ee9485bf2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(0.69)-Φ(-0.69)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516127} {:answer/id #uuid "54ade330-5c85-51a0-9b3d-21d097f4a0f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(2.26)-Φ(-2.26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516290} {:answer/id #uuid "551a8f15-eac2-577e-8054-fb57a38b76a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.36)-Φ(-1.36)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516292} {:answer/id #uuid "f918d5a6-7a95-5bf9-9d8e-e78ed7535f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.95)-Φ(-1.95)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516631} :answer-field/id #uuid "e66048ec-88b7-5ae4-8b5d-5521b6f747e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9faef3f5-2412-5ae3-81dd-9f520c533b8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-146937" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population where $70\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $60\\%$ and $80\\%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=80,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.7.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=80(0.7)=56>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=80(1-0.7)=24>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=80$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{80}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,0.002\\,625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.6\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.8).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.6\\le \\widehat{\\,p}\\le 0.8) & =P(\\frac{0.6-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.002\\,625}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.8-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.002\\,625}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.95\\le Z\\le 1.95) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.95)-P(Z\\le -1.95) \\\\  & =Φ(1.95)-Φ(-1.95)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516829 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ccc9c21-c3ce-5f4a-ba59-583860541dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516269} :answer-field/id #uuid "9688ef1e-aa0f-557f-92e7-8f181132182a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a160ea33-0abc-551b-81fb-aa9297d75028" :question/math-academy-id "q-343758" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}-5y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}-5y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-2.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{5(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ -2 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=-2{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-2{e}^{5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f65945-b500-528c-b740-17a557b9ff0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516155} {:answer/id #uuid "492377d5-2038-59ab-be78-068c1d4f8b93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "150\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516260} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9a5554-46d2-5bd3-9bac-5ac0beea217a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.015\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516403} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a5523c-6f55-59d2-b7c2-6c598b3d19c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.001\\,5\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516451} {:answer/id #uuid "aab27a45-ad45-576b-b764-0f80ee6bf45e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.15\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516451} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecc56b39-eec2-58a2-8d1d-910e05bec8f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a4020071-b095-5022-af4b-4598557e943b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105547" :question/problem "The acceleration of a cyclist is $1.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ What is the cyclist's acceleration in ${\\text{km/s}}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{m}$ with $\\text{km}.$ To get rid of $\\text{m},$ we can put $\\text{m}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{km},$ we can put $\\text{km}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{m}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The acceleration given in the problem statement is $1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ meters per kilometer. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\frac{1}{1\\,000}$ kilometers per meter.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}} & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{m}} \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{1\\,000}(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}) \\\\  & =0.001\\,5\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the acceleration of the cyclist is $0.001\\,5\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce1e87fa-a67e-5e2b-a4d6-91106614efde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x+18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516548}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516548} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a6982d7-16d9-5cb0-8eec-9bbb54099cee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a4eeca3f-6b34-52eb-84b0-9a3e73ae1349" :question/math-academy-id "q-341812" :question/problem "If $y=\\operatorname{arsinh} \\,(\\frac{x-3}{3}),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(\\operatorname{arsinh} \\,(\\frac{x-3}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{x-3}{3})}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {(\\frac{x-3}{3})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}+1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{\\frac{(x-3)^{2}+9}{9}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(x-3)^{2}+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-6x+9)+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x+18}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a559b83c-53ab-5a83-9edd-ae76985624bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{4x^8+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516457}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516457} :answer-field/id #uuid "6349b651-0355-5796-8a79-80d15ad9f15e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa4e4da2-fcbb-5aa7-a6f1-72dee8a3b1b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-341820" :question/problem "If $y=3\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{4}),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $24{x}^{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{4})) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{4})) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{4})}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{4})}^{′} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}{x}^{8}+1}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{8}+9}{9}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{8}+9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e35f18e-1bf6-5d1a-8e59-13ab06067872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516355} {:answer/id #uuid "9f019a42-6b8b-554e-91e3-893c44124a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516437} {:answer/id #uuid "cccb4d2d-b8aa-572e-aae2-f7163ea703a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516544} {:answer/id #uuid "e80d0d28-5add-54e4-97f4-c1b73ae40982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516593} {:answer/id #uuid "fb074b86-3190-5f6d-8a8e-9fbdb9461d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516636}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516437} :answer-field/id #uuid "606b941c-af91-539a-a82d-135b0666034b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bb189cdf-2b97-5db2-ae7a-d4991a5c036b" :question/math-academy-id "q-132711" :question/problem "The annual income of an employee in a large company is normally distributed with a mean of $\\$50\\,000$ and a standard deviation of $\\$15\\,000.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected employee's income is more than $\\$46\\,700$ but less than $\\$56\\,150?$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4207\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4168\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4129\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4602\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4562\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4522\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5000\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4960\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4920\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.2\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5793\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5832\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5871\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.3\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6179\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6217\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6255\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.4\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6554\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6591\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6628\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the annual income (in dollars) of a randomly selected employee. Then we have $X∼N(50\\,000,15\\,{000}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(46\\,700<X<56\\,150).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(46\\,700<X<56\\,150) & =P(\\frac{46\\,700-50\\,000}{15\\,000}<Z<\\frac{56\\,150-50\\,000}{15\\,000}) \\\\  & =P(-0.22<Z<0.41)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.22<Z<0.41) & =P(Z<0.41)-P(Z\\le -0.22) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.41)-P(Z\\le -0.22) \\\\  & =Φ(0.41)-Φ(-0.22)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.41) & =0.6591 \\\\ Φ(-0.22) & =0.4129.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.22<Z<0.41) & =Φ(0.41)-Φ(-0.22) \\\\  & =0.6591-0.4129 \\\\  & =0.2462 \\\\  & ≈0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected employee's income is more than $\\$46\\,700$ but less than $\\$56\\,150$ is approximately $0.25.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dac4121-cbe6-553e-bd16-6fab29fec507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9{x}^{5}}{9-{x}^{12}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516143} {:answer/id #uuid "380efcee-b3e5-51ba-b34a-0999cc8d3601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{3-{x}^{12}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516228} {:answer/id #uuid "4f793398-fc2c-5c69-a5eb-7e3e8a2143ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18{x}^{5}}{9-{x}^{12}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516275} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e05fce-5995-5a9b-8200-cbf720d49a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{5}}{9-{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516554} {:answer/id #uuid "ffbcdb37-7a76-5a7a-b5f5-5c2b7570f9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{5}}{3-{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516648}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516143} :answer-field/id #uuid "6744b580-35a3-5368-ab4c-1be0682aa649" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bc18352d-fce3-5063-b58a-47ac19e5cf8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95512" :question/problem "If $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{3}),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{3}\\right)}^{2}}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{3}\\right)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{{x}^{12}}{9})}\\cdot 2{x}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{9{x}^{5}}{9-{x}^{12}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b595a3e-31c3-551c-aa15-2adbb1a368be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516133} {:answer/id #uuid "41921c02-93c9-559a-a5a1-ba1cc7f58824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516241} {:answer/id #uuid "88b0143e-7552-5d17-a22a-24748c27d899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516398} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b23766-cc77-57ee-8748-4e55410f3e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516459} {:answer/id #uuid "d99a11bb-26fc-599e-b35a-158a8e9b882d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516570} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d787048-8743-53b0-8f95-62517b984fc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bee8fa81-1bb7-5df3-bc77-fd80b7f5193e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162568" :question/problem "![](images/40/40e6a88e309f6ce5fece628a6d9bd66ffd00105edd3816fac04ae534ef7558b5.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(2,1)$ and $B(-1,-1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(4x+2y)\\,i-(8x+4y)\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ can be parametrized as*\n\n$$\nr=(2-3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-8x-4y))=4-4=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/36/363ebd04d2629ed2b1b29ccab326e22737cf42e4c026d62910a50a1dd88e80f2.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(2-3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4x+2y=4(2-3t)+2(1-2t)=-16t+10, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-8x-4y=-8(2-3t)-4(1-2t)=32t-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)=-3, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-16t+10)\\cdot (-2)-(32t-20)\\cdot (-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(32t-20)+(96t-60)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}128t-80\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[64{t}^{2}-80t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =64-80 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-16.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96e14832-25ec-5cc2-9b80-eef2d0a5f2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516425} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8746fb-2ea4-58ca-a484-de8db975ddb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516429} {:answer/id #uuid "be7962f1-2c0b-553d-affa-31d3acba17bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516514} {:answer/id #uuid "f9874a00-5d86-506a-b77b-4b0ce2fc6efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516425} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e8e4fce-3b23-5eb0-9715-6b625c2b4dc5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1ec13b9-1623-5452-ac2a-c54844694d83" :question/math-academy-id "q-344034" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an even, $3$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an even function and the differential operator contains no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits the symmetry and is also an even function consisting only of cosine terms. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+5=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Differentiating the total solution gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}{a}_{n}\\sin \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =-A\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ 0 & =0+B\\sqrt{5}-0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because the total solution is an even function, its derivative is an odd function, which must vanish at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we use the condition $y(0)=3.$ With $B=0,$ the total solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x).\n$$\n\nApplying $y(0)=3$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =A+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\ A & =3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A$ back into ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)(3-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cos \\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d564762-e4a4-5bb0-afe8-b8b8f6fac3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516141} {:answer/id #uuid "b123ec58-423f-51d9-9afa-58d0e3e2907d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516480} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5bb2aa-9b98-5e14-a1e1-eb9f5bf08258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516506} {:answer/id #uuid "bc23addf-a23c-586d-8c9e-b7440595c852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516507} {:answer/id #uuid "be5c6d3f-c2f5-5f4b-a5b6-a35aad7bbaac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516506} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2781212-fedc-5b0a-8c49-342c3e418b01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c428bbdb-3474-5c6d-a12b-bbe7007eab5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162566" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f84859100733c56d6381f6ae5c3f5fa55c693621190b3f921e3e1e8f850a4f9.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(1,1)$ and $B(-1,-1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\,i+(3-2y)\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ can be parametrized as*\n\n$$\nr=(1-2t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3-2y)=2-2=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5186fb872f03e73124d04c2e9ff07e77fb336bef121484a9ff1b697ea0cf8bcd.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(1-2t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =2x=2(1-2t)=2-4t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =3-2y=3-2(1-2t)=1+4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-4t)\\cdot (-2)-(1+4t)\\cdot (-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-4t)-(1+4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}1-8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot {[t-4{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (1-4) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d48108-d2d6-587b-bfe3-2ffd1073736d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516156} {:answer/id #uuid "2606f266-1daa-5d7d-8ca5-771dec8b5b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516185} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf03bd5-254e-5534-bc31-f8df36341221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516311} {:answer/id #uuid "a54621c5-4d43-5cff-83c2-5c5c66d64ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516456} {:answer/id #uuid "f6449119-7803-5c83-b8f8-f21cb84631f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516627}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516156} :answer-field/id #uuid "71b3a12d-2d4a-5204-9a4e-bc2880e6dedc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca24054b-57db-5111-abb2-9a1c0e62e7da" :question/math-academy-id "q-95428" :question/problem "If $y=6\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{′} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179a74a5-b3d8-5e7a-9c3e-5819837efef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{4}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516160} {:answer/id #uuid "31172cf1-3a3e-5cb6-afa8-4b91537a5882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{4}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516215} {:answer/id #uuid "43ac0f87-1caa-5c8f-989a-102c5cc7de2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{4}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516249} {:answer/id #uuid "a35fde23-056b-5940-8d9a-cc873ac086d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516450} {:answer/id #uuid "c55de174-20a2-5857-a097-babc7925bbfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{4}-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516531} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae6ae2c8-5130-53cc-bb64-372c2f7de751" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4997d00-7652-5cc7-a200-60025211209a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95504" :question/problem "If $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡2{x}^{2},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(2{x}^{2})}^{2}-1}}\\cdot {(2{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{4}-1}}\\cdot 4x \\\\  & =\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{4}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086d4688-33de-5a64-866f-902df4384ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6.9$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516128} {:answer/id #uuid "4d872133-f4d9-5e17-b850-ddc1193b671a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8.1$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516270} {:answer/id #uuid "6f915334-6b87-5751-ad38-2d634085831f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7.8$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516359} {:answer/id #uuid "cd64ecf1-525c-5508-8649-19fa1da0870a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6.5$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516545} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3dad55-53b9-5149-9af5-5e3b2c18ac2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7.3$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516644}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516644} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a0b5d40-1768-5377-8893-9229f2f0b027" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e856700f-73de-59c0-8d3a-1f40df24d089" :question/math-academy-id "q-52044" :question/problem "A tree sapling grows by $2$ centimeters per day. Assuming that one year has $365$ days, how many meters does the tree grow in one year?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}.$ We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\text{cm}$ to $\\text{m}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\text{day}$ into $\\text{year}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\cancel{\\text{day}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{days}}}{\\text{year}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{year}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $2\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}}.$\n\n- There are $100$ centimeters in one meter, therefore, there are $\\frac{1}{100}$ meters in one centimeter.\n\n- There are $365$ days in one year.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}} & =2\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}}\\cdot 365\\,\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{year}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\cdot 365(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\cancel{\\text{day}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{days}}}{\\text{year}}) \\\\  & =7.3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $2$ centimeters per day is equivalent to $7.3$ meters per year. In one year, the tree grows by $7.3$ meters."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02150670-428b-56e2-9985-6194ff4b8ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516116} {:answer/id #uuid "0c56636a-f906-5735-8c37-7b2138e8f77d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516136} {:answer/id #uuid "4562e56f-79a7-5953-82dc-cdefccb01ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516253} {:answer/id #uuid "bf315d70-83f5-5ea5-abd9-3ae3cfc47fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516517} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d2bf34-d828-5bcd-970b-c54ad54643d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "860" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516253} :answer-field/id #uuid "701ad097-1360-59f4-8891-d19c7b1ca762" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f4a129bc-20d0-5e0c-8e4d-26304c226b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-118558" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of beavers in a river follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.2y(8-\\frac{y}{100}),\\,y(0)=200,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible beaver population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.2y(8-\\frac{y}{100}) \\\\  & =0.2y\\left(\\frac{800-y}{100}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0.2y}{100}(800-y) \\\\  & =0.002y(800-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=800.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $800,$ and we conclude that the number of beavers cannot exceed $800.$"} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e08accb-7eda-5b3f-8f7f-cf6049c19834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517023} {:answer/id #uuid "53ceef81-cbcf-5469-a654-9c8dc64c15b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517029}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517023} :answer-field/id #uuid "01a3e318-485f-572e-9d51-28ff9e7bc115" :answer-field/key "The point $(\\frac{9\\pi }{2},-1)$ is on the graph." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77393571-ce31-5f63-beaf-003ff290501d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517043} {:answer/id #uuid "7822cda3-c180-54b1-ae7d-4b6df399d2db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517044}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517043} :answer-field/id #uuid "278a75f0-f4d2-535c-b4fe-bdfe3ca2fc93" :answer-field/key "$x=-8\\pi $ is a vertical asymptote." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517001} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a060bac0-d972-5929-954d-efb7c692c018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517058} {:answer/id #uuid "be313153-7819-5ff9-bbc0-b12b82ba8e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517070}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517070} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d06a9c3-5924-51ae-a2fa-9e0de39ccee5" :answer-field/key "$x=\\frac{11\\pi }{2}$ is in the domain." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517005} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f0264c2-6175-53c9-a115-e6ef053e0035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517014} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c3d0a3-03b6-5bbf-80e4-439eb7c076a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517084}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517014} :answer-field/id #uuid "70c498a9-ee8e-5f91-b9c6-c1194e84a9eb" :answer-field/key "The point $(\\frac{11\\pi }{2},1)$ is on the graph." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "58f443a0-5e86-50b9-8fb5-cff53ee8ef40" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\csc x$?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\csc x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The cosecant takes the value $-1$ at $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{2}$. Hence, statement “The point $(\\frac{11\\pi }{2},1)$ is on the graph” is false.\n\n- The lines $x=n\\pi$ for any integer $n$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. Hence, statement “$x=-8\\pi $ is a vertical asymptote” is true.\n\n- The domain excludes exactly the integer multiples of $\\pi$. The number $\\frac{11\\pi }{2}$ is not an integer multiple of $\\pi$. Hence, statement “$x=\\frac{11\\pi }{2}$ is in the domain” is true."} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132c303a-8995-5199-8b2c-686733100f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516994} {:answer/id #uuid "191950ed-caf3-5570-a252-a222618d2c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516996}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516994} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b6fd2d4-bb06-531f-b02d-ef7412c694de" :answer-field/key "The point $(\\frac{9\\pi }{2},-1)$ is on the graph." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517037} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f0264c2-6175-53c9-a115-e6ef053e0035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517014} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c3d0a3-03b6-5bbf-80e4-439eb7c076a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517084}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517014} :answer-field/id #uuid "70c498a9-ee8e-5f91-b9c6-c1194e84a9eb" :answer-field/key "The point $(\\frac{11\\pi }{2},1)$ is on the graph." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517040} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454820d6-