aph” is false.\n\n- The lines $x=n\\pi$ for any integer $n$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. Hence, statement “$x=-3\\pi $ is a vertical asymptote” is true.\n\n- The domain excludes exactly the integer multiples of $\\pi$. The number $\\frac{-4\\pi }{2}$ is an integer multiple of $\\pi$. Hence, statement “$x=\\frac{-4\\pi }{2}$ is in the domain” is false."} {:answer/id #uuid "44daafcd-40e6-532a-89aa-ac6f698763a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517105} {:answer/id #uuid "50040d57-2b2c-562b-9b76-6080dd9806ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(7,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517106} {:answer/id #uuid "11b3afc9-f9cc-5c8a-b6ab-4390c55f86f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(8,- \\frac{27}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517120} {:answer/id #uuid "97d37dda-73f6-5def-b61e-7b30f2c18eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-8,- \\frac{27}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517129} {:answer/id #uuid "98fc6441-004a-50b8-be13-2c8d2fa211bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-8,- \\frac{27}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517130} {:answer/id #uuid "b05f20f9-ffc8-5085-b0e1-8fa22e17eee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(8,- \\frac{27}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d286aa7-b2d6-58d2-bf29-61fb1720d50e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-7,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517121} {:answer/id #uuid "48f83f88-0613-5776-915c-a5994a6fc5f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(7,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517122} {:answer/id #uuid "61e8efb1-edb5-5857-a2e3-2d735f94c05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(7,-16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517124} {:answer/id #uuid "65e4c577-9efa-5639-9d6b-751511f27cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-7,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517126} {:answer/id #uuid "d79a5116-a0ab-50fa-a0de-8d76eaba32ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-7,16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517126} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2bc82bb-e2ed-5d33-b951-229f9e8624e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b988807a-34eb-52af-995a-9e3c8a1f0e2f" :question/problem "The point $(-7,8)$ lies on the curve $y=2f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-4?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-7,8)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)-4:$\n\n- First, take the graph $y=2f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (8))=(-7,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,-(4))=(-7,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $4$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,-4-4)=(-7,-8).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-7,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=2f(x),$ we have that\n\n$$\n2f(-7)=8\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(-7)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ Substituting $x=-7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=-f(-7)-4\\\\&=-(4)-4\\\\&=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-7,-8)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-4.$"} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f14205-061b-58e6-8ec2-15a66df7a546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-8,\\frac{27}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517138} {:answer/id #uuid "f7dbd166-b5b2-5ec2-a66a-c579223ce6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11b3afc9-f9cc-5c8a-b6ab-4390c55f86f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(8,- \\frac{27}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517120} {:answer/id #uuid "97d37dda-73f6-5def-b61e-7b30f2c18eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-8,- \\frac{27}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517129} {:answer/id #uuid "98fc6441-004a-50b8-be13-2c8d2fa211bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-8,- \\frac{27}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517130} {:answer/id #uuid "b05f20f9-ffc8-5085-b0e1-8fa22e17eee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(8,- \\frac{27}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517131} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f14205-061b-58e6-8ec2-15a66df7a546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-8,\\frac{27}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517129} :answer-field/id #uuid "95633b9d-4569-53c0-963e-77e08465b5f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f8cb0f82-4ced-5ad3-8499-c17a38448a7a" :question/problem "The point $(-8,7)$ lies on the curve $y=4f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-5?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-8,7)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)-5:$\n\n- First, take the graph $y=4f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,7)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (7))=(-8,\\frac{7}{4}).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,\\frac{7}{4})$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,-\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right))=(-8,- \\frac{7}{4}).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-5.$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,- \\frac{7}{4})$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,- \\frac{7}{4}-5)=(-8,- \\frac{27}{4}).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-8,7)$ lies on the graph of $y=4f(x),$ we have that\n\n$$\n4f(-8)=7\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(-8)=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-5.$ Substituting $x=-8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=-f(-8)-5\\\\&=-\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right)-5\\\\&=- \\frac{27}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-8,- \\frac{27}{4})$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-5.$"} {:answer/id #uuid "3d506d7f-07cd-59ac-a403-64c70b810b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{a}{2},b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517146} {:answer/id #uuid "6f955108-7f48-51fc-aaaa-ed9327143578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{a}{2},b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26ada36c-1091-5f42-8c63-eccc8b641f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$255$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517327} {:answer/id #uuid "b937214f-6d30-5777-98d4-63d7c6dc1a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-105$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517358} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2e00ae-4e6a-5d71-aef7-bdb151691a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-75$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517359} {:answer/id #uuid "cc796484-ad70-51d7-bb01-5b8342bff754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$180$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517363} {:answer/id #uuid "d16bf7d6-ca47-5047-90d1-e323cc45a658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$105$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517367} :answer-field/id #uuid "60454d3c-fec5-59e1-9526-d25e77d4da41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7f4724c3-2921-55f4-a281-340d20927d04" :question/problem "A factory begins work at $8\\text{:}00$ am. Its order rate is $O(t)=140t e^{-0.2t}$ cars per hour and its completion rate is $C(t)=90+15\\sin(2\\pi t/14)$ cars per hour, where $t$ is hours after work begins. What is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction at the 24-hour time $18\\text{:}30$? Orders enter construction immediately. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "The rate of change is the order rate minus the completion rate. The elapsed time is $t=(1110-480)/60=10.5$ hours.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(10.5)-C(10.5) & =140(10.5){e}^{-0.2(10.5)}-(90+15\\sin(\\frac{2\\pi }{14}(10.5))) \\\\  & =140(10.5){e}^{-2.1}-90-15\\sin\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =140(10.5){e}^{-2.1}-75 \\\\  & \\approx 105.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $105$ cars per hour."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b221c18-6d59-5ed7-9a36-5ef2cdd6198b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$228$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517325} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea5bf78-0a77-58d1-9e2a-bacef9819834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-228$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517326} {:answer/id #uuid "5baff62d-cf1c-57ec-b9d7-d7a353611316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$288$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517336} {:answer/id #uuid "9b301213-2943-5d08-a28e-082e30a5e73f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$348$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517351} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d8817e-e114-506e-a9b2-a1055123a32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-60$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517357}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517325} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf15e5a0-9fea-5686-9f0a-1969e5135c52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8e5ec36a-ecef-5d7c-8e17-1882b5fa8983" :question/problem "A factory begins work at $8\\text{:}00$ am. Its order rate is $O(t)=190t e^{-0.2t}$ cars per hour and its completion rate is $C(t)=50+10\\sin(2\\pi t/10)$ cars per hour, where $t$ is hours after work begins. What is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction at the 24-hour time $10\\text{:}30$? Orders enter construction immediately. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "The rate of change is the order rate minus the completion rate. The elapsed time is $t=(630-480)/60=2.5$ hours.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(2.5)-C(2.5) & =190(2.5){e}^{-0.2(2.5)}-(50+10\\sin(\\frac{2\\pi }{10}(2.5))) \\\\  & =190(2.5){e}^{-0.5}-50-10\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =190(2.5){e}^{-0.5}-60 \\\\  & \\approx 228.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $228$ cars per hour."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6c992b-a9bf-50ec-a63b-953cb1055324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-121$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517328} {:answer/id #uuid "48c1409d-fc69-54ea-b9a0-2713986d307a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-35$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517333} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb55a74-3f47-50c3-a675-afc6dec9b4f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$121$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517337} {:answer/id #uuid "b03e3130-ebeb-56a0-a735-21aa477c71ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$156$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517354} {:answer/id #uuid "de1c0611-7856-5339-b7b2-d83aedf32026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$191$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517371}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517337} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a42ed69-259b-5d9d-b5a0-92f539029c30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bd6eb95d-7375-5343-986b-68f30ad5b2d2" :question/problem "A factory begins work at $8\\text{:}00$ am. Its order rate is $O(t)=90t e^{-0.2t}$ cars per hour and its completion rate is $C(t)=20+15\\sin(2\\pi t/14)$ cars per hour, where $t$ is hours after work begins. What is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction at the 24-hour time $11\\text{:}30$? Orders enter construction immediately. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "The rate of change is the order rate minus the completion rate. The elapsed time is $t=(690-480)/60=3.5$ hours.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(3.5)-C(3.5) & =90(3.5){e}^{-0.2(3.5)}-(20+15\\sin(\\frac{2\\pi }{14}(3.5))) \\\\  & =90(3.5){e}^{-0.7}-20-15\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =90(3.5){e}^{-0.7}-35 \\\\  & \\approx 121.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $121$ cars per hour."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f23811-b1ee-5151-b6b6-f608a52a57d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$76$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517323} {:answer/id #uuid "87ffd388-fbdd-5be1-b220-e00d63314682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-100$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517345} {:answer/id #uuid "90fa38bd-a1a3-5389-bd58-781b51e91810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-76$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517348} {:answer/id #uuid "cf7e8946-b8bd-5c22-9ef3-b4fcc20d9ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$24$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517365} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9bf1a9-9ed3-510e-8652-4bea14db43c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$124$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517379}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517348} :answer-field/id #uuid "d104f5d3-66ed-5d0f-978b-7632a7a40047" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d1c8cfbb-47f4-5fb3-a9b0-9087d81918f8" :question/problem "A factory begins work at $8\\text{:}00$ am. Its order rate is $O(t)=50t e^{-0.1t}$ cars per hour and its completion rate is $C(t)=80+20\\sin(2\\pi t/2)$ cars per hour, where $t$ is hours after work begins. What is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction at the 24-hour time $8\\text{:}30$? Orders enter construction immediately. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "The rate of change is the order rate minus the completion rate. The elapsed time is $t=(510-480)/60=0.5$ hours.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(0.5)-C(0.5) & =50(0.5){e}^{-0.1(0.5)}-(80+20\\sin(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}(0.5))) \\\\  & =50(0.5){e}^{-0.05}-80-20\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =50(0.5){e}^{-0.05}-100 \\\\  & \\approx -76.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $-76$ cars per hour."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088dec50-c3c6-50e5-9b69-0e7ce15be0f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-60$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517322} {:answer/id #uuid "6de37857-a139-5858-9e56-fc09b996a51d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$129$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517341} {:answer/id #uuid "b20f23c0-8c87-5d2b-ba53-496addf07a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$69$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517356} {:answer/id #uuid "e224aaed-7ace-5ee6-9efa-77876f3b74e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-69$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517373} {:answer/id #uuid "e2822869-04a9-5b0d-8a9d-94e5390e105e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$189$ cars per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517374}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517356} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c87e9ae-3f6c-51f1-aa5d-040dc2bf3415" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f2e135c6-4cc0-5a93-bc48-e0df9714cad2" :question/problem "A factory begins work at $8\\text{:}00$ am. Its order rate is $O(t)=100t e^{-0.1t}$ cars per hour and its completion rate is $C(t)=40+20\\sin(2\\pi t/6)$ cars per hour, where $t$ is hours after work begins. What is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction at the 24-hour time $9\\text{:}30$? Orders enter construction immediately. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "The rate of change is the order rate minus the completion rate. The elapsed time is $t=(570-480)/60=1.5$ hours.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(1.5)-C(1.5) & =100(1.5){e}^{-0.1(1.5)}-(40+20\\sin(\\frac{2\\pi }{6}(1.5))) \\\\  & =100(1.5){e}^{-0.15}-40-20\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =100(1.5){e}^{-0.15}-60 \\\\  & \\approx 69.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change is $69$ cars per hour."} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8ca8f9-1d26-55d7-9b86-8c52d09b0ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517660} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ea4930-c661-5fcb-a591-ae748e162fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517804} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6eb5e3-7ae1-506a-bc7d-d480b4fe04c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517807} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a6152f-0b20-5bb6-96d5-09c1f86baab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517870} {:answer/id #uuid "eac115c2-4ed8-51a6-aa73-4f0569e0d56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1fab290-16e7-5979-bd17-fff2703816f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517822} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7e79ac7-4b47-5738-a30f-3fb1606b45fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "35ae1f33-1dce-5ef7-af9a-1cf242b3dd81" :question/math-academy-id "q-131248" :question/problem "$f(6)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-f(-3) \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-\\left(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ on $[-3,6]$ consists of two parts: \n\n* an arc of a quarter circle for $x\\in [-3,3],$ which lies above the $x$-axis, and \n\n* a line segment for $x\\in [3,6],$ which lies below the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,3].$ Earlier, we found that\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,6].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ab \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the original integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find $f(6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6) \\\\  & =2\\pi -6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e252ff-bff2-5a0c-8722-678c22a7c16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517501} {:answer/id #uuid "070f81d7-bbdd-59f5-971a-c1e85fd656fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517503} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8dff85-9179-52c1-a1eb-e2b80b8d392e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517603} {:answer/id #uuid "636cdbc8-2c6a-553b-a040-1b1eb24c4aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517671} {:answer/id #uuid "9a89bbd5-05e0-5b9e-875f-8fbff4dcb441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517783} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa6bc3a1-b754-5de6-a15a-17b5690dd1bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "36bc1d86-9d95-5fad-8592-9df3687ca62a" :question/math-academy-id "q-86533" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,-1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,0⟩-⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨3,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8x(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}),\\,36{y}^{3}-24{x}^{2}y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1) & =⟨8(-1)\\cdot (2(-1)^{2}-3(-1)^{2}),36(-1)^{3}-24(-1)^{2}(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,-1) & =∇f(-1,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca5ecce-5260-5c1e-8f33-b8f542d178e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517517} {:answer/id #uuid "3072f94c-01b5-54a9-b170-a51dc79492ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517581} {:answer/id #uuid "5eaedd3e-5549-5a47-bec3-16e990341664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517661} {:answer/id #uuid "b151edaa-d755-5566-a03a-6ad23995bfa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517821} {:answer/id #uuid "d55eae46-1605-5397-8e51-d16aa7ce1714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517892}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517517} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bd3a5dd-dd4c-547c-b5d1-8d0a45894fb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5810c34a-c4d1-5c91-ad48-b47e68d3d961" :question/math-academy-id "q-163495" :question/problem "![](images/85/8524ffd2ff653703bd6fcfa2b44d36821aeb34877d58bbc6df388497cac8e07d.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $x$-axis and the lines $y=2x$ and $y=4-2x$, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $y=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 2-\\frac{y}{2}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/29/293f8454cdda5897de111d6444baa4674eccf28a36e2053f3f2cb71f948e07fe.png)\n\n  \n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $y=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|y-2|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y/2}\\frac{x}{2}|y-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y/2}x(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y/2}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4){[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=y/2}^{x=2-y/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[{(2-\\frac{y}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)}^{2}]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)(4-2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}-2{y}^{3}+12{y}^{2}-24y+16\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}+4{y}^{3}-12{y}^{2}+16y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-8+32-48+32) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2501361d-dea7-52d8-95f4-909628b1ca49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1e/1e356ae7a05aed6c957e3f4bc4d0bb9cd3306fde032cdc4fce13b3f2670590f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517557} {:answer/id #uuid "662d296e-2fb6-522b-9d87-8b74a1007e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/01/01ee0bf05b0e8e3e3c42ef77d38a9613b33ef16106fd661646cedf1aead67652.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517676} {:answer/id #uuid "70937625-c7a6-5e1c-afbe-168a45b60842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/25/257a44d0001820f6df4c3039d7fd24b267bca0031140ccfb5fadd791848eb2d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517704} {:answer/id #uuid "77d8afa2-9300-57e8-9603-62547c09412e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/58/58ca054838929e04cbc16afaf793f46eef9bcbc9e29dc816419467b478ebdc60.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517714} {:answer/id #uuid "9b279c76-6d9a-53b2-819c-9b6329f30287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1c/1ca104b65355875e55dcabee9e2e553c265812eb6b43fdf1b42df17d9590799f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517785}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517714} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3499959-bd1a-5110-afef-51a07c10785e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "585b182e-7b17-5dc5-940a-b3dd1c063524" :question/math-academy-id "q-116853" :question/problem "If ${f}^{′}(x)=-|2x-5|$ and $f(1)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function  $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/5f/5f48f6278983a74be84b95984808b23745ab4960dbd427f942efe18df86768e8.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2})$ | $2$ | $(\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical line  $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$ \n\n- Since we are told that $f(1)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(1,\\frac{5}{2}),$ the point at $x=1$ must be higher than the point at $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\n![](images/1f/1ff02109b863615e5274b9ac41306644d8d42ce62671e0e76ecbda6655d249e0.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2})$ and on $(\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left of $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ and goes down to the right of $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=\\frac{5}{2}$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x=\\frac{5}{2}$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/93/931e7763569df18dd0aea8cd1363f6d8414f1a0cc8deddc964ba11a9c74fe127.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/ad/ad5016295fb3d33baac6af6e74dd3b2cbf563c74aef78bf27e5aed28222db3e7.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e8ca8f9-1d26-55d7-9b86-8c52d09b0ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517660} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ea4930-c661-5fcb-a591-ae748e162fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517804} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6eb5e3-7ae1-506a-bc7d-d480b4fe04c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517807} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a6152f-0b20-5bb6-96d5-09c1f86baab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517870} {:answer/id #uuid "eac115c2-4ed8-51a6-aa73-4f0569e0d56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517934}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517807} :answer-field/id #uuid "082380e4-f20f-513e-9d31-b936b5054d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6d63b608-6564-54eb-94c2-8afc07416275" :question/math-academy-id "q-86540" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,-1,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,-\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,8z⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,0) & =⟨\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,-\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,8\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b0c2e13-f46b-5cc2-a1f8-4fae6a1f6c14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{(1-x^{4})\\ln x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517749} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6519ced-6442-570a-bd64-3c6fcf3d8465" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "75d28ef3-1631-5b9b-9daa-067de055774b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342834" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}  $-\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAnd the quotient rule is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}(\\frac{u}{v})}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, chain rule, and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2})}^{′}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-{({x}^{2})}^{2}}\\cdot {({x}^{2})}^{′}-\\frac{\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{4}}\\cdot 2x-\\frac{\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(1-{x}^{4})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{2}}{x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf9422de-70d7-5b56-86c9-b6e0e3f8ac48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{16x^{12}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517855}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517855} :answer-field/id #uuid "7980ba46-cdbd-57c7-a587-1058e543ca1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7e33df1c-2868-500a-90eb-4948dc41205c" :question/math-academy-id "q-344305" :question/problem "If $y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡4{x}^{6},$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $8{x}^{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡4{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡4{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(4{x}^{6})}^{2}+1}}\\cdot {(4{x}^{6})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{12}+1}}\\cdot 24{x}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{12}+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44a5b82a-32ba-5916-9e64-71bad821a989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{16x^{12}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517622} :answer-field/id #uuid "6235e9dc-58a1-59ae-99c9-7e3dc3328c5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "81194d8a-eb61-596d-a974-e9c1b95b27d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-342186" :question/problem "If $y=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡4{x}^{6},$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $40{x}^{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡4{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡4{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{(4{x}^{6})}^{2}-1}}\\cdot {(4{x}^{6})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{12}-1}}\\cdot 24{x}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{40{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{12}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eac5166-dabb-580e-b725-9123713934f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x(3-2x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517578} {:answer/id #uuid "34c509d9-c9e3-5e5a-96bf-5cdd105d47dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3x(2-3x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517591} {:answer/id #uuid "38d279e8-7cae-5b93-951c-3a28c5addc73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2x(2-3x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517600} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd1ece5-ab7e-5496-92a9-91b0efce86bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3x(2x-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517874} {:answer/id #uuid "d5415a38-3db7-5d44-8a65-954ad5158627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x(3x-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517591} :answer-field/id #uuid "234ebf93-a04c-5d8d-8e1c-b425df18a6da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "96d64ea4-0cd9-5375-8219-9bae598e5ccf" :question/math-academy-id "q-96442" :question/problem "If $y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(3x-1),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(3x-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(3x-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-{(3x-1)}^{2}}\\cdot {(3x-1)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-(9{x}^{2}-6x+1)}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6x-9{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x(2-3x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e86dacb-298b-544e-9c38-9ba72f2fd531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21M}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517576} {:answer/id #uuid "6af1bedd-39d1-53f8-a5fa-012aad761837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19M}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517685} {:answer/id #uuid "97a1c003-97ab-53e3-ace4-a7b2493ed8a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{63M}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517775} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2bbd50-7d0e-58aa-a9e9-5914bb7ed4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23M}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517782} {:answer/id #uuid "ed99f3ae-797d-5ac0-9a9e-b9fcda70eaa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23M}{120}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517939}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517775} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ba9cf1e-7b09-5bd1-9530-9886c7103582" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c9bc333f-6b0c-5f6d-9c9d-d8671bcc2662" :question/math-academy-id "q-163668" :question/problem "![](images/de/de1ac9ccf84134913bbf8847ea9795a1ec83084602db705c8bef723897e7bd92.png)\n\nA triangular plate with mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed between the axes and the line $y=1-x.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $y$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a092d57585bd7b2803473c30328dd72f82a6611d8398de821099db72e2ed3621.png)\n\nAlso, the distance between a point and the line $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-3|.\n$$\n\nNext, the mass density can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k|x|=kx,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find the value of $k$ by integrating the mass density function over the plate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}kx\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{[y]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}x(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=kx=6Mx.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}6Mx\\,|x-3{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}6Mx(x-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6M{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x)[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6M{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x){[y]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6M{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x)(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6M{[-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}+\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6M[-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{7}{4}-5+\\frac{9}{2}-0] \\\\  & =6M[\\frac{21}{20}] \\\\  & =\\frac{63M}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "348d3e63-246e-544f-943d-87eec084c7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-2\\pi +4x-\\pi y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517589} {:answer/id #uuid "871ed938-ce33-556f-8ef5-892006482a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈4\\pi -x-2\\pi y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517743} {:answer/id #uuid "89f60499-597c-504c-905d-9aa123018b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈4\\pi -4x-\\pi y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517747} {:answer/id #uuid "8eab8e94-7ae8-562e-b6e4-834aa6cc6887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈-\\frac{1}{2}+\\pi -\\pi x+4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517755} {:answer/id #uuid "d43587aa-caee-5e81-afc1-75cb5372bcd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-4x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517887}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517747} :answer-field/id #uuid "95092209-0ff6-5c1f-932a-fa94ab6826d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf3c18cd-d64f-5eb9-99df-fc0a97727985" :question/math-academy-id "q-65339" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)+∇f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},y-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & ={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\, & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=(2)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)=-4, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)=-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2) & =⟨-4,-\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)+∇\\,f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,y-2⟩ \\\\  & =0+⟨-4,-\\pi ⟩\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,y-2⟩ \\\\  & =-4(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi (y-2) \\\\  & =4\\pi -4x-\\pi y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40f7c195-a751-5b71-9a87-0de1ab89d81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{x}^{3}-\\frac{21}{{x}^{5}}-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517615} {:answer/id #uuid "43bd9732-3c9a-5769-9c00-bbcb2a48446f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{x}^{3}-\\frac{28}{{x}^{5}}-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517620} {:answer/id #uuid "cf947bc2-e090-5053-8204-9ff869376488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{x}^{3}+\\frac{28}{{x}^{5}}-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517880} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d435a4-9bec-54a8-9486-d0e6717fcf24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24{x}^{3}-\\frac{28}{{x}^{3}}-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517917} {:answer/id #uuid "f2405522-cc9c-5a15-9cc7-6150b213efad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}+\\frac{21}{{x}^{3}}-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517620} :answer-field/id #uuid "059dbd28-656a-59be-8ed5-e0971d025a8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ec8a3447-04a1-55a0-a68b-6b9044cae34d" :question/math-academy-id "q-1866" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=3{x}^{4}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}}-12x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}}-12x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{-4})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12x) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4})+7\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-4})-12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4{x}^{(4-1)}+7\\cdot (-4){x}^{(-4-1)}-12\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}-28{x}^{-5}-12 \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}-\\frac{28}{{x}^{5}}-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215d83fe-4933-5de8-986e-15f0aee87618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨4,-3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517546} {:answer/id #uuid "2d55e643-74fe-5152-bfd8-c76baf2debc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-3,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517574} {:answer/id #uuid "6b204e6b-3938-557e-9c36-e6465fac0526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517687} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5a1144-2bb6-52a6-ae6a-f5e1b580463b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517790} {:answer/id #uuid "f5758c55-ad74-5dfd-b215-f4a7c6749383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,3,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517955}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517546} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0c283d-e80d-5a92-84dc-65204c4fbaa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f39e417c-6152-5bd7-a05f-5fd83a9d6cdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-130272" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(0,1,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{z}^{2},\\,-3{y}^{2},\\,2xz⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1,2) & =⟨{2}^{2},-3(1)^{2},2(0)(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨4,-3,0⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eacb6ae-6056-590f-819d-a963272134e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517579} {:answer/id #uuid "35a9c1f4-2e16-576e-924a-d4b678c80437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517594} {:answer/id #uuid "772eaf55-132c-5352-905f-c57fafed7229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12M}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517713} {:answer/id #uuid "91698260-fd1f-560e-af4f-3325f33e5dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8M}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517761} {:answer/id #uuid "c8bb2c02-211c-5a2e-a640-026cbe50166a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10M}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517869} :answer-field/id #uuid "35069258-2167-5032-8d01-4365ee34fd5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f58ffe9b-0496-58b1-afb0-490bd07410f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-163644" :question/problem "![](images/16/1664a86a1fee25cccbb710ad4335e4d69b36c24d64c95ae7bff9e54d2ac3a6b6.png)\n\nA plate with mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ and $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}$, as shown above. Given that the region has an area of $\\frac{4}{3},$ find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $y=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{2y}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d9c529fe2d238a3d6ac4e20cfcd571f980c28a4eb2c13497fab3ccb0d15ac6eb.png)\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $y=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|y-2|.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{3M}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $y=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2y}}\\frac{3M}{4}|y-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2y}}(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-2)^{2}[x]_{x={y}^{2}/2}^{x=\\sqrt{2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y-2)^{2}(\\sqrt{2y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\sqrt{2}{y}^{5/2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}-4\\sqrt{2}{y}^{3/2}+2{y}^{3}+4\\sqrt{2}{y}^{1/2}-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{[\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}{y}^{7/2}}{7}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{10}-\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}{y}^{5/2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}{y}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}(\\frac{32}{7}-\\frac{32}{10}-\\frac{64}{5}+8+\\frac{32}{3}-\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}\\left(\\frac{40}{21}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{10M}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cd0dcd1-823c-5bfe-a3b4-87f697bc1cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/88/88d6157b6d961bfcfe66f9d0635c7c75e754fa33d182971f3e8722821995a7bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518772} {:answer/id #uuid "3522f0ef-1ff6-5054-b459-1bc1a99959f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e2/e23ab97241e392652578b744558f27abf17a6f10887b4532bfd9198a0168d6de.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518778} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0ad3fe-aab5-5c80-a718-10250bc0a44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0f/0f22b1314960b7ddc22461df115e3cc8b883f463e39e0717f494486c9145c461.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518791} {:answer/id #uuid "7500cd0d-e37b-5f4b-928d-4f02799db350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8b/8bdff7dd1b78b495112bb64fcf8c7f88eaa5434924c248654afb645f9146db17.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518794} {:answer/id #uuid "935a7585-1072-568f-8a0c-50849d049cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/00/00fae4ff8c65eb9be03ec51e68ddf89e03122c366feefa1cc2f370d2fea8b789.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518794} :answer-field/id #uuid "07507bc4-4f53-51a3-a550-08f3dc917fb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "014ab122-2134-517b-b9a9-d71b60e66dee" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=-2 x y\\,i-x^{2}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,\\frac{3}{2}),(-2,- \\frac{3}{2}),$ and $(-2,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=-2 x y\\,i-x^{2}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-1,\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\left((-2)(-1)\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(-1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=3\\,i-j \\\\ F(-2,- \\frac{3}{2}) & =\\left((-2)(-2)\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(-2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-6\\,i-4\\,j \\\\ F(-2,\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\left((-2)(-2)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(-2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/8b/8bdff7dd1b78b495112bb64fcf8c7f88eaa5434924c248654afb645f9146db17.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a304352-c54f-51ef-8906-32ee9cdc8b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6c/6c75e8fe5ad41fd863eca4152c172bbba3084eba38b6e54c96f9e084669279ca.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518783} {:answer/id #uuid "67f43eb9-dbc2-5820-b523-2f926f10b9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e8/e829b1ebe911b3c3ae1a6e871d10a36931fef7f950933abda5ea226fd4c9ae66.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518790} {:answer/id #uuid "6f17cf54-8843-57c9-a3d9-0c5e55c95936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/09/09fee256ed52affac40d46982cdb3b2e3957677e81ec0aa3266d9885be9e1add.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518792} {:answer/id #uuid "773a8b98-66de-5e5e-b398-a5e44ac67b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5d/5decd9cc7f7088571cf3eecf9f50c7ec87e2f6182df1fbc4b55caaf8bc3e73d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518795} {:answer/id #uuid "d2fa96a1-5147-5de1-b5da-a48e24f11b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/26/267f2a39c365664697da7256d49767c2018b3f63d94a6f55e90b566cc554a27e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518790} :answer-field/id #uuid "d55fcac1-f13e-5aca-8efd-d6b03f32a8f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1b8d00f8-3412-52c9-9322-b2ebe7eaa245" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=2 x y\\,i-2 x^{2}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,\\frac{1}{2}),(-2,- \\frac{3}{2}),$ and $(2,- \\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=2 x y\\,i-2 x^{2}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\left((2)(2)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=2\\,i-8\\,j \\\\ F(-2,- \\frac{3}{2}) & =\\left((2)(-2)\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(-2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=6\\,i-8\\,j \\\\ F(2,- \\frac{3}{2}) & =\\left((2)(2)\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-6\\,i-8\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/e8/e829b1ebe911b3c3ae1a6e871d10a36931fef7f950933abda5ea226fd4c9ae66.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0deeee54-5137-5ac3-b9b4-0f091bb47502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/07/07235837dbd839f3603359295ecae29383f96956b0d5724fab8fcfb053dbfd46.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518768} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1efae7-38d6-5363-b86d-fd79f952e5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8e/8e3a8f6c8b863dd68bc426baf82151476a347d231f87a763199288b93cdf585c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518801} {:answer/id #uuid "923fd78a-1c49-58e4-81a3-5a3d262a0a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/61/61fff51ff54e097ae5a320948e16d1f93f1bbc1c5a170206b016dbcca1cb4fa5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518807} {:answer/id #uuid "b66cc1d0-bd1d-535d-be46-13f0c13305cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8f/8f967f2dccd9a990c7781f25d8d362a1ac26a