d "04b689a3-39ce-59dd-8039-cf17d3a57d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/98/987e9aabd76d28c679efb27b92a2536d103e99eb3f915e6731ec37c56286876f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518764} {:answer/id #uuid "27ad4c1e-99ab-58dd-85d9-2307786676a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fc/fc92738151b6a9f4c35708a3f911287a739b7f43366f3bb18c888ca4758ac4c2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518777} {:answer/id #uuid "56105024-507d-5779-8bfb-522422369448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2d/2d5356db4c62ed3cd1b0fd068f5d16b8ef6a3b8750011ca37ca3657ed2eaab28.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518786} {:answer/id #uuid "94538c99-4a36-5413-b710-fcab9dfdd249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e3/e3540bee55bf4207205efc609a695d4222c7707e614c86bfa8cb50b22b524d5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518809} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd868e0-9e5d-5768-8b1a-bb8fe5d92407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ed/ed2c4c2be8527c466b58b3c56d15559161e05dccdbcaaaf380320347f367bc47.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518812}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518764} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c4be32b-ebec-539c-b41e-ace6fb5e23bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b5efd26b-a36e-5669-8d57-24827ceb919a" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=2 x y\\,i-2 x^{2}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,- \\frac{1}{2}),(-1,\\frac{3}{2}),$ and $(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=2 x y\\,i-2 x^{2}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,- \\frac{1}{2}) & =\\left((2)(1)\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-i-2\\,j \\\\ F(-1,\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\left((2)(-1)\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(-1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-3\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(1,\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\left((2)(1)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/98/987e9aabd76d28c679efb27b92a2536d103e99eb3f915e6731ec37c56286876f.png)"} {:answer/id #uuid "35a0d67f-2001-5b60-b42a-f979cd7b3038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{4x}(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519074} {:answer/id #uuid "753c754d-a64b-5a86-bf3d-b11e9cb247e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4({e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519175} {:answer/id #uuid "762e2957-2bdc-5c9f-8c82-28884cca64d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4({e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519177} {:answer/id #uuid "a25c36b2-66bd-5740-89f4-e95b59786f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{2x}({e}^{2x}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519258} {:answer/id #uuid "c60203e4-de60-549e-8d11-366f51f688e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{4x}(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab5ae73e-ff79-5c66-9c67-13be3d4cfbbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4x+y}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519274} {:answer/id #uuid "b91672bb-93d2-50b7-8eb9-de13911fe838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2y+1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519294} {:answer/id #uuid "c97c539f-b49d-5a10-804a-fdc7d978fe94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+1}{4x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519320} {:answer/id #uuid "de6ccd7b-feeb-5a8f-8280-45a2401779e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+y}{x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519358} {:answer/id #uuid "e0409b5a-bd0f-5146-b9ba-ff2fcb1d149b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+y}{2(2x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519363} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e16ecfd-ecda-506a-a72f-57bf0aaac1ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0309d911-015b-5e4c-9346-30c2d14fb0b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-145100" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{4}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2x+y}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [4x+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+1}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2x+y}{4}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2x+1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+y}{4x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{2(2x+1)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ea33cc-5f23-589d-8c23-7c092cbc2f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519019} {:answer/id #uuid "2589e333-131b-51b5-ad48-bd1130f65246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519052} {:answer/id #uuid "871f5b77-fc09-5ab2-85ec-496ea72f30af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519207} {:answer/id #uuid "8ccd639c-35b2-5792-9629-fe551a25b276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519221} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b2770a-9e7a-586b-b171-0fbdfcac8124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519052} :answer-field/id #uuid "6064d30a-0a9e-55a1-8d02-c3cb038cef8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "05787bc3-261f-5b82-bb66-810cc0eb28ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-145108" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y+x}{4y+2}, & \\,0<x<2,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<Y\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{3}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<Y\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{3}{4}) & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{y}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{3}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+x)}{(4\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}(x+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3x}{2}]}_{1/4}^{3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[{x}^{2}+3x]_{1/4}^{3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}({\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}+3\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)-{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}-3\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\frac{9}{16}+\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c19089e-e70c-5d3e-89a1-8b1f43d5dfc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519062} {:answer/id #uuid "5373c9c4-344c-5fdd-ab6a-8f94ae077c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519120} {:answer/id #uuid "78bc99e5-2323-56ec-9a3c-1a503b64e353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519181} {:answer/id #uuid "8d69e6d5-8e64-51d5-9437-2837aa08c01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519223} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b7b55b-e405-57e3-9cfb-bb0f23fba298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519314}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519062} :answer-field/id #uuid "482e304e-d433-57bd-9c20-e198c527d454" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "069bdd21-6b0e-5746-9f9a-48c390ea491e" :question/math-academy-id "q-67539" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1e40e0ffa7880b3b3b63070f063de207dcfafbdbed5fdf02464e180feba715e2.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},3).$ Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=3$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nWhen we approach $x=3$ from the left, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "287f4c69-8bd4-5569-9333-3f219db98ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1)-Φ(-1.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519056} {:answer/id #uuid "38fb3acb-053d-552f-a825-7ddc2d36cb45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(2)-Φ(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519079} {:answer/id #uuid "728b0f84-6442-5c3f-96b2-7e64f3c56829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1.5)-Φ(-1.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519168} {:answer/id #uuid "798138a1-9c96-5b5d-9bc0-6edae93977b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519182} {:answer/id #uuid "f67a96ba-09f5-59df-948a-4c2d70d715d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(1)-Φ(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519397}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519182} :answer-field/id #uuid "64ef3aa0-5b31-5f1c-8e8c-420a16ef0aa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1e9c32a8-c5ef-5dca-8493-0a63cb017fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-143512" :question/problem "| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{101}$ be a random sample of size $n=101$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $2.5$ and $3.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=101,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+2\\cdot 0.7+4\\cdot 0.1+8\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0+1.4+0.4+0.8 \\\\  & =2.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.7+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{8}^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-{2.6}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+2.8+1.6+6.4)-6.76 \\\\  & =4.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2.6,\\frac{4.04}{101}) \\\\  & ∼N(2.6,0.04).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3) & ≈P(\\frac{2.5-2.6}{\\sqrt{0.04}}<Z<\\frac{3-2.6}{\\sqrt{0.04}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.5<Z<2) \\\\  & =Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(2.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3)=Φ(2)-Φ(-0.5).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "032b45ef-0f8d-5228-ab53-44d951ab7b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10.8\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518991} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef38244-8fdf-5af9-a467-8e572c929ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15.4\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519015} {:answer/id #uuid "3f275ff8-361f-502e-8e26-7ac774e7b63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.3\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519093} {:answer/id #uuid "c3efee66-91d8-51df-9c99-66043ccf9a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.7\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519316} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a1db81-4260-5c90-a443-a427b1dbd8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6.3\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518991} :answer-field/id #uuid "d314da54-c3a8-55ac-8066-a2906576f626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "217980d1-12ff-5532-90b2-461f43899f04" :question/math-academy-id "q-95091" :question/problem "A truck travels an average distance of $21\\,\\text{mi}$ with a gallon of gas. However, $15\\%$ of the time, a truck travels more than $26\\,\\text{mi}$ on a gallon of gas. Assuming that the distance the truck can travel with a gallon of gas is normally distributed, find, as a percent, the proportion of journeys that the truck travels less than $15\\,\\text{mi}$ on a gallon of gas. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0918\\,$ | $\\,0.0901\\,$ | $\\,0.0885\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ | $\\,0.1075\\,$ | $\\,0.1056\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ | $\\,0.1271\\,$ | $\\,0.1251\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the distance traveled by the truck with a gallon of gas. Then we have $X∼N(21,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X\\le 15).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>26)=0.15.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 26) & =1-P(X>26) \\\\  & =1-0.15 \\\\  & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 26) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{26-21}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{5}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)≈1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.2073 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.2073} \\\\  & ≈4.8239.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X\\le 15)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 15) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{15-21}{4.8239}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.24) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.24) \\\\  & =0.1075 \\\\  & ≈10.8\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $10.8\\%$ of the time, a gallon of gas travels only $15\\,\\text{mi}$ or less."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fa9e367-921c-5703-b5c8-f4ef0ec283c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519228}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519228} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b26d762-7065-5240-845a-7fa72921023d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "24bbcf4d-e35e-5166-86db-6c3a81f4f4ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-341493" :question/problem "If $y=-3\\,\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[-3\\,\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x)] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x)] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}]\\cdot (2x)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2x\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d76f20-9c01-572e-9bec-b4534a624512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16xy(4-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519095} {:answer/id #uuid "697349f9-9995-58d2-a52b-dd64ff7d8349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{y}^{2}(4-y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519146} {:answer/id #uuid "9307b7d8-60d8-57f7-a4d6-b9b56f00ed28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}(4-y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519235} {:answer/id #uuid "b28cbfc4-beef-5aec-838d-bb8099b27e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8x}{y(4-y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519285} {:answer/id #uuid "bd07ced2-884b-510d-b5ef-173e5b1d1caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4xy}{4-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519303}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519285} :answer-field/id #uuid "f41b096e-82c3-5dae-bf25-98903c7a8157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3b02f20a-35d0-57b1-ada9-f1273f1f3904" :question/math-academy-id "q-143641" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x{y}^{2}}{32} & \\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\frac{5x{y}^{2}}{32}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{32}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{32}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{64}\\cdot (y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256}, & \\,0<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{5x{y}^{2}}{32}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8x}{y(4-y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x}{y(4-y)}, & \\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b5b39e-b50f-5667-85aa-10f98316c5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+y}{3(y+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518997} {:answer/id #uuid "099b70eb-adf5-5c0b-935e-950c1e096556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{2y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519007} {:answer/id #uuid "41e1bf01-9793-54f8-9d28-af6f78aec84b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2y+2}{x+2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519096} {:answer/id #uuid "476db03a-613d-54d1-8c90-950c1e79d5b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2y+1}{2({x}^{2}+y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519104} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfec5ee-76b0-5274-a7c7-3fbca567739b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{3(2y+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519133} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cb8d45a-30b0-5de1-a6e8-4ab5704badae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "60bd267c-251c-5e47-b502-67f097fb7ef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-145101" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{12}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{12}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{y}{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{36}+\\frac{xy}{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{36}+\\frac{3y}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{y}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y+3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y+3}{4}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{12}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2y+3}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{3(2y+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{3(2y+3)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f577fac-247a-5f5a-8564-52657ee5b19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2x+2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519227} :answer-field/id #uuid "5de9fb92-f20f-5c9e-9ed0-941e3ef44838" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6edfa6ba-1072-5bf1-bdfe-2aaa6d5af318" :question/math-academy-id "q-341457" :question/problem "If $y=3\\,\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x-1),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{|x-1|}$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[3\\,\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x-1)] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x-1)] \\\\  & =3\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{|x-1|\\sqrt{1+(x-1)^{2}}}]\\cdot (x-1)^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{|x-1|\\sqrt{1+({x}^{2}-2x+1)}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{|x-1|}\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2x+2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0662a46f-bec7-54ca-b240-f60d5d5f1afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518998} {:answer/id #uuid "0e19b2e0-2b38-57c0-b50d-3e99a4fb345c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519013} {:answer/id #uuid "12efe453-d9bc-5e34-bdeb-04e1a1d22aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519022} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fe6c9c-6868-551d-8a19-e70fb67ab8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519256} {:answer/id #uuid "d13ea829-20bc-5000-ad9a-b878fad5ad6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518998} :answer-field/id #uuid "69041780-f401-5aab-a5e0-f58cc39dca02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "881df761-fca2-5afa-aea8-d6a6e63a7521" :question/math-academy-id "q-126866" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f1dd3c66e3bf7d25f286720b360d6af1d6361804ab3ff52835d94f78e5084ee.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-3y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(-3))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot ({4}^{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (16-2) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7d0ba3-02f6-59a7-be65-bf8f3801013a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519040} {:answer/id #uuid "3da4eaa1-6cda-5d99-b813-20a4d9738e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519091} {:answer/id #uuid "58bb21c1-450d-505d-b6dc-9db3c242bf5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519125} {:answer/id #uuid "70730109-0401-592f-81a2-e4405b7b0f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519162} {:answer/id #uuid "e7050eac-3947-55c2-b302-d2bd01b29003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519371}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519040} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c446274-85cc-56ab-805a-72556fc77fd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "91a2a3a1-8ae1-5b13-976f-9bc558602e26" :question/math-academy-id "q-143619" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x, & \\,y<x<1\\,,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$  \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{y}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{y}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}), & \\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $y<x<1\\,,\\,0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{(\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{1-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{1-{y}^{2}}, & \\,0<y<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}) & =P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{1}{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}\\frac{2x}{(1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2})}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}\\frac{2x}{\\left(\\frac{35}{36}\\right)}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}{[{x}^{2}]}_{1/4}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}(1-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{28}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07cfc785-fa96-5e22-b467-263332177c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519003} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae4d64b-01dc-56eb-9afe-2fc8eba19d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519082} {:answer/id #uuid "bb134a2b-094e-5864-a589-8e05c37b430c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519298} {:answer/id #uuid "c11f045a-855b-58ea-9cc2-fe6f72264ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519310} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e63b2b-06b8-588f-bae2-b8c4173b063c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519364}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519310} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c4d7f13-80f9-5cf8-8777-77cdf1cbaa54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9adae9aa-2d5a-51d9-80a6-fbe056bdc031" :question/math-academy-id "q-146394" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(0,\\frac{3}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0,\\frac{3}{2}) & =P(X\\le 0,Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 0,Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0,\\frac{3}{2}) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 0}{\\sum }_{y\\le 3/2}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(-1,-1)+f(-1,0)+f(-1,1) \\\\  & =0.15+0.25+0.05 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4444a1b2-e52a-551a-af2f-64eee4dc99a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8x^3}{\\sqrt{1+(x^4+2)^2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519097}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519097} :answer-field/id #uuid "cee78980-cb6b-5efb-8a9d-827bb3ab4617" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a0460884-e620-5591-b0cf-266181de3168" :question/math-academy-id "q-341470" :question/problem "If $y=2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({x}^{4}+2),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2}$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({x}^{4}+2)] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{|{x}^{4}+2|\\sqrt{1+{({x}^{4}+2)}^{2}}}]\\cdot {({x}^{4}+2)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{({x}^{4}+2)\\sqrt{1+({x}^{8}+4{x}^{4}+4)}}\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2}\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{8}+4{x}^{4}+5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519568 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30be591d-18e4-56ca-bd11-2cb0e73aa66b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519068} {:answer/id #uuid "52c36d35-dfd3-59ca-b51c-6f8f7df4b4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519119} {:answer/id #uuid "8ccaa219-a0ea-583d-9e0c-efdebeabb3e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519220} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f9475b-b7d3-5932-90b4-6269e7ee9da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519255} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b6de00-aaa3-58b8-919b-6ae993100929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519255} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7448890-beaa-51f5-b2f0-83828889fb49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a05be7fd-94f3-5604-ad03-bc6e74737579" :question/math-academy-id "q-143620" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{5}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{4}{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{5}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{4}{3}) & ={\\int }_{1/5}^{1}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{4}{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/5}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{1/5}^{1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28a6421c-8bb1-5e74-b5ce-4488dab6060e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519057} {:answer/id #uuid "93edd067-6d4d-5723-af3e-e6c5a01044b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519238} {:answer/id #uuid "a929b9b7-76e2-511c-af43-a8af03c8efa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519271} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f0f68c-757f-5ea6-b804-da070251f3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519296} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc4d93c-b651-5691-9445-a738e7a4241d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519300}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519300} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2ec7e2d-e700-5713-ba66-48bf13c5d6e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d2c3b01f-8b1d-5c9b-95df-4bf4aec9fac1" :question/math-academy-id "q-143622" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{4}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{x}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{x}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,0<y<2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4}y\\sqrt{x})}{(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y, & \\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}) & =P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y<\\frac{3}{4}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\frac{3}{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{1/4}^{3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09021ce8-047e-5b0d-92f8-36d32076c19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "78.2\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519005} {:answer/id #uuid "2d58aa30-649a-5d30-86fd-836c82705dcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "83.4\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519065} {:answer/id #uuid "7bce9ce0-a3ff-570e-86db-d819bc4f24bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80.8\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519187} {:answer/id #uuid "99059b85-08c0-5911-bef5-b258cf55a646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "76.9\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519244} {:answer/id #uuid "99eaaeec-7b23-5191-b1ac-c727b2e8bc60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "81.1\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519246}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519005} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d8d7f1f-3c0d-5b85-87f8-4a2364ded62c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d34b6be0-737c-5c5c-907f-c713ce8e6b32" :question/math-academy-id "q-133236" :question/problem "The average height of a player in a particular professional basketball league is $78$ inches. Additionally, $15\\%$ of players are over $82$ inches tall. Assuming that a player's height is normally distributed, find the percentage of players over $75$ inches tall, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ | $\\,0.1611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ | $\\,0.1867\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2206\\,$ | $\\,0.2177\\,$ | $\\,0.2148\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly selected player. Then we have $X∼N(78,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>75).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>82)=0.15.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 82) & =1-P(X>82) \\\\  & =1-0.15 \\\\  & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 82) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{82-78}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{4}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)≈1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.2591 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.2591} \\\\  & ≈3.85951.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X>75)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>75) & =1-P(X\\le 75) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{75-78}{3.85951}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le -0.78) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.78) \\\\  & =1-0.2177 \\\\  & =0.7823 \\\\  & ≈78.2\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $78.2\\%$ of players are over $75$ inch tall."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f88efb-26a7-5927-a3a0-854d67c40077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519199}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519199} :answer-field/id #uuid "9151a6ca-f2c2-5987-ba7e-6def5ae3cbbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d6b501ec-18d5-51e8-9a88-2c1c73e44bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-324738" :question/problem "The lifespan of LED light bulbs is normally distributed with a mean of $40,000$ hours and an unknown standard deviation $\\sigma .$ Tests show that $10\\%$ of bulbs fail before $36,000$ hours. Assuming a normal model, find the standard deviation, **rounded to the nearest integer.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $\\,z\\,$ | $0.75$ | $0.8$ | $0.85$ | $0.9$ | $0.95$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$h$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the lifespan of an LED bulb. Then we have $X∼N(40,000,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<36,000)=0.10.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<36,000) & =0.10 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{36,000-40,000}{\\sigma }) & =0.10 \\\\ Φ\\left(-\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.10 \\\\ -\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.9)=1.2816,\n$$\n\nand therefore, by symmetry, we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.10)=-1.2816.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & =-1.2816 \\\\ \\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & =1.2816 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{4,000}{1.2816} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈3,121\\,\\text{h.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb02d74-74dc-50e4-a17f-f8ce53c1c0a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{(x-1)\\sqrt{\\,x(2-x)\\,}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519017}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519017} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2e24b88-1d44-5062-9037-62a3b48a7fe6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "db2fa4fe-6b60-51ee-9a41-fc33e6f273b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-341497" :question/problem "If $y=-4\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x-1),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-4\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x-1)] \\\\  & =-4\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{(x-1)\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}]\\cdot (x-1)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(x-1)\\sqrt{1-({x}^{2}-2x+1)}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(x-1)\\sqrt{2x-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(x-1)\\sqrt{\\,x(2-x)\\,}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf6bb84-99f4-501b-898f-55beecfd0ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519011} {:answer/id #uuid "6e877167-064e-5961-ba0d-9292251730e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519157} {:answer/id #uuid "a07531b8-8752-5b56-b8df-4b8c0357540d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519253} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0bfe5f-9010-5b76-900e-a0a88d617f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519356} {:answer/id #uuid "f64b54b4-f561-592c-ab77-9cb0208b121a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15.51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519395}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519356} :answer-field/id #uuid "c153adbb-ebdd-563f-aa07-ecead9219cce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e64f0682-cd52-5135-b0b3-08e76e7a1873" :question/math-academy-id "q-95082" :question/problem "A group of students took a test and got an average score of $79.$ Also, $15\\%$ of the students scored lower than $66.$ Assuming that the scores of the students are normally distributed, find the standard deviation of their scores, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the grade obtained by a student. Then we have $X∼N(79,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 66)=0.15.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 66) & =0.15 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{66-79}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ Φ\\left(-\\frac{13}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.15 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)=1.0364,\n$$\n\nand therefore by symmetry, we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(1-0.85)={Φ}^{-1}(0.15)=-1.0364.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{13}{\\sigma } & =-1.0364 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{-13}{-1.0364} \\\\  & ≈12.54.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519603 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35a0d67f-2001-5b60-b42a-f979cd7b3038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{4x}(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519074} {:answer/id #uuid "753c754d-a64b-5a86-bf3d-b11e9cb247e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4({e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519175} {:answer/id #uuid "762e2957-2bdc-5c9f-8c82-28884cca64d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4({e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519177} {:answer/id #uuid "a25c36b2-66bd-5740-89f4-e95b59786f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{2x}({e}^{2x}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519258} {:answer/id #uuid "c60203e4-de60-549e-8d11-366f51f688e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{4x}(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519175} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac258ea5-31b3-5f32-97e0-aac682319621" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7c92bc5-8061-56c3-9e0d-1e01c4282d73" :question/math-academy-id "q-95511" :question/problem "If $y={e}^{4x}-2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡{e}^{2x},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{4x}-2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{4x})-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{4x}-2\\left(-\\frac{1}{|{e}^{2x}|\\sqrt{1+({e}^{2x})^{2}}}\\right)\\cdot ({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =4{e}^{4x}+\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4{e}^{4x}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}} \\\\  & =4({e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{e}^{4x}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "120c6703-0634-58a8-a321-af4d885ee95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519673} {:answer/id #uuid "5943f4aa-b2e2-5a97-a87c-3b111ac9e761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-9)^{2}+{y}^{2}=81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519699} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bccac3-0c41-58e0-9ee1-15518d59a74b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+9)^{2}+{y}^{2}=81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519722} {:answer/id #uuid "e779ed08-2645-52e9-a03a-13e3b57521fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=324" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519724} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a54f66-6469-5a59-9d1b-a092c3d32eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519727}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519673} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad71d333-d973-53d2-abe1-74b72dc17a5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "31669fee-6340-55d5-a19f-a1a86dd81325" :question/problem "![](images/56/56e82ecf0aa2a54d7281882c095f3c2b081f719cf3398be20155301d0f0361a0.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-9,0)$ and $(9,0)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-9,0)$ and $Y(9,0),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{((-9)-9)^2+(0-0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-18)^2+(0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{18}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{-9+9}{2},\\frac{0+0}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=9$ and center $(a,b)=(0,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(9)^2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "171998fa-a8e6-54cf-80fe-3036de63fc75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519674} {:answer/id #uuid "651dff32-22fe-5869-a86d-9b87382bde3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519703} {:answer/id #uuid "66bdb15e-6b1d-586c-9dde-95f0195f9fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+6)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519705} {:answer/id #uuid "b54ba1e3-0432-5d2b-a1ec-fc1fd635f071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519715} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc53105-6c05-5d72-a4dd-502ef32511be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519717}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519715} :answer-field/id #uuid "5870e946-998f-51a7-bde8-1513faefd91b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3d3ac566-19a8-54d2-a32a-fff15a573dbb" :question/problem "![](images/95/95467ab2e67bc1e188a5783ef201859c4e1216f071d6dc21b44adafcba8195c4.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-5,-4)$ and $(-1,2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-5,-4)$ and $Y(-1,2),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{((-5)-(-1))^2+((-4)-2)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^2+(-6)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 13} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{2}=\\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{-5+(-1)}{2},\\frac{-4+2}{2})=(-3,-1)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=\\sqrt{13}$ and center $(a,b)=(-3,-1),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-(-3))^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & =(\\sqrt{13})^2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f0b78b-b7f2-5576-a995-558bca6ff0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519672} {:answer/id #uuid "1f262cb3-168a-5972-b828-febf492f66cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+6)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519676} {:answer/id #uuid "50fb0255-70cf-5ecb-a1a4-dc8185b2fc80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519695} {:answer/id #uuid "920ee0b8-9faf-552a-91f9-3be587d126eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+9)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519711} {:answer/id #uuid "a68b3ca5-cfdf-53fa-b851-2ddcfe3fd25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519712}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519695} :answer-field/id #uuid "62018733-3b56-5178-89ef-816288b19d4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e71d13b-143e-5100-ac38-b145c988b060" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4afda0c1af30967941c20c9f70ce926a04e029eedfa605eb3c20d62af8303947.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-9,-1)$ and $(3,-1)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-9,-1)$ and $Y(3,-1),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{((-9)-3)^2+((-1)-(-1))^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12)^2+(0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{12}{2}=6.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{-9+3}{2},\\frac{-1+(-1)}{2})=(-3,-1)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=6$ and center $(a,b)=(-3,-1),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-(-3))^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & =(6)^2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a3f64a6-7826-547e-a7b7-53df738b623f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-7)^{2}+{y}^{2}=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519692} {:answer/id #uuid "50de2ef5-ddd9-528e-ab8f-241817f3dca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519694} {:answer/id #uuid "55708c35-0697-5f17-b709-5ae8338a0b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+7)^{2}+{y}^{2}=49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519696} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d01676-d1ba-5a15-8d7a-8cd472232df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=196" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519719} {:answer/id #uuid "eb21ace1-79e6-5dbe-a280-1c9fd5977a76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519694} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5dc3cc-6d23-518e-973a-a46e3aea3a49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "60ffb3ff-5c9f-5c7d-98b1-bdc0a2df9a06" :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce6082e3e2563073817e143e8ce91b10c171d0e894d5ed3bea25d925c6fe718f.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(7,0)$ and $(-7,0)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(7,0)$ and $Y(-7,0),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(7-(-7))^2+(0-0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(14)^2+(0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{196+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{196} \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{14}{2}=7.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{7+(-7)}{2},\\frac{0+0}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=7$ and center $(a,b)=(0,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(7)^2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =49\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19ee18de-8c7c-5191-abba-d9e423dbb249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519675} {:answer/id #uuid "2b367407-bcae-513a-b44f-ca4234bd257f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519682} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5d8570-211a-5bfa-a5cd-2ed26e97c918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519683} {:answer/id #uuid "ac40b80e-2187-5a82-b20f-a135cfe89815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519713} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaf3c40-9e4f-5ad6-9324-b888875f8e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519721}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519713} :answer-field/id #uuid "23d9e69b-cf15-53f1-9f50-45549244746a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7bbab9ef-16cb-51b1-9e9b-361fc6944044" :question/problem "![](images/80/806745d02166fd14df9f563b58fb05a40bc0d9bc96460f57c51ba3e7c6f6d15a.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(0,-4)$ and $(0,4)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(0,-4)$ and $Y(0,4),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(0-0)^2+((-4)-4)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(-8)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{0+0}{2},\\frac{-4+4}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=4$ and center $(a,b)=(0,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(4)^2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "294de1e0-df17-5790-8526-741804107d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519680} {:answer/id #uuid "30e03e58-9723-53bc-8602-1ba18cff8fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y-8)^{2}=64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519686} {:answer/id #uuid "661e3a68-f3e0-5e0e-bbe3-d0eb2b937889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y+8)^{2}=64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519704} {:answer/id #uuid "756b9ce5-8320-5c58-af78-93478cd41aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519707} {:answer/id #uuid "e2666db6-2efb-5fe7-9d45-09d1690ffd96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519723}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519680} :answer-field/id #uuid "28787899-aad1-54a0-b2cb-f9d4320ec70b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8cc7e9d5-ea1d-5e48-81ee-0a8128ff7c92" :question/problem "![](images/ba/ba4e8d2e873670ae8e941879bd54d1331af8c542b192d8a7a9407552326fbccc.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(0,-8)$ and $(0,8)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(0,-8)$ and $Y(0,8),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(0-0)^2+((-8)-8)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(-16)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+256} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{256} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{16}{2}=8.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{0+0}{2},\\frac{-8+8}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=8$ and center $(a,b)=(0,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(8)^2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bb36ef2-bedf-59c4-9261-353d7aa86fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519684} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8965f1-eac5-5caa-8cbf-b15f799e8586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519685} {:answer/id #uuid "5586edf2-a5a9-5859-8b98-fb7051bdbe45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519697} {:answer/id #uuid "8081d93e-a29f-592d-ab48-7265bbc11d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519709} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe67200-854d-55e4-9b30-70d3dca5c480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519709} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f2dd7ee-0390-5e7d-b56a-bdb4d7b3a503" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb6d3d4e-b5c4-5725-aabe-7476ef4c77c1" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d851d51fea696fe66461a27557f85d9182ac476578923905d113a2fa28237a4.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(0,-3)$ and $(0,3)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(0,-3)$ and $Y(0,3),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(0-0)^2+((-3)-3)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(-6)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{6}{2}=3.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{0+0}{2},\\frac{-3+3}{2})=(0,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=3$ and center $(a,b)=(0,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(3)^2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2adb3417-ab63-5657-8903-01b4dc4a2e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519681} {:answer/id #uuid "4464dc23-ef50-549b-8a55-b20c697f97d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519691} {:answer/id #uuid "64f02e67-b064-5f59-bd9d-187ca8ef0c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-8)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519702} {:answer/id #uuid "739c144f-3658-510e-99f8-2dcd6514ba59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519706} {:answer/id #uuid "b6127a40-494f-5e5c-87be-b73d338a9555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519706} :answer-field/id #uuid "20e42abd-473b-542b-bbb5-046316983b2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f169aee4-58d1-5815-b8f9-e4167dc8b0c0" :question/problem "![](images/16/16fe72490f7df0dcdd1b3ab78809da1091eccb69a3dda98e3bef08635af322ed.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(4,2)$ and $(4,-2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(4,2)$ and $Y(4,-2),$ we find that the length of the diameter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(4-4)^2+(2-(-2))^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(4)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2})=(\\frac{4+4}{2},\\frac{2+(-2)}{2})=(4,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=2$ and center $(a,b)=(4,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2 & =r^2 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & =(2)^2 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519776 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c01bee6-c22f-53b0-8e4c-2830202db2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519787} {:answer/id #uuid "4001b8b9-d69a-5393-aa8a-56dbb7b71d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}1\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519788} {:answer/id #uuid "810e6f01-13a2-54f8-a124-b8db7ad3029c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}- r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519808} {:answer/id #uuid "ec51caf8-2b6b-5744-bff0-e4556b7398b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519834} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f1c350-f5d6-57ff-b3fe-c711f8c6eee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}3 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519834} :answer-field/id #uuid "647d6e0d-520d-5948-bf78-b3d3e4c75a3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0ba10e69-e889-58f2-81da-5e14a75e9ef5" :question/problem "![](images/26/26fffdfc736d59a285ec7beb916417657c50a48494eace96e0db5d70cd7ce522.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=1$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=2$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/0a/0a8351f5cdfa59df7ec8a328c18b6e83342796728e11ff26911ce661eda1513b.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(1\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(1\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(1\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b85568-6d71-5d6e-b0c0-e13be22ee1d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}4 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519774} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5fa325-0187-5d8f-8b92-c7984a6bad98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519819} {:answer/id #uuid "e57a89d7-76e8-5cf7-9b59-0f813172b2dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519831} {:answer/id #uuid "e70e10fe-71f2-5728-9617-73637c24f0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{7}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519832} {:answer/id #uuid "f1080001-7044-5cae-8c5e-ca6c9563b93b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}-2\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519835}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519831} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e9f5a2a-077b-5bd1-8962-eac9dfdd211a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "442c0b16-fa87-5944-8279-ae2d47c10e31" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fb603f43f97d3444e3c0f520e340369f168a26ec7262e3ffa2cbc63490c4885.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(3 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$\n\nThe cross partials are $\\partial P/\\partial y=3$ and $\\partial Q/\\partial x=1$." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/1e/1e49b98f2c21c06e451fa70de5a19198fbf31a97f205c57138e8358725ded108.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=3,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=1.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(3 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(1-\\left(3\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(-2\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(-2\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- 2 r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eb32c19-5f84-5544-9090-e48b70d03e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{7}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519793} {:answer/id #uuid "580b60ef-002e-59af-a542-1b98052329fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519798} {:answer/id #uuid "b507406a-a117-557c-90c1-94673aefcc05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519822} {:answer/id #uuid "d7cd7c14-dc85-513f-9915-0266e55b64cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519829} {:answer/id #uuid "f79cb302-5362-5bd3-b93a-451fdc8f9345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r \\left(3 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r^{2} + 3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519829} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fb4e3ac-666f-5743-8a54-1264a234ed40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "54990ea5-aa3e-570c-b3da-1d09e2497f35" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fb603f43f97d3444e3c0f520e340369f168a26ec7262e3ffa2cbc63490c4885.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 3 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(3 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/1e/1e49b98f2c21c06e451fa70de5a19198fbf31a97f205c57138e8358725ded108.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x y^{2} + 3 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=3 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x y + 9 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=9 x^{2} + 4 x y + 3 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 3 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(3 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 3 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(9 x^{2} + 4 x y + 3 y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}-\\left(2 x y + 9 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(9 x^{2} + 2 x y - 9 y^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(9 x^{2} + 2 x y - 9 y^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 9 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008b25e3-2816-5f9f-8cbf-dc9c78374362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r \\left(3 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 12 r^{2} + r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519772} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd7deea-09e0-5087-a672-a35e9194cf64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{7}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 12 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519777} {:answer/id #uuid "298b7594-7652-50a3-b1aa-da40aaf63f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}- r^{2} \\left