ht)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x y + 12 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=12 x^{2} + 4 x y + y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 4 x y + y - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}-\\left(2 x y + 12 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,dA\\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 2 x y - 12 y^{2} + y\\right)\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 6,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 2 x y - 12 y^{2} + y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 12 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 12 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 12 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519800 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3533d152-2ad5-5d2e-97c3-7c3a8ef866ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}- r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta" :