ion/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "20ccc6f9-9078-5553-ae96-b36095f293ae" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 10 x + 5 y^{2})\\,i+(10 x y + 4 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}) & =(- 10 \\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right) + 5 \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^{2})\\,i+(10 \\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) + 4 \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right))\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{185}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{55}{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2})\\cdot ⟨x-\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right),\\,y-\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "21544ba6-ef06-5266-93a1-313a526067e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{85}{9},- \\frac{13}{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{3}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521685} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb23044-bcc9-5962-9105-7de29057842a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value 