493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8eb72b3b-1817-5b03-8db4-35e0f0a341f5" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=6 x^{2} - 6 x y^{2} - 5 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{5}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6 x^{2} - 6 x y^{2} - 5 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6 x^{2} - 6 x y^{2} - 5 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(12 x - 6 y^{2})\\,i+(- 12 x y - 10 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{5}{2}) & =(12 \\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right) - 6 \\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right)^{2})\\,i+(- 12 \\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right) - 10 \\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right))\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{111}{2}\\right)\\,i+(-20)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{111}{2},-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{5}{2})\\cdot ⟨x-\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right),\\,y-\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨- \\frac{111}{2},-20⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y + \\frac{5}{2}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bd85355-271e-50bb-88b1-d2b13a06d756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y + \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521693} {:answer/id #uuid "5706501c-de86-5634-87a7-ddcfe0773c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521697} {:answer/id #uuid "66a910cc-c44b-5f7c-b648-cb1fe9aff95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x - \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521701} {:answer/id #uuid "672bcb75-b4ca-5b71-9acb-7ec472d66c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{119}{4}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521702} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3912a8-6f0e-56f9-a5f1-d09dbac9861c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521728}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521697} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e6fa0b3-7471-5b24-b6ed-74973563e2be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "973a3ce1-53a3-5558-8746-a27a1f5bd217" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=6 x^{2} + x y^{2} + 4 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(- \\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(- \\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6 x^{2} + x y^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6 x^{2} + x y^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(12 x + y^{2})\\,i+(2 x y + 8 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =(12 \\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2})\\,i+(2 \\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) + 8 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right))\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{119}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot ⟨x-\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right),\\,y-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨- \\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3912a8-6f0e-56f9-a5f1-d09dbac9861c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d22a663-48a2-55aa-87ea-92afafe41064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{1}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521699} {:answer/id #uuid "67e3d0eb-1a49-5657-bd18-9920d2fdd192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,- \\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{1}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521703} {:answer/id #uuid "7e070eab-5072-5578-8ddd-95b7ea361e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{1}{2},\\,y + \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521711} {:answer/id #uuid "84fdbbbf-e7b5-59c4-baca-28b3b77d3ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\cdot ⟨x - \\frac{1}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521714} {:answer/id #uuid "e5326f5b-b542-524b-ac5a-a375a939b9ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{1}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521699} :answer-field/id #uuid "e884b373-05ab-556b-be57-d21657a01fc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d655f08e-ae0f-5e47-86c7-e30d50fc3764" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + 6 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x^{2} + 6 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x^{2} + 6 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6 x + 6 y^{2})\\,i+(12 x y + 12 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =(6 \\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right) + 6 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2})\\,i+(12 \\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) + 12 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right))\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\,i+(3)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot ⟨x-\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right),\\,y-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨- \\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{1}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "e5326f5b-b542-524b-ac5a-a375a939b9ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{1}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521740} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a67e1549-d894-58c3-ab0c-c5878c485999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521835} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1f00c6-1b40-51e8-8084-53cf529cfa21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521867}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521867} :answer-field/id #uuid "15959162-8202-56a1-a0f2-83319529d308" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{1}{2},2)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521763} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a081b4b-d2c2-54f6-ab72-e97e24330f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521827} {:answer/id #uuid "f122121f-13ed-5d40-84a7-05ee73afda8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521871} :answer-field/id #uuid "31f0b4d9-85ff-5f7b-a017-2aaa76c4d97a" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{1}{2},4)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521780} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06fb16ac-e893-51b0-abb9-b191df46e080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521750} {:answer/id #uuid "5135b9f5-3ff0-5dcb-bf6f-6769b8e27e1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521750} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac7dbd6c-1a1e-51c6-a4ba-ad8bbcb82f75" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{3}{2},12)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2566b226-78ba-5620-a2bb-20ed184b4f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{208}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521933} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b59d48-1162-5793-b1a3-916982210776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{416}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521953} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0e9667-6120-5443-8dc8-ac8fa4fa68ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521956} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c73d73-78d0-5721-ba8f-6cd192b7dbed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{208}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521958} {:answer/id #uuid "f31dccd4-370d-54df-a144-7a97b4575e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{208}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521958} :answer-field/id #uuid "b62663b9-d0d4-5a1d-8fb7-e42be263d7fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1b956245-4ce3-574c-9b58-4a7a33618b2d" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(3 x - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(\\frac{5}{3},1)$, $(\\frac{7}{3},1)$, $(\\frac{11}{3},3)$ and $(3,3)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=y^{2} \\left(3 x - 2 y\\right)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=3 x - 2 y,\\qquad v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y)\\mapsto (u(x,y),v(x,y)).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T^{-1}(\\frac{5}{3},1)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(3,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(\\frac{7}{3},1)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(5,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(\\frac{11}{3},3)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{11}{3}\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{11}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(5,3) \\\\ T^{-1}(3,3)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):3\\le u\\le 5,\\ 1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f9/f9dc752c5aeff34bc911428090212f09e0b3322041842726bd53dd7185563e7d.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\partial u/\\partial x&\\partial u/\\partial y\\\\\\partial v/\\partial x&\\partial v/\\partial y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}3&-2\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving these affine equations also gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u}{3} + \\frac{2 v}{3},\\qquad y=v.\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(3 x - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{1}{3}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{3}^{5}u v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int_{1}^{3}v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{3}^{5}u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{1}^{3}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{3}^{5} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\left(3\\right)^{3}/3-\\left(\\left(1\\right)^{3}/3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)^{2}/2-\\left(\\left(3\\right)^{2}/2\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\left(9\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{26}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(8\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{208}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0c6fa9-aade-53d1-abd5-5cb1c1bbefe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521928} {:answer/id #uuid "3748060b-856d-5d0b-97ae-459b4bd36b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{315}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521934} {:answer/id #uuid "6c92578a-fff4-5328-96e6-ec06e6681c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{105}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521940} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9e5a16-ac9b-581c-bf65-33d1385d0a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521941} {:answer/id #uuid "906ac085-5460-5a4d-85d7-d73344f3d71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{105}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521943}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521943} :answer-field/id #uuid "d646d279-52c1-531c-b6e3-5251737b2429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bf56e97a-0ba5-52a7-9ed0-519e4ee2c25e" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(- \\frac{1}{3},1)$, $(\\frac{2}{3},1)$, $(- \\frac{4}{3},4)$ and $(- \\frac{7}{3},4)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=y^{2} \\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=3 x + 2 y,\\qquad v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y)\\mapsto (u(x,y),v(x,y)).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T^{-1}(- \\frac{1}{3},1)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(1,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(\\frac{2}{3},1)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(- \\frac{4}{3},4)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{4}{3}\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{4}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,4) \\\\ T^{-1}(- \\frac{7}{3},4)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{7}{3}\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{7}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(1,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\le u\\le 4,\\ 1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/35/35d966f8a5a8e6e04353e202788230edbe6581fa7820e04fbe86406285c2194d.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\partial u/\\partial x&\\partial u/\\partial y\\\\\\partial v/\\partial x&\\partial v/\\partial y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}3&2\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving these affine equations also gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u}{3} - \\frac{2 v}{3},\\qquad y=v.\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{1}{3}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{4}u v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{1}^{4}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{1}^{4} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)^{3}/3-\\left(\\left(1\\right)^{3}/3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)^{2}/2-\\left(\\left(1\\right)^{2}/2\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(8\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(21\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{105}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f7bc959-ffc0-515f-8e2d-91ce2f38383f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522348} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3b46e6b-bbfb-526b-9cc5-0bb7578fda4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13eab2a4-15b8-5aae-888d-a5c1961fc009" :question/math-academy-id "q-343334" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac1e157b59bb632068e45776cdeb6bdc2dee42fd7f9b92ee2ea61401c1087d29.png)\n\nA rectangular block of ice is melting in a narrow channel that keeps its length fixed at $12\\,\\text{in}.$ Its width is decreasing by $1\\,\\text{in}\\text{/h},$ while its height is decreasing by $0.4\\,\\text{in}\\text{/h}.$ How fast is its volume decreasing when its dimensions are $5\\,\\text{in}$ in width and $4\\,\\text{in}$ in height? Assume that the volume's rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe volume is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L,$ $W,$ and $H$ be the length, width, and height of the ice block, respectively, and\n\n- let $V$ be the volume of the ice block.\n\nNote that $H,$ $W,$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ is constant. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=12\\text{ in},\\, & W=5\\text{ in}, \\\\ H=4\\text{ in},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\text{ in/h}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-0.4\\text{ in/h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the three time-dependent variables, $V,$ $W$ and $H,$ is given by the formula for the volume of ice block:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ treating $L$ as a constant, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(WH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L[W\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+H\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}\\right)] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L\\cdot W\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+L\\cdot H\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(12)(5)(-0.4)+(12)(4)(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-24+(-48) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of ice is decreasing at a rate of $72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122e6ac5-9cff-55b6-b18d-000a481d0709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(-0.75)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522055} {:answer/id #uuid "2a41c234-f9c9-5382-8487-7886f440fe59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(-0.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522115} {:answer/id #uuid "8465315b-a190-5203-b11a-3e21ec72ee2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(-1.25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522284} {:answer/id #uuid "a173100e-d855-5223-a3de-e706ed5de992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522356} {:answer/id #uuid "a4cbe218-05ef-50ce-b350-172a085d5edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(-1.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522364}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522284} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3d35207-52bf-5e87-8342-86fa07643744" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "62924b2e-7117-58f6-a2b2-963583643766" :question/math-academy-id "q-143515" :question/problem "| $x$ | $-8$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.3$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{60}$ be a random sample of size $n=60$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is less than $-4.5.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=60,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 0.3+(-4)\\cdot 0.3+(-2)\\cdot 0.3+2\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =-2.4-1.2-0.6+0.2 \\\\  & =-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =((-8)^{2}\\cdot 0.3+(-4)^{2}\\cdot 0.3+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 0.3+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =(19.2+4.8+1.2+0.4)-16 \\\\  & =9.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(-4,\\frac{9.6}{60}) \\\\  & ∼N(-4,0.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.5).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.5) & ≈P(Z<\\frac{-4.5-(-4)}{\\sqrt{0.16}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1.25) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(\\overset{―}{X}<-4.5)=Φ(-1.25).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0fc3bc0-4495-5062-ae38-1bffc23ec0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522353}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522353} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0041f63-d78b-5da5-99c4-ad55d0e109f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "68787380-f6be-5299-8d7e-90d33587bc3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-133019" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)\\,=\\,\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we can assume that $x>-1.$ So, we use the following expression for $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{x+1}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can evaluate the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{x+1} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x}-\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x-\\frac{1}{x})}{(1+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x-0}{1+0} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e5a6f2-9272-5161-919b-04845a8c04cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522016} {:answer/id #uuid "227a5d12-1270-57e4-a81e-b7bae3293ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522096} {:answer/id #uuid "6939f3f9-14d3-5391-9ce7-fd73d9fe6ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522233} {:answer/id #uuid "f49a3493-cb55-5f67-9873-fc200a3a90df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522016} :answer-field/id #uuid "29458fa7-bc42-566e-b9be-960433c46a54" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522571} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a889c5f-232c-56ec-a946-3158ebcdf724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522116} {:answer/id #uuid "573c88a4-54e3-5d7b-89ae-ccedc09f8124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522203}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522203} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f347663-10f1-5532-a12b-d4da98b820ac" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522577} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c01b521-60b6-5e5d-b0cc-b60e357ca0d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522183} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9b5a66-386d-56fd-81ac-ba2d1b72d927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522211} {:answer/id #uuid "7761490e-2471-53cd-b24c-0eb82d5b5ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522255} {:answer/id #uuid "c01de03c-b009-5c19-b2bd-5a84db6492ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522255} :answer-field/id #uuid "38a73ecb-974e-5f13-bd82-ea43d6ba0541" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522584} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33ddc21f-7e7b-50ee-9358-1aa4cfe9ea84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522135} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a5d271-a082-52cc-b59c-a8d7a6852f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522430} {:answer/id #uuid "dd21a3dd-c95f-569a-b81d-f3400fccdd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522480} {:answer/id #uuid "ef289e17-8821-5fba-bd64-4b54b677eb1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522480} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccf42bc2-7de5-54af-91ad-b973a99aeb58" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522654} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66779975-74ee-50cf-a35c-645d398b77e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522231} {:answer/id #uuid "b5aa77fe-dec0-5160-8a2e-1b65c8cb3c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522392} {:answer/id #uuid "c18abbb0-d30b-551c-9178-0ec9bf716e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522421} {:answer/id #uuid "e69ac2ef-dd0e-5e72-9a1f-61bf0d3b2e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522421} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1467dc5-08ea-5550-9843-e23f5fff69d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b0fd3313-c314-58d3-a500-207bbe04cd58" :question/math-academy-id "q-343403" :question/problem "Consider the function $F(x)=x$ defined on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-3}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we separate the Fourier series into its odd and even terms as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)∼2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)x)-2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1}-2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n}\\cdot 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dbc4c08-f008-5e72-b85a-8f2b4179ee34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522152} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfefb36-0c70-50b6-aa6a-4945530149e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522185} {:answer/id #uuid "97e3dd6a-5cc0-5fa9-95f1-4e769dfdd8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522330} {:answer/id #uuid "c921974f-fc45-5558-acc3-f2310130ec21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522438} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8b09ec-156b-5830-8ce7-eac0fbd892c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522330} :answer-field/id #uuid "e00110c2-7288-57e8-9508-75b52e302e96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c40cfd48-e992-514e-93b1-f5d1aa09838d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162128" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=3x+2z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{12}{1+(3x+2z)^{2}}\\,dV,\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\le 2x+y\\le \\frac{2}{\\pi },\\,\\,0\\le 3x+2z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=3x+2z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\le u\\le \\frac{2}{\\pi },\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{12}{1+(3x+2z)^{2}}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{12}{1+{v}^{2}}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{4}{(1+{v}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/\\pi }^{2/\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{4}{(1+{v}^{2})}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/\\pi }^{2/\\pi }\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{(1+{v}^{2})}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{1/\\pi }^{2/\\pi }\\cdot {[4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡v]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{\\pi })\\cdot (4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08668600-cdfa-52f8-ab7a-d4a36c8ad7e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522032} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffc0230-8021-5fb9-97c8-3bdfd65c4744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522091} {:answer/id #uuid "aa044da1-85a9-511d-b5d4-f73842833f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522376} {:answer/id #uuid "b9fdd295-7d5b-5a27-b880-407ff1f5f955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522403} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd04df4-f6d0-543d-b41f-3f62636bc031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522478}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522376} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed19dedc-ec19-5969-a25e-5d950eb640c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f5454beb-e917-5a8e-86e1-4e0232e2e094" :question/math-academy-id "q-170681" :question/problem "Two groups of high school students are competing in a math contest. Group $A$ consists of $5$ girls and $3$ boys, while Group $B$ consists of $2$ girls and $4$ boys. For the first challenge, one group is randomly selected, and one student is randomly selected from that group. If the selected student is a girl, what is the probability that she is from Group $B?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that Group $B$ is selected, and let $G$ be the event that a randomly selected student is a girl. Then, we require $P(B|G),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(B|G)=\\frac{P(G|B)P(B)}{P(G)}=\\frac{P(G|B)P(B)}{P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${B}^{′}$ is the event that Group A is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since Group $A$ consists of $5$ girls and $3$ boys and Group $B$ consists of $2$ girls and $4$ boys, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|B)=\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P(G|{B}^{′})=\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\n- Since the group is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=P({B}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|G) & =\\frac{P(G|B)P(B)}{P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{5}{16})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{23}{48}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34829eb7-6719-552f-8266-3bcf7f219ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522944} {:answer/id #uuid "8bea5321-bfba-5f11-882e-597a3ae32f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 49 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522975} {:answer/id #uuid "957ec940-be00-564b-84f4-3a10e064bebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "49 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522979} {:answer/id #uuid "baab0cd4-8a65-55f8-bca2-1b1c2c758696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "49 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522989} {:answer/id #uuid "ca345e82-08eb-5f23-8234-ec70304b7331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "98 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522997}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522989} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6fa5255-2305-5def-8b7b-fbdb0735307d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0345c7e2-c321-58ac-aefe-5fccd5158803" :question/problem "![](images/19/19689860cee70dfdb45a3d2f6708bcdab42b82b3ea20ea352ee0b2060fe7be13.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨7\\sin(t),\\,7\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\nr'(t)=⟨7\\cos(t),\\,-7\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}7\\cos(t)\\cdot 7\\cos(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}49\\cos^{2}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a7ae7de-4b39-5199-a3af-3688452d8e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12 e^{\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}} e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522936} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff9f6b2-7616-54e5-a633-bdcd628c3b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24 e^{\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}} e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522947} {:answer/id #uuid "50df1486-59c2-558a-9e12-a5e77bcb6b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6 e^{\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}} e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522952} {:answer/id #uuid "94b9da8f-4636-51b4-a729-7c089c8b493f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 12 e^{\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}} e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522978} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b0af74-9ede-5f11-9941-9a3c114b4dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12 e^{\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}} e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523004} :answer-field/id #uuid "158cdc91-dce0-52b4-a1a1-b986b80318b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "04fa4661-86e7-5801-8b1a-25ba13abd1bf" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0ac24d590f74a62bd45885b90190b82018372fd086d8cdda6ee8fde115d6fdd.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨6{e}^{\\sin(t)},\\,2{e}^{\\cos(t)}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨6\\cos(t)\\,{e}^{\\sin(t)},\\,-2\\sin(t)\\,{e}^{\\cos(t)}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{e}^{\\cos(t)}\\cdot 6\\cos(t)\\,{e}^{\\sin(t)}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }12\\cos(t)\\,{e}^{\\cos(t)+\\sin(t)}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f22653-76d9-5256-9778-31f07dcd3d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522924} {:answer/id #uuid "5c552cc3-2adb-520e-a6b3-039e7ed2d9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522958} {:answer/id #uuid "ab669a85-8fb2-5f65-82b9-02c289003c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 144 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522986} {:answer/id #uuid "b430d3ae-454a-5f43-ac42-5b5ac3bdd892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "288 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522987} {:answer/id #uuid "c98ef4ba-9946-5878-9a9c-87565e927c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522996}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522924} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3d47b0d-4636-5f8a-a6bc-cc39c7cfd9c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0660c121-2d4f-5acb-9dc8-3ef2b434821c" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3fc4346f3603d6d844584973c30b8ecf1e738a4d0a393ed1ab3ec62f764596cc.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨12\\sin(t),\\,12\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\nr'(t)=⟨12\\cos(t),\\,-12\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}12\\cos(t)\\cdot 12\\cos(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}144\\cos^{2}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "452acad0-00a0-5eec-ab27-f71c61d53934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522948} {:answer/id #uuid "58d221ae-abe3-5f2b-be11-4806b917be10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 48 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522955} {:answer/id #uuid "d51db3ba-41c7-5f75-9e3b-5a6f7020c0d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523001} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1b872d-a88f-56e4-b708-d873c11f1437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 24 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523006} {:answer/id #uuid "f861190f-37e1-526c-9a49-e7857165cb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523006} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeb87e21-a6e7-5c99-aa1e-16ae42bb31df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "09703150-b035-52b8-8944-6a1032133d7c" :question/problem "![](images/70/70ef514109413878d6cafb2931b51ee1d1b4