C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\cos(t)\\cdot 2\\cos(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }12\\cos^{2}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3203a4fa-5ae0-5125-8fbb-39e17ffbb694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522943} {:answer/id #uuid "3e9740e7-aef1-572d-9f3a-1adfe447e4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522945} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf95782-8ccd-5997-a933-6e6fc450a2dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522957} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7e13f9-34cb-5d83-97ac-3c2e929b44e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522961} {:answer/id #uuid "eaed16ae-ef41-5288-8962-473d94694a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 40 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522945} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bfc73f6-00d7-59f6-908a-659d4aeea6cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "98a5e8f9-74c0-516d-a55a-8d8752614077" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8ab774269b76212bc10b4364104217fe24209cf6f3fdf910cba6a63ff1876a49.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨10\\cos(t),\\,4\\sin(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-10\\sin(t),\\,4\\cos(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }10\\cos(t)\\cdot 4\\cos(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }40\\cos^{2}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f4a1110-34fc-5148-a06f-96646119ba61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 72 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 48 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522942} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3bdd1f-89e4-5d99-bc21-dfa17ad588d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 9 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522946} {:answer/id #uuid "5169bc62-4492-5bdf-b8a8-6d2f84ea9544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 12 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 12 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522953} {:answer/id #uuid "9c99c8aa-da9b-5733-98eb-0e6ec4d6b9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 36 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 24 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522982} {:answer/id #uuid "c12808f6-237f-5b1f-86c7-3023f50a79af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 36 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 24 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522991}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522982} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a97a502-7cc1-5fd9-bd8a-0e28fefbb90b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a3bed103-88e7-5529-bf46-ac64ed093a94" :question/problem "![](images/de/de9f562dd470e2377e39a7f604b34ca044a4da158ddb0877757b65387c3e2003.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨4(\\sin(t)-\\sin(2t)),\\,3(\\cos(t)-\\cos(2t))⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨4(\\cos(t)-2\\cos(2t)),\\,3(-\\sin(t)+2\\sin(2t))⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}4(-\\sin(t)+\\sin(2t))\\cdot 3(-\\sin(t)+2\\sin(2t))\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}12(\\sin(2t)-\\sin(t))(2\\sin(2t)-\\sin(t))\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0532a3d5-8ee1-550f-b0bf-c235d929f9f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25\\pi}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523018}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523018} :answer-field/id #uuid "02d2a19b-0275-5b3e-a499-333cc75b2412" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523017} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a169120-7df4-5f55-beae-de78fbf968d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19\\pi}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523050} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b168f6f-49e1-5695-9ee4-29603fc3f97a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523044} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4087d16f-41ff-55cd-8c2e-08b69a6826be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523038}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523038} :answer-field/id #uuid "781b488a-edfc-5c44-84c9-4de420c90a9f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523049} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c01f4bfb-77a4-54f6-98c5-f72c64b9a004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523072}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523072} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0931c90-812a-5983-b711-8c99d2ced8b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "01b49672-000e-56a4-a472-53c03786af3d" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})+8,\\,y=4\\sin(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})-9,\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le t<\\frac{5\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-8\\sin(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8\\cos(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8\\cos(2t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{7\\pi}{12}, \\frac{13\\pi}{12}, \\frac{19\\pi}{12}, \\frac{25\\pi}{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{12}\\right) & =-8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{13\\pi}{12}\\right) & =8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{19\\pi}{12}\\right) & =-8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{25\\pi}{12}\\right) & =8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{7\\pi}{12}, \\frac{13\\pi}{12}, \\frac{19\\pi}{12}, \\frac{25\\pi}{12}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025a69e7-e144-5e44-be3d-307e07729fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523016}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523016} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f620562-bc36-5065-97f2-e05de27b5688" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523056} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7a04206-b140-5d42-bde5-4231171751d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523067}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523067} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f366e8-f2dc-5583-8eb0-e347df6aa6ab" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523059} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f7c0d8c-1437-5316-8794-8415de166fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523057} :answer-field/id #uuid "a84c30dc-6e6a-537e-aa83-0c1014fdcbb7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523061} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ea4a20f-8288-58e3-bdf8-14dbf16d306b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523033}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523033} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb4a414a-b80a-51ce-8141-525ff7e47ea3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "146bf2ed-a4b6-50b6-a118-3016f8e80fe9" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})-4,\\,y=3\\sin(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})-7,\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le t<\\frac{5\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-12\\sin(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\cos(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5\\pi}{8}, \\frac{9\\pi}{8}, \\frac{13\\pi}{8}, \\frac{17\\pi}{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{8}\\right) & =-12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{9\\pi}{8}\\right) & =12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{13\\pi}{8}\\right) & =-12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{17\\pi}{8}\\right) & =12\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{5\\pi}{8}, \\frac{9\\pi}{8}, \\frac{13\\pi}{8}, \\frac{17\\pi}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523029 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33356aae-e56a-5b04-9377-c9bf2dc3b354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{29\\pi}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523034}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523034} :answer-field/id #uuid "9884991f-b474-5bed-ac12-6297bbde8db8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523055} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b5663f-ab15-59a8-aa75-83c593f9fcb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{47\\pi}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523020}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523020} :answer-field/id #uuid "aca313d6-1360-5578-942b-e8db280a47a5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523063} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24556c01-0a33-5acf-b303-408270710678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{35\\pi}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523030}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523030} :answer-field/id #uuid "be7d311c-6f18-572a-96ad-7b471600ecb8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb4b3d8b-8408-5a07-8b96-b611499bb8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{41\\pi}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523078}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523078} :answer-field/id #uuid "e310d615-5e31-5b3a-a159-6f6f20ad7686" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "236ca542-1844-556b-915c-8a48dc544a06" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})+3,\\,y=3\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})+4,\\,2\\pi\\le t<4\\pi.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-8\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{29\\pi}{12}, \\frac{35\\pi}{12}, \\frac{41\\pi}{12}, \\frac{47\\pi}{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{29\\pi}{12}\\right) & =-8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{35\\pi}{12}\\right) & =8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{41\\pi}{12}\\right) & =-8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{47\\pi}{12}\\right) & =8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{29\\pi}{12}, \\frac{35\\pi}{12}, \\frac{41\\pi}{12}, \\frac{47\\pi}{12}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e3498e6-008a-5057-ad9d-7ec79c9ade95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523037} :answer-field/id #uuid "5435fa61-0ac5-5850-a883-5353217fc66d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523042} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2771ef99-a72c-56a3-8f66-5915b40c7073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23\\pi}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523032}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523032} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e507193-5813-543c-a568-58480e966525" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523052} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eda112aa-4599-59b4-a5ab-157f6286fa38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523087} :answer-field/id #uuid "cabeb7bf-f695-5008-be83-5182dc2ec2c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523076} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23126074-a8ae-566b-ad2a-40e0588f8cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\pi}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523028} :answer-field/id #uuid "fba0ab4b-a383-5071-9d0a-ae7ee25d54f8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "af1539f2-8c56-5a03-b4a2-7d4037b0ecf8" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})+6,\\,y=6\\sin(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})-1,\\,\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\le t<\\frac{4\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-24\\sin(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=36\\cos(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n36\\cos(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{7\\pi}{9}, \\frac{17\\pi}{18}, \\frac{10\\pi}{9}, \\frac{23\\pi}{18}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{9}\\right) & =-24\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{17\\pi}{18}\\right) & =24\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{10\\pi}{9}\\right) & =-24\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{23\\pi}{18}\\right) & =24\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{7\\pi}{9}, \\frac{17\\pi}{18}, \\frac{10\\pi}{9}, \\frac{23\\pi}{18}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523073 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad1ca34-78e2-523d-911c-fa01d5fc3a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523021}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523021} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ea9c41f-be24-58d4-9192-6a384e99af4d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523024} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8191e8c4-c9d3-55c5-8230-73422d47d05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523053}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523053} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc59c592-b979-560e-9f61-df9e6c82b700" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c2913b10-d5b8-54ac-8c33-5b35f70e8000" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+8,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-8,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) & =-5\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) & =5\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8fb29d2-3496-55af-83fd-601b30be6313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523082} :answer-field/id #uuid "64162304-9344-5df1-b9e3-4a3a21b55152" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523043} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0dc50dc-6e5f-5e29-b38d-35a18468bf05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\pi}