id "9a0443d6-5de1-5cf6-a9fa-57df36d5b4d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{5y}{8}+2]}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535678} {:answer/id #uuid "b604d99c-c5fb-5b46-b2b9-bfe266992e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}({[\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}-{[1-{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535713} {:answer/id #uuid "ba912d5b-c16a-5c98-8772-74fb6eb18f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535719} {:answer/id #uuid "c18caf2a-05e8-5f0e-acf6-74e8d8e57477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}([\\frac{5y}{8}+2]-[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2])\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535731} {:answer/id #uuid "e2aca940-e7f6-56a7-a52f-f3fc13aea2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)-(1-2{y}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535784} {:answer/id #uuid "fc85b5f0-dcd6-58f0-b09d-4cc19f8fcf5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-(1-2{y}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f1ee210-e8c3-5b0b-b961-e2ea6bb04afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535464} {:answer/id #uuid "378a3f70-b83c-543f-8230-8a9c470b7cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[\\frac{5}{2}-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{y}]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535519} {:answer/id #uuid "60fcac8a-9636-51dd-b16e-559340914a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}({[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535586} {:answer/id #uuid "7e880303-2fe8-5c05-b664-1056f270eb21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535634} {:answer/id #uuid "9907a464-6ae3-5500-b762-ac9746b04080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535676}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535586} :answer-field/id #uuid "7988a85f-c1d9-5415-bd21-c53e94524cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0ad0e9c9-a63e-55c4-a91b-b9b32de9ed5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-50324" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0e8cbe1a26b5a012f6098b67320070e882fa38783d8eda07dd0b76011a47b21.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{1}{y}$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2}-y$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{5}{2}y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-\\frac{5}{2}y+1 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-5y+2 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(2y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2},2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ to $y=2.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/d5/d5067706f9ff1abbdfd9dfce94711cb854f79c1156ddc0d81c1c35a5ed555f7a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{1}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{2}-y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{2}-y.$\n\n![](images/a9/a944979bc352b0e40afea73836b22c1fb1513fabbc727fb60d4c96793ee6fbb5.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{5}{2}-y)-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{1}{y}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1eb04cf2f3ae0669dd82106b064dd822da618e9ca31bb3b96e4e30c71b7c3193.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}({[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f83b0c08-5ea8-5827-be29-e60a8c51de64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1250\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535815}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535815} :answer-field/id #uuid "10353d36-c2a6-5baa-bc0e-12fb4776c147" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0c99954c-8e68-5e26-befe-7d930f5b079f" :question/math-academy-id "q-351346" :question/problem "![](images/64/64b0f84b62adc0db3385a75d8fdc6e6707eb6778084b9e21de863ca6954c4c1d.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+{y}^{2}=25$ and $x+5y=25$ for $y\\in [0,5]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=25.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2} & =25\\,⟹\\,x=25-{y}^{2} \\\\ x+5y & =25\\,⟹\\,x=25-5y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f5/f5b7cafc0ad6ab7624c1274451f16ba2c3c9a111ac9dad3abd90f082cc42d9b9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=25-{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=25-5y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=25-5y.$\n\n![](images/16/166dbb3f75000dfc155abfc3452ff6874dab1542409edc7e4867c5a5ee73c0b0.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}{[\\,(25)-(25-5y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}{[5y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=25-{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/93/9322b6f4bc9a0e913670f8dcbc9657206c3ae6a30de2f26308054d75b67d0861.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(25)-(25-{y}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[{y}^{2}]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}({(5y)}^{2}-{({y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}(25{y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{25{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{25(5)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(5)^{5}}{5})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{3125}{3}-625) \\\\  & =\\frac{1250\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ed12692-e337-5916-a234-aaa8ffd8cb1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e-{e}^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535482} {:answer/id #uuid "50bbae27-58bd-5797-8bfa-c17fe6627c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{6}}{3{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535558} {:answer/id #uuid "7c336942-42ac-5381-b315-51098c5b5b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3-2{e}^{6}}{2{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535627} {:answer/id #uuid "9944fc8f-2117-5170-b12f-702d4177c803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3e-{e}^{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535677} {:answer/id #uuid "ea10fa2d-ab40-54c3-a266-d1a7f2cd6885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2-{e}^{4}}{3{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535558} :answer-field/id #uuid "4daaebdb-5eac-5f4d-bcbe-069ee00fd432" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1c089741-5544-5275-8ceb-bbd95cf5e4fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-124659" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(1,3)$ to the point $N(3,-1),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{e}^{x-y}\\,dx+{e}^{x-y}\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i-j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+t(3\\,i-j-(i+3\\,j)) \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+t(2\\,i-4\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(3-4t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t+1$ and $y(t)=3-4t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i-4\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x-y}\\,dx+{e}^{x-y}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{e}^{x-y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x-y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+1-(3-4t)}(2+(-4))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{6t-2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{6t-2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[{e}^{6(1)-2}-{e}^{6(0)-2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{6}}{3{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e74ed4-4bf4-58a5-82f1-bb5ffa3a7eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535474} {:answer/id #uuid "4bbbf7a3-60c8-55b3-8fe4-dad2f3bdc622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535545} {:answer/id #uuid "6899860b-81ea-5720-98fc-80926761f0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535599} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb453c5-9589-5071-99e3-645a2efd0834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535747} {:answer/id #uuid "e03abd1a-722a-5b07-a324-580b73ea930e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535780}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535545} :answer-field/id #uuid "da97b470-1a5e-578a-80de-9d4315049c8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "21f7ba2a-9a0f-57d4-9aff-030342b38301" :question/math-academy-id "q-86453" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the second of these integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u=1+{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }({-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(1+0)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b5bcc35-60c7-54bb-b133-39f6fc654277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535525} {:answer/id #uuid "67e852b0-b9c4-54a2-b205-8a42f1866dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535598} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1eabee-f679-5494-9115-f00cebaa9bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535683} {:answer/id #uuid "e77f7513-a64c-53f2-b5d3-d5040243a753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535791} {:answer/id #uuid "eb88f1de-5cdb-542c-9488-e6ab9bd38152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535683} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d5160fe-ce43-5a25-8b6a-5f217be58058" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "25934858-d636-5a89-8459-35c28b65815f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124728" :question/problem "![](images/ef/efe8084c2e5b9d5eff4f75dffc9acce77aa66ab0dc62c699589573d96f884d49.png)\n\nThe region above enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-2$ and $y=-{x}^{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-2.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2 & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/45/4514019be21deb238c92f557cc041aec9b5e6629bc9d7dcac133a7aa2aed89c7.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y={x}^{2}-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=-{x}^{2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=-{x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/61/611d10f83c76467c69a1510809738611962dd5d1e1c22a10b0cb3081d2aa52cc.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(-{x}^{2})-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y={x}^{2}-2.$\n\n![](images/68/683244c0b5be598da85a8875708f459d4f87319677111f1072c997d911075d68.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,({x}^{2}-2)-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}((2-{x}^{2})^{2}-({x}^{2})^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4-4{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}-{x}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4-4{x}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(4x-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi ((4(1)-\\frac{4(1)^{3}}{3})-(4(-1)-\\frac{4(-1)^{3}}{3})) \\\\  & =\\pi ((4-\\frac{4}{3})-(-4+\\frac{4}{3})) \\\\  & =\\pi (8-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49ef83ba-2eda-583f-b227-7dc00e66db76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535543} {:answer/id #uuid "50a9d952-ca58-515d-b1b3-d5444dbbbd57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535557} {:answer/id #uuid "63aaf731-d747-572e-8b0f-86051724c920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535592} {:answer/id #uuid "79a69091-2708-57af-a95c-cea22cfae627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535623} {:answer/id #uuid "87845c36-7288-53ed-a176-4fdc76ecaa99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535650} :answer-field/id #uuid "af40c564-a593-533d-9007-1735d6d363e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "41b5b51f-ba4d-5684-ad06-c34aebd809fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-70510" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for  $f(x)={x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{(2-1)}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{1/2-1}-(-1){x}^{(-1-1)} \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c5a170f-7065-5de8-8d67-32ce20245ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535603} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cd8ab93-f2fe-5eea-85cd-ce5fd45592c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5d03566e-e00c-5038-a04e-9a446a86a195" :question/math-academy-id "q-361829" :question/problem "![](images/77/778041d84c40044d6f047203a017bd64c0569bcfc9442afca9baa5160b494ae7.png)\n\nThe region shown above enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x}+1$ and $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-1.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x}+1 & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & =x \\\\ x & =0,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=4.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7c/7ce02bca7667a9c32971b7840ff2ba4b96ef3a5c9b18aceaeb5b9b713cc7b36f.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1.$\n\n![](images/6c/6cb557a57a189a426b6cccacd599562332b05aace5bbbfc5d996b0d53fed3359.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\sqrt{x}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\sqrt{x}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1.$\n\n![](images/26/26886afd61251cae273c086dba59dcfa912665cdb788b0c3ef866bf61907efa2.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\frac{x}{2}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{x}{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\sqrt{x}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{x}{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}({(\\sqrt{x}+2)}^{2}-{(\\frac{x}{2}+2)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x+4\\sqrt{x})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{1}{12}\\,{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}\\,{x}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\pi ((-\\frac{1}{12}\\,(4)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,(4)^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}\\,(4)^{3/2})-(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (8) \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3386990f-4a12-5bfb-bca6-dc705891815e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f4/f4f6a383a5aced3a181ad9b910a5e3990ee9a2febe2af6a8f27bb53a32f07c32.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535512} {:answer/id #uuid "7329dedf-72c5-5967-ba9a-7094f5e60b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b8/b84aa56a8e238e80e673ebec28dc9de1463f32f07fe0163650905e56a9ec0765.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535614} {:answer/id #uuid "855a8c4a-da91-54ff-97cc-06728b6eaf2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0a/0ab97722306686003c288a1dbf39038f0db6d28bedd6d92bb7626dbf18a637b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535647} {:answer/id #uuid "ddcee905-986e-51be-9e24-d58126a44580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/cb/cbbf6d9ef8a860214bf8ec1ae867184f96f783ea39288c68483af7984c3e0b1b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535775} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b70be3-592d-5923-9391-fcfd435fd44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8c/8cb47f7a93ce259ff03e04ecd848dd199edf26f357c8c82da29449302728187e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535820}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535775} :answer-field/id #uuid "00281969-c87d-5b23-a09d-d3467e250aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5e9dde9d-e070-5830-aa05-46ebbed13863" :question/math-academy-id "q-125558" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8730b1936d1ff9f0c8d3845a70327e351b23c7085f248bcb6e99e73f0cb8c9e.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the point $(8,-4)$ under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ can be represented by the function  \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(\\frac{1}{2}x,\\frac{1}{2}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(8,-4),$ we have\n\n$$\n(8,-4)↦(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4))=(4,-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(4,-2).$\n\n![](images/c2/c2c00f1380ce776da3fe4e1ab2b30c969d5d7436966d907db7a0ceb5f0a13f33.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0538fe89-8dc3-52a1-ae06-27b01e1b4965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535445} {:answer/id #uuid "55d1530f-3fa2-5a08-8bcc-98aa2c0fd192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535569} {:answer/id #uuid "972bf55d-1dab-510d-9213-5a9e7ecde914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535672} {:answer/id #uuid "e2aca940-e7f6-56a7-a52f-f3fc13aea2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)-(1-2{y}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535784} {:answer/id #uuid "fc85b5f0-dcd6-58f0-b09d-4cc19f8fcf5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-(1-2{y}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535826}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535445} :answer-field/id #uuid "58834ca7-0094-5902-ba0d-d4e72392d1de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6be7f528-8a2e-5e94-a08f-bb7c33bc8c26" :question/math-academy-id "q-125400" :question/problem "![](images/90/90980bb04eb7dd9906ec9a99ea3a1b7cfd9c00969bbd9b12c60fe0f80eb88840.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+2{y}^{2}=1$ and $x=2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)$ for $y\\in [-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equation of our first curve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2{y}^{2} & =1\\,⟹\\,x=1-2{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/16/16ca1c96786eb8e7ef12608f70d03b34b679e63d34ef41135b4fd6801bcccc27.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=1-2{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right).$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right).$\n\n![](images/27/276dea7b2d9f50e3fe171cceb64c1c7a12f84cfb18bdbe242102802ad9790f49.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\,(1)-(2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right))\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}\\right)]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=1-2{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/b6/b6c6fb140bb3eaf4c49cba76af157fb46a568bc2dc24e4763facede62720f844.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\,(1)-(1-2{y}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[2{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right))}^{2}-{(2{y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}\\right)-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535966 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f7c7324-2c11-5c83-acfa-b2ee47b3214b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{x}^{7}-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535483} {:answer/id #uuid "628bae58-14c7-519d-8c7b-b62c7c8633da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{x}^{7}-3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535589} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3b37bf-ff7e-534a-a633-94c74472b5b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7{x}^{7}-3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535745} {:answer/id #uuid "db9820bc-9141-5706-b4b9-696ff0f4d463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{x}^{7}-3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535770} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc03597-2e55-5dbe-b2dc-cac7ff614227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{x}^{7}-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535770} :answer-field/id #uuid "86ad7852-6087-5de6-850f-cd1888a6eb84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8a7a18a3-9c4b-5644-bbba-c016ec6ec070" :question/math-academy-id "q-70507" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for  $f(x)={x}^{8}-{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8}-{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =8{x}^{(8-1)}-3{x}^{(3-1)}+(-2){x}^{-2-1} \\\\  & =8{x}^{7}-3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{-3} \\\\  & =8{x}^{7}-3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eace44a-9542-5986-9bb4-4a121809282b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535551} {:answer/id #uuid "5184e724-e665-5cf4-9d18-27d961cdc95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535561} {:answer/id #uuid "537b4f55-d454-56dc-93a5-b86d06a5d655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535565} {:answer/id #uuid "71ee97c0-dcb3-5432-b6ad-3f697f647db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535609} {:answer/id #uuid "a39bc14a-c886-535c-9199-d7ab15d57b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-k{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535689}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535551} :answer-field/id #uuid "13b9af92-d428-5ecd-80c2-21192da8e8da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "907d5b64-98c1-552d-a43d-a699db06e688" :question/math-academy-id "q-119167" :question/problem "The volume of a sphere decreases at a rate that's proportional to its surface area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $k>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*Its **volume decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **surface area**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its surface area $S.$* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kS,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$ Now, using the fact that $S=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\pi k{r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{2}}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e428057-03fb-5069-bc10-8f485b235085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}({[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535462} {:answer/id #uuid "22a2ea8b-dab7-5699-8e84-0ecaba7bd180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}({[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}-{[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535490} {:answer/id #uuid "b604d99c-c5fb-5b46-b2b9-bfe266992e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}({[\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}-{[1-{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535713} {:answer/id #uuid "ba912d5b-c16a-5c98-8772-74fb6eb18f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535719} {:answer/id #uuid "de2ba3e0-ce17-5d40-841f-829521df6752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}({[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}+{5}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535776}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535462} :answer-field/id #uuid "279f0e25-70bd-5ea5-9539-e50f5ef9c3b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "923c9707-06d1-5031-b8df-221476502c14" :question/math-academy-id "q-30045" :question/problem "![](images/43/43cff74165732892223841f6780e7aa22c024e3b3c173e60ce15d8d6841a5a08.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-1$ and $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x$ for $x\\in [-2,\\frac{1}{2}]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=5.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7a/7aad98fe0513514aa8827bea4e9ffbac5b70bcb8da7923effd789988c81e286f.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{2}-1.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{2}-1.$\n\n![](images/62/623b59b7aca8b33c6149ac6b0916d1eeb425adc3f74568868383806cd9b03b12.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}[\\,(5)-({x}^{2}-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x.$\n\n![](images/c0/c0467a3bf02287589f897586a1358e4abb02759134b6b58622125319dc7331da.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[\\,(5)-(-\\frac{3}{2}x)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}({[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9e9c0db-7f76-520c-aaea-771c467b95be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2048\\pi}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535821} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c6f2fe1-3336-5fe7-90da-fba93d12c56d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "92412be4-49e1-54ee-8609-445cb6da4a1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-348267" :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc70e0f7c7ac82e399e520faa39cf04d252501da46834e88c06d8eba2b8d192d.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+{y}^{2}=16$ and $x+4y=16$ for $y\\in [0,4]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=16.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2} & =16\\,⟹\\,x=16-{y}^{2} \\\\ x+4y & =16\\,⟹\\,x=16-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f3/f39f9f399ea4d0e65a499f34d41158ddbaf5319a02e057e097b4f80c8197ba9e.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=16-{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=16-4y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=16-4y.$\n\n![](images/47/47d985510b37b7a14e82dfc20bc136694e7ba2e17172b35be45ebde88d238677.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\,(16)-(16-4y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[4y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=16-{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e5494005010f3cfb8fb8b116c4b8ca91e8d3bf2712ad45c60f0766a2021b19a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(16)-(16-{y}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[{y}^{2}]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}({(4y)}^{2}-{({y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(16{y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{16{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{16(4)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(4)^{5}}{5})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1024}{3}-\\frac{1024}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2048\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5859ef23-64ea-5f95-8062-f7664d1b6145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535576} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ab3c0e2-4bdc-50da-b2cf-5a837a9baa2c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "948c8637-9616-53cf-93bb-0432892cbe59" :question/math-academy-id "q-354500" :question/problem "A particle’s acceleration in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is given by the function $a(t)=\\sqrt{t},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds.  \n\nFind the average acceleration ${a}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the particle on the interval $[1,4].$ Do not round your answer.\n\n${a}_{\\text{avg}}=$ \n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the average value of the acceleration on $[{t}_{1},{t}_{2}],$ we use the average value formula\n\n$$\n{a}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{4-1}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2})|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 8)-(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{14}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{9}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average acceleration is $\\frac{14}{9}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c41717b-c907-5031-97ec-c6ceee1cef10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}({[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}-{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535501} {:answer/id #uuid "662f9b8b-3b6f-5917-96ff-e6a29123f219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}({[5+3{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[7-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535596} {:answer/id #uuid "79083731-d85c-58a3-ad1e-660ad2fcee5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{3/5}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x-3)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535620} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2b46ce-9749-5847-8d8e-c71feca07989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}({[7-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}-{[5+3{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535725} {:answer/id #uuid "ef590c46-ffb7-51f4-b8a4-b0f49dc57c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{3/5}^{1}({[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}+{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535501} :answer-field/id #uuid "527a8b8b-3d1f-5a1f-9fab-9a12c4893e26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a2e19710-89b7-5129-86a4-6f0be5f110ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-30047" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf2c500974f9c88751b9dec98cc53b5d3616b736f3ab0504adae5879cac2001a.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}-2x$ and $y=3-3{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [-\\frac{3}{5},1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=5.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/d5/d5593e8c1e1d544acb8824d9428823d3d6cef3971a1cedd4b087a7902b0e2239.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=3-3{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=2{x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=2{x}^{2}-2x.$\n\n![](images/bd/bddc78f683e28ec6e97fddb32f95dfdaaff27d3aafbdfc61f424857916f81a6c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(5)-(2{x}^{2}-2x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=3-3{x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/cc/cc049c46593baf1d96c04b95680d6b444ab9ceffc64d1f23f310f70e78684c14.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(5)-(3-3{x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}({[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}-{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11aebb25-3c41-52e1-bf66-1dcd7ee2ce0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14{x}^{6}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535466} {:answer/id #uuid "3e200482-d133-5eb7-8136-1d3336aff1e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14{x}^{6}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535528} {:answer/id #uuid "a80acf4a-b6f9-58b9-ac0e-e6ed94b44fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14{x}^{6}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535696} {:answer/id #uuid "a849b7f1-4748-5906-8130-819ebcc7baa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7{x}^{6}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535697} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1c08e7-f8dc-506c-9813-764d2dd4e5df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14{x}^{6}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535742} :answer-field/id #uuid "4419ee0e-8651-52b9-9b46-c0466ee66f3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a746a92c-9180-5bad-bfc6-5ca557baf9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-10292" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=2{x}^{7}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+2x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{7}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+2x-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2})+2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 7{x}^{(7-1)}+(-2){x}^{(-2-1)}+2\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =14{x}^{6}-2{x}^{-3}+2 \\\\  & =14{x}^{6}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a665b85-1226-5bdb-bb70-29b874a55538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/70/70ab8c2fe67dbf76952995240d2bdcf102111c2ce8cfe91850886e9a439dcd45.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535475} {:answer/id #uuid "c24f84de-5c00-518c-ab34-899bc789aed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/63/6390b39e47b76519fe13b4cb4ed8a666ec45ff81a3f26d5d737ba11e7b8a2fa1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535732} {:answer/id #uuid "cbef1f47-0b08-587d-9e20-b7fbcb525255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/90/904c2c513ad7899b0c031b469d4f0d3505cc95d5e365c9437408e6f9828906fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535741} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b6343b-0aab-5a56-b101-de38eae283b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c5/c5acbc87cedde74b975d86c528d66bd779dfc72778d178fc8eacca09c7ff9ad9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535809} {:answer/id #uuid "f91e2358-9481-5e26-8eb3-f2aff1529abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1a/1aebfca6500abd5c7298492121f0c087c2c1861ee4bd770c27867d7e37fb631a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535817}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535817} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa59387b-3aeb-5780-923e-0243d4eb6960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a76fa1c3-fa48-5812-bf8b-fa26865dd75b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125692" :question/problem "![](images/98/98e64a245737736bfc987fe9230b1ee3fc81eef4aedfea8c2e5e9bcf949f8203.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the polygon shown above under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $-\\frac{2}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "The dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $-\\frac{2}{3}$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(-\\frac{2}{3}x,-\\frac{2}{3}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the vertices $(-3,-3),$ $(0,0),$  and $(-3,3),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3,-3) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3),\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3))=(2,2) \\\\ (0,0) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0,\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0)=(0,0) \\\\ (-3,3) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3),\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3)=(2,-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following polygon.\n\n![](images/29/2921272ffd1c38c082bf2123086d8c7b19a910c91d132810042b86728b820f8d.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "561dec26-edfd-5f06-be74-04ce011e37e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535571} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d89cc03-11cb-5a33-a4d3-defd63d2cbf9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c00bebe5-d517-59a2-a7ee-fce25fbbc516" :question/math-academy-id "q-354478" :question/problem "A small rover moves along a straight track with velocity \n$v(t)=\\sqrt{t}+2,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds and $v$ is measured in meters per second.\n\nFind the average velocity ${v}_{\\text{avg}}$ on the interval $[1,4].$ Do not round your answer.\n\n${v}_{\\text{avg}}=$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the average value of the velocity on $[{t}_{1},{t}_{2}],$ we use the average value formula\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{4-1}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\sqrt{t}+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}+2t)|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 8+8)-(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\frac{16}{3}+8)-(\\frac{2}{3}+2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{40}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{32}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{9}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average velocity is $\\frac{32}{9}\\,\\text{m/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2612f47d-0452-52a5-b697-b1b879af646c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d2/d238f021b9afd090dbc768cae0a6516969a0b281e88175889c5c616874739f10.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535494} {:answer/id #uuid "2e1b842b-03ba-5e9b-84c1-8c4e10efb822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d6/d6c03d6d7cf77a6cf53bef74f08b823c4c53e60fa500aabeeead4b9fe6aac6d9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535503} {:answer/id #uuid "3604c806-fdc9-5cbd-952d-ce15ba543740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/44/44e6342a13efb1aff9a223a48c5da9ba41d04a5a2944fdf4bdb08b3ff947c47f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535517} {:answer/id #uuid "8f24f992-4e40-57bc-8429-0ac08457c506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d5/d535d5bb169ecd50b41de6900dca3fbaa3314f1b510145b0266b680e60f22179.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535664} {:answer/id #uuid "d7270f80-e748-5db4-a204-f36a17d9bcea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1b/1b6adf3c5a87eee1dc41b4b8bfe10303f8f11d9c84e992e71d51f62cb3cdd363.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535764}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535664} :answer-field/id #uuid "275fc850-3e33-59ce-9392-09c44d4021e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cac69546-49cc-507e-802a-92a175e045ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-125574" :question/problem "![](images/84/841dfcfabb27fe93002cb05c8d84ad9da2e0a9f6b810476e7964651e515e757c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the point $(8,-6)$ under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(\\frac{1}{2}x,\\frac{1}{2}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(8,-6),$ we have\n\n$$\n(8,-6)↦(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-6))=(4,-3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(4,-3).$\n\n![](images/53/53df2214415b81380c96af98b2d073e2a16480715386926603cd471eec66dec6.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535994 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215a3196-aa1e-53fe-ba9a-dd584d06fa02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14{x}^{6}-24x+\\frac{5\\sqrt[4]{x}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535484} {:answer/id #uuid "29372cbb-de9f-5045-bc68-a6f3e039aa5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{6}-24x+\\frac{5}{4\\sqrt[3]{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535498} {:answer/id #uuid "8e201c30-afed-5567-a27a-5f1d97078f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14{x}^{6}+24x+\\frac{5{x}^{-\\frac{1}{4}}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535662} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7fe356-6709-56ff-968f-0b948652e8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14{x}^{6}+22x-\\frac{5{x}^{\\frac{1}{4}}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535743} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f7bb4e-6f66-5b0b-ba5e-25005bb49ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14{x}^{6}-12{x}^{2}+\\frac{5{x}^{\\frac{1}{2}}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535785}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535484} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba01c00d-e4ab-58b9-976c-4280882e9d75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cd92c8db-d853-53b3-99d2-6ad9bcebe052" :question/math-academy-id "q-1861" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=-4+2{x}^{7}-12{x}^{2}+{x}^{5/4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4+2{x}^{7}-12{x}^{2}+{x}^{5/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{7})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4)+2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7})-12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4}) \\\\  & =0+2\\cdot 7{x}^{(7-1)}-12\\cdot 2{x}^{(2-1)}+\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {x}^{(\\frac{5}{4}-1)} \\\\  & =14{x}^{6}-24x+\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{1/4} \\\\  & =14{x}^{6}-24x+\\frac{5\\sqrt[4]{x}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4f9d099-d483-540e-9e95-fcbce5c20534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{56\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535693}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535693} :answer-field/id #uuid "32684ee3-c288-58d9-93e1-88b30f665d28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d651054f-0a78-573d-a5b9-95d84b459c37" :question/math-academy-id "q-361808" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5a242a5bec4be4cb58b77289ea2ccf49bf22a368c19ec18f10790a417bfbff72.png)\n\nThe region shown above enclosed by the curves $y=3-x$ and $y={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=4.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/25/255abcfb1a9034df297dc4939d945d8c3b1c4bd717cebde2844aa2a229fd5258.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=3-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b480a3bb7312a59edcabb133100bee1ebd5777664ea490b58744171e05e3083.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(4)-({x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=3-x.$\n\n![](images/20/20de93738463a5b04c0c5c0884d1db7d8867503931122c99a27fc82493b64edb.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(4)-(3-x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({(4-{x}^{2})}^{2}-{(1+x)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}-2x+15)\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}-3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+15x)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}(1)^{5}-3(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}+15(1))-0] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}-3-1+15) \\\\  & =\\pi (11+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{55}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{56\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29429d07-f776-589c-b450-b75891b15930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8b/8be8f57344a57823633011369efda0559726af65bac3af2e88ac5323ef653ac6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535499} {:answer/id #uuid "3fdd8d35-d9a7-5d19-b7a9-d4f954803563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6a/6a60e312e154995129aa750878dc41b5dfdb10d95af629e2ce96ff0469ecc63e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535531} {:answer/id #uuid "71ecab29-a47f-5134-9720-b57436f489c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9c/9c32a2847a8027942d8b8e275b470a63920bc05ace6866cbf60bfa4bdd3ef322.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535608} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9abd05-e3b6-58c7-a525-78bff06276fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/62/62b28c3c84e9cc5d4013eef8a75bb826e71fe63cacfc42d1ef96ccdad1dd1ab8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535720} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcdbe44-93f8-5fd0-b357-ad330a45faa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3c/3c2790d0b0fe66da1c1eab58ccea08db6991c3245af4308b1dd2fcb1da06adb9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535831}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535720} :answer-field/id #uuid "cefe190e-880c-5995-8a4f-2586cdd0f966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d78079e7-2f05-54ed-bf64-8723ddffd37e" :question/math-academy-id "q-88115" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a512ef7399ac46c611335a9f957bfae9a14e802ddd39e492f6184e0e149fb7e.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(\\frac{1}{3}x,\\frac{1}{3}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the endpoints $A(-6,-3)$ and $B(3,6),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6,-3) & ↦(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6),\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot -3)=(-2,-1) \\\\ (3,6) & ↦(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3),\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6)=(1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting points are ${A}^{′}(-2,-1)$ and ${B}^{′}(1,2).$\n\nFinally, we connect ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ using a segment, as shown below. \n\n![](images/ba/ba27f325ca104397544c7b5d19078d59cf63209a83d89de73cdeb752f5c0fc84.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0769fe07-7b97-54d3-9da7-2e16745b3d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535450} {:answer/id #uuid "09466455-4240-5580-81c8-13e20c58266d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{112\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535451} {:answer/id #uuid "21bd23c7-f81d-5056-b97e-1aaed7cf5025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535485} {:answer/id #uuid "300fc692-4366-5f31-b23d-7017bcadb5d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535505} {:answer/id #uuid "d607b83b-6442-5533-98d0-d815d6fc8ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535451} :answer-field/id #uuid "68f68768-c4e2-51d6-ae21-d67ca3a8019c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "db9dcd58-6ee1-5525-a1ac-d82114a9c0f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-86194" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6ba207cd34725ec2e3995eb0f90e63bedc8573300335b2d31163457ef8ee9bad.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2}+1$ and $x=2{y}^{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-2.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "First, to determine the limits of integration, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =-1,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$\n\nNext, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/41/41f8dff0676c5cf8952f46b174ff4f98deeb97eec272e417f145b4782770c156.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=2{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x={y}^{2}+1.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={y}^{2}+1.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4d3d75dfab4aa6382bf47f06a57036ec612a05c90d0764cd48fdc1f150f2453.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\,({y}^{2}+1)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{y}^{2}+3]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=2{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/6f/6f79c95e2b2d3aaf71e6dfac5ca93fa20261f5f40e1660cefc4ac23af3e56cf8.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\,(2{y}^{2})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2{y}^{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[{y}^{2}+3]}^{2}-{[2{y}^{2}+2]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-3{y}^{4}-2{y}^{2}+5)\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{3{y}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}+5y)|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(-\\frac{3\\cdot {1}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{3}}{3}+5\\cdot 1)-(-\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)^{3}}{3}+5\\cdot (-1))] \\\\  & =\\frac{112\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13772a60-d9e6-519e-81ff-a87b69705c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{5}({[\\frac{5y}{8}+2]}^{2}-{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535468} {:answer/id #uuid "1b1973fe-fa48-5311-9b7e-e2acae1001af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}({[\\frac{5y}{8}]}^{2}-{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535477} {:answer/id #uuid "4f48cd37-a024-5a0c-b78d-161e36a73060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}({[\\frac{5y}{8}+2]}^{2}-{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535553} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0443d6-5de1-5cf6-a9fa-57df36d5b4d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{5y}{8}+2]}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535678} {:answer/id #uuid "c18caf2a-05e8-5f0e-acf6-74e8d8e57477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}([\\frac{5y}{8}+2]-[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2])\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535731}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535553} :answer-field/id #uuid "a538906d-e42b-5de1-87a1-cf764b3258be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dd731e5d-ee05-5b06-811e-7a9b6ae9709a" :question/math-academy-id "q-49945" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1c22369a6f1abc89437de1977135208ef5d0e762a4971df1c9ace14b6af932cd.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{5y}{8}$ and $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{5y}{8} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =5y \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-5y & =0 \\\\ y(2y-5) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/50/504e204d73a1f87d72a321e7c0f58eadf01840ff3b8dbc5214f08e50fcbdcf9b.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{8}y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{8}y.$\n\n![](images/0c/0c43a01a8548112d8dc1f30f9059c560ac7bec1e679a6c76394d83e4bfbea668.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\,(\\frac{5}{8}y)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{5}{8}y+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8478ef13983e6529fa10147f9659171e31a5555cb799ec1fad13d1fe8b25191.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\,\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\right)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{5}{8}y+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}({[\\frac{5}{8}y+2]}^{2}-{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fd21d2-40f2-5b58-913c-e84aa062ff61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535439} {:answer/id #uuid "37db0990-fced-5795-aeb4-f498e0afce12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535520} {:answer/id #uuid "54aca898-1a55-5bcb-8d3f-340b7f07bc20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535567} {:answer/id #uuid "57595cac-95b5-5f6d-b989-b8d5bdf94881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535574} {:answer/id #uuid "c512d8f7-5585-5111-bc8a-cb18cd5fde29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535733}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535574} :answer-field/id #uuid "cee9f237-ef40-55b1-b748-bc0ff7960913" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e6367608-0b6c-56a6-854c-a12592ac7a14" :question/math-academy-id "q-124662" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(1,1)$ to the point $N(3,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+j$ and $b=3\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+j+t(3\\,i+3\\,j-(i+j)) \\\\  & =i+j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t+1$ and $y(t)=2t+1$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\cdot 2+\\frac{1}{(2t+1)^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)-\\frac{1}{2t+1}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot 1+1)-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1+1}]-[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot 0+1)-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 0+1}] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504eada9-0247-54fc-b6dc-cbc0f935842b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535556} {:answer/id #uuid "8af8d130-ce97-50dd-8f18-515090d74071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535657} {:answer/id #uuid "8bff95c5-eea5-547b-9c2a-215dee3b9aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535658} {:answer/id #uuid "9ace08dc-6b71-5df3-a9ac-066f0a3dea8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535680} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe67cfc-1a30-5c73-9315-623a9704cafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535658} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f225294-fdb8-504b-93be-81ef45509833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fc2bc671-1b50-5524-a44c-d196ad9a937f" :question/math-academy-id "q-85687" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0aa7d362596dcea976fead0467b9eb76c04b85c02c55dbd12e9a95652eda276.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y=\\sqrt{x}$ for $y\\in [0,1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=3.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=x & \\,⟹\\,x=y \\\\ y=\\sqrt{x} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f1/f15165acc18f4cf828671e3f9085274b26598a643be9eeb97be617272d7008d7.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x={y}^{2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/54/54820b3b386c9a435de04e0b3f07d4e2221a3e026ba5ba93ca25fa6640aa0ab1.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(3)-({y}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e1a6067500f81cabeac8dc43c4c585da608ea655667dc5e00f29a6fad0dda60.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(3)-(y)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[3-y]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({(3-{y}^{2})}^{2}-{(3-y)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{4}-7{y}^{2}+6y)\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{7{y}^{3}}{3}+3{y}^{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{7\\cdot {1}^{3}}{3}+3\\cdot {1}^{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "055f7f68-8860-5fcd-ba89-65573245d570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536053} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5b1be4-7c6e-5722-a2f4-2aa1b2aae130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536054} {:answer/id #uuid "6675c608-2d79-51da-8ce1-3d915d6d59c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{y}{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536055} {:answer/id #uuid "6e160412-70c4-5898-9627-1e775d2346d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{9y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536056} {:answer/id #uuid "8c584b97-62a0-5d84-bf7d-5b2abde11f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536057} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc377f79-c91d-5163-84d3-cf26a1ed63ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d7a2ead7-a103-561d-ae19-16273e0e5510" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4efe76bd06d22c5735b427c150f5a09b7288f8281b90921aeb11902c864176e5.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our ellipse is centered at the origin and has semiaxes $1$ and $3,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\right) & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2y}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{9x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c49a10-bf3d-5b30-aa62-004fc1530f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(- 12 \\pi - 32 - \\pi^{2}\\right)}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536068} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3c99e80-1859-5501-94e2-065df60ee30c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "33ab5b35-dd61-5afa-9e50-76ac2177002e" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- x^{2} - x - 1)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- x^{2} - x - 1$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- 2 x - 1,\\quad v'(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- x^{2} - x - 1)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})+(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 x - 1) \\\\ &=- x^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}} &=(- \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)^{2} - \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) - 1)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)})+(\\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) - 1) \\\\ &=(-1 - \\frac{\\pi}{4} - \\frac{\\pi^{2}}{16})\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot (- \\frac{\\pi}{2} - 1) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(- 12 \\pi - 32 - \\pi^{2}\\right)}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(- 12 \\pi - 32 - \\pi^{2}\\right)}{32}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536087 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e717ad5-c501-5b39-af7e-8878ba6ee6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(9 \\pi^{2} + 112 + 60 \\pi\\right)}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536063}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536063} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d6f8461-59dd-570a-8790-4ee15ed4552d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "89a63571-dce9-5ef0-8359-7d12fffc0179" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- x^{2} - 3 x - 4)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- x^{2} - 3 x - 4$ and $v(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- 2 x - 3,\\quad v'(x)=- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- x^{2} - 3 x - 4)\\cdot (- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 x - 3) \\\\ &=x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 3 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}} &=(- \\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)^{2} - 3 \\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right) - 4)\\cdot (- \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)})+(\\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 \\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right) - 3) \\\\ &=(- \\frac{9 \\pi}{4} - \\frac{9 \\pi^{2}}{16} - 4)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot (- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2} - 3) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(9 \\pi^{2} + 112 + 60 \\pi\\right)}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(9 \\pi^{2} + 112 + 60 \\pi\\right)}{32}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09dbf362-0ea5-5f52-a5a9-966c3c7d12bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(2 e^{2 t} + \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536102} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3fe6f8-cd4f-5ff8-9a88-a56b4315d98e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536122} {:answer/id #uuid "742a3fd6-b9c4-5147-a632-39b718c9a4c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536138} {:answer/id #uuid "9011cc4b-f8e1-5639-a6a0-59f04c5aa9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 \\left(- \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536148} {:answer/id #uuid "9a209fc2-c827-5e3f-995a-0fc60aebe583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\left(e^{4 t} + \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536122} :answer-field/id #uuid "78c7be4e-6cd5-5ed1-a27a-c153bf398a09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1cb8adf3-5059-5402-b886-e9c6e0d73f1e" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4xy}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{2t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{2}}{1+4({e}^{2t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4({e}^{2t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)}{1+4({e}^{2t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,j)\\cdot (2{e}^{2t}\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}+\\frac{16{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{2t}(2\\sin^{2}⁡4t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a57213b-4fa3-5117-81e3-a2d1f931c7b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536103} {:answer/id #uuid "0f22572f-894b-53df-a836-434bde50cdac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3 e^{3 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536105} {:answer/id #uuid "6cda11da-4b3d-5818-8261-a86fabce7b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\left(2 e^{3 t} + \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536137} {:answer/id #uuid "c38550f7-4952-57df-9ae9-77ce137fab61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(3 e^{6 t} + \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536162} {:answer/id #uuid "cd04a189-9235-59c8-a370-b182647b1c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536164} :answer-field/id #uuid "a578ac36-cb48-5e2b-b8a0-622f82cf0b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3e7834dc-d7fc-52ab-b7ab-d76e42910958" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{3t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{3t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}}{1+({e}^{3t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2({e}^{3t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}{1+({e}^{3t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,i+\\frac{2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,j \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,i+\\frac{2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,j)\\cdot (3{e}^{3t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3t}\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}+\\frac{4{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3t}(3\\sin^{2}⁡2t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cded62a-f1b3-5de4-935c-37ceeec7974e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(8 e^{8 t} + 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536104} {:answer/id #uuid "155e3333-446f-5203-8b48-f118c022d0a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536106} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8442c5-7b76-56f1-8d9d-dbbf442f8af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536140} {:answer/id #uuid "8935393a-dead-5e86-84ee-9daf21b07d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536145} {:answer/id #uuid "ed22cb74-f897-5443-861c-8e8078c31742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(2 e^{4 t} + \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536168}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536145} :answer-field/id #uuid "18ed12c2-f057-5824-bd7e-43c4cfaca9a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5a59dfd2-3508-59f4-99f3-603cc9ac3e42" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{4t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4xy}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{4t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}}{1+4({e}^{4t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4({e}^{4t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)}{1+4({e}^{4t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{4t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)\\,j \\\\  & =4{e}^{4t}\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}\\,j)\\cdot (4{e}^{4t}\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{8{e}^{4t}\\sin^{2}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}+\\frac{12{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{4t}(4\\sin^{2}⁡3t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ad3c34-7920-5a0f-aa67-8bf1dce8f6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{18 t e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 9 e^{2 t} - 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536107} {:answer/id #uuid "42690b01-2e49-5771-869f-15c475b0958c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18 t e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 9 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536118} {:answer/id #uuid "4609bfb4-e0ca-56b1-8aa9-80620eea6074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9 e^{2 t} + 6 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536120} {:answer/id #uuid "52f917e4-2460-586b-a26e-61222e1b7ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\left(9 t e^{2 t} + 2 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536126} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d46d3a-bccb-523d-885d-4d3c7e6255a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536118} :answer-field/id #uuid "66b25e82-59b6-5991-9378-8d3bf44fdfe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "699d81bf-486b-5fcd-891b-b8329a36d705" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x^{2} + x y^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+3 e^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + x y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + x y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x + y^{2})\\,i+2 x y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=3 e^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})+(3 e^{t})^{2})\\,i+2(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(3 e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =(9 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+6 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+3 e^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((9 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+6 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+3 e^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =((9 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}))\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)+(6 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(3 e^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}+\\frac{9 e^{2 t}}{t}+18 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =18 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\frac{9 e^{2 t}}{t} + \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b2d657-12ba-5244-855a-127cd7306b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 \\left(6 e^{2 t} + 6 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536100} {:answer/id #uuid "40d0cfbc-c3e0-5010-8a27-3ad659f00932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(6 e^{2 t} + \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536117} {:answer/id #uuid "a4cbccd4-10a9-5603-9d7c-31e8c4299e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 \\left(12 t e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 e^{2 t} - \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536152} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a2428f-4efd-534e-b8a5-9d7f8763b84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(12 t e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 e^{2 t} + \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536158} {:answer/id #uuid "dcff26e5-35a3-5a9b-8d7d-385dad335180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(6 t e^{2 t} + t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536167}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536158} :answer-field/id #uuid "07def19c-4051-567c-8664-2166dc149246" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "82600b93-632f-5ad5-9122-0f9e18ec5a13" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x^{2} + 3 x y^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+2 e^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 3 x y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + 3 x y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x + 3 y^{2})\\,i+6 x y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=2 e^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})+3(2 e^{t})^{2})\\,i+6(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(2 e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =(12 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+12 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+2 e^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((12 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+12 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+2 e^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =((12 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}))\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)+(12 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(2 e^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}+\\frac{12 e^{2 t}}{t}+24 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =24 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\frac{12 e^{2 t}}{t} + \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c65a274-bf1c-510e-93f7-af87e6992999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 t e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536109} {:answer/id #uuid "2e078f7c-7fb4-5408-83b3-9282edfe1151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\left(t e^{2 t} + 2 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536114} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd6b5b5-82a5-5bbc-af24-64251de049af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{2 t} + 2 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536142} {:answer/id #uuid "a5893d8d-8c9d-58d1-a951-4116dd536f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536154} {:answer/id #uuid "c165d35f-0e3e-54f0-bd59-5e02a049ced0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 t e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - e^{2 t} - 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536109} :answer-field/id #uuid "219519b6-e351-5891-9ab3-febd1842d844" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "870cb2f9-447e-5e98-bbd0-de46e4f5add3" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x^{2} + x y^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+e^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + x y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + x y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x + y^{2})\\,i+2 x y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=e^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})+(e^{t})^{2})\\,i+2(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =(e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+2 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+e^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+2 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+e^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =((e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}))\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)+(2 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(e^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}+\\frac{e^{2 t}}{t}+2 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =2 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\frac{e^{2 t}}{t} + \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23479d6b-0017-5db5-a587-dd7c374c7ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(5 t \\right)}}{16 e^{8 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(5 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536112} {:answer/id #uuid "530366db-79a8-50df-ad45-b44ecc227b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{8 \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(5 t \\right)} + 5 \\cos{\\left(5 t \\right)}\\right) e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(5 t \\right)}}{16 e^{8 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(5 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536127} {:answer/id #uuid "6bcda1e6-11d8-59b8-b27c-b1988618d72c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(2 e^{4 t} + \\sin{\\left(5 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(5 t \\right)}}{16 e^{8 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(5 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536136} {:answer/id #uuid "c1282498-7aba-573b-bc25-80c6fd8a1f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(5 t \\right)} + 5 \\cos{\\left(5 t \\right)}\\right) e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(5 t \\right)}}{16 e^{8 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(5 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536159} {:answer/id #uuid "d83ad5fa-5e5c-5504-9219-e20de2bf81b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(8 e^{8 t} + 5 \\sin{\\left(5 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(5 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(5 t \\right)}}{16 e^{8 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(5 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536166}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536159} :answer-field/id #uuid "572be089-a689-5cb6-88f1-0301b7c058df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8c9981e4-00b6-58ae-b640-e83086d82404" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{4t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+16{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+16{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{1+16{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{8xy}{1+16{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{4t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)^{2}}{1+16({e}^{4t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{8({e}^{4t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)}{1+16({e}^{4t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sin^{2}⁡5t}{1+16{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡5t}\\,i+\\frac{8{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t}{1+16{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡5t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{4t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)\\,j \\\\  & =4{e}^{4t}\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4\\sin^{2}⁡5t}{1+16{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡5t}\\,i+\\frac{8{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t}{1+16{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡5t}\\,j)\\cdot (4{e}^{4t}\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{16{e}^{4t}\\sin^{2}⁡5t}{1+16{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡5t}+\\frac{40{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t}{1+16{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡5t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{4t}(4\\sin^{2}⁡5t+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t)}{1+16{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25e4a664-828b-5860-8931-3f7a67e771ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(2 e^{2 t} + \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536113} {:answer/id #uuid "43863874-ca72-58e4-99b6-b2310513b7d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 \\left(4 t e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 e^{2 t} - \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536119} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9ceac7-621c-54b7-b41f-8994e3294d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 \\left(2 e^{2 t} + 2 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536123} {:answer/id #uuid "af473d8e-16e9-570c-81d8-5f12c2cd647c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(4 t e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} + \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536155} {:answer/id #uuid "c2579dd5-b29d-546c-85ee-76fc7fc743a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\left(2 t e^{2 t} + t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536161}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536155} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d12e6ff-94be-5dcd-84cc-d93391333c27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e3d3273-ab0d-559d-b480-c40a8896fe51" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 2 x y^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+2 e^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} + 2 x y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{2} + 2 x y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(4 x + 2 y^{2})\\,i+4 x y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=2 e^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(4(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})+2(2 e^{t})^{2})\\,i+4(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(2 e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =(8 e^{2 t} + 4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+8 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+2 e^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((8 e^{2 t} + 4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+8 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+2 e^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =((8 e^{2 t} + 4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}))\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)+(8 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(2 e^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}+\\frac{8 e^{2 t}}{t}+16 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =16 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\frac{8 e^{2 t}}{t} + \\frac{4 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fcbb94e-faef-5525-9470-4b6ce168bca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(2 e^{3 t} + \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{4 e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536124} {:answer/id #uuid "554fc290-ebd5-56da-99e2-35b578477e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 \\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{4 e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536128} {:answer/id #uuid "5849863d-28ec-5073-8260-c3b9880b8690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(3 e^{6 t} + \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{4 e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536130} {:answer/id #uuid "918aa55b-03d6-5a24-af2c-a013acb6995a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{4 e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536149} {:answer/id #uuid "d30558e5-dd40-58c6-a497-8b68fb6bb95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6 e^{3 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{4 e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536165}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536149} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e322836-255a-51e3-a105-98fd7d7b75f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c7286e11-a67f-5b3a-a6ac-713b2737570a" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{3t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4xy}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{3t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}}{1+4({e}^{3t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4({e}^{3t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}{1+4({e}^{3t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+4{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{1+4{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,j \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+4{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{1+4{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,j)\\cdot (3{e}^{3t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{6{e}^{3t}\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+4{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}+\\frac{8{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{1+4{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{3t}(3\\sin^{2}⁡2t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}{1+4{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536169 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51bb9ef3-9793-536d-83c9-dd2d55598726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 \\left(e^{4 t} + 2 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536125} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce67cdf-7635-55db-9f92-06eb0b47dfbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(e^{4 t} + \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536132} {:answer/id #uuid "6b18450d-20dc-57d6-a4dc-8ccb1ef0781c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(4 t e^{4 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + e^{4 t} + \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536135} {:answer/id #uuid "94efb8a3-edb0-515f-9afd-69942a6e2b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(t e^{4 t} + t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t}}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536150} {:answer/id #uuid "afb69994-495a-5e34-808e-984f82739d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 \\left(4 t e^{4 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - e^{4 t} - \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536156}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536135} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b802581-781c-5f13-9c9e-406f2d098414" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f6e87588-a6f0-5803-a115-953266b2efe6" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 x y^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+e^{2 t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 2 x y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + 2 x y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x + 2 y^{2})\\,i+4 x y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=e^{2 t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})+2(e^{2 t})^{2})\\,i+4(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(e^{2 t})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 e^{4 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+4 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{2 t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+2 e^{2 t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2 e^{4 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+4 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+2 e^{2 t}\\,j) \\\\  & =((2 e^{4 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}))\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)+(4 e^{2 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(2 e^{2 t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}+\\frac{2 e^{4 t}}{t}+8 e^{4 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =8 e^{4 t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + \\frac{2 e^{4 t}}{t} + \\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57f928b7-f3c1-52b5-b1f3-e51f2ac25685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{15 \\sqrt{14}}{26} + \\frac{45 \\sqrt{3}}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536191} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2f52fd-d5a6-5f97-8553-e4f43e399741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 70 \\sqrt{14} + 405 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536192} {:answer/id #uuid "6a2ab4f5-0f77-542a-ae0c-3c24c174ddd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536198} {:answer/id #uuid "de7178fd-a762-590b-a176-d5866a3e8366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{35 \\sqrt{14}}{13} + \\frac{405 \\sqrt{3}}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536231} {:answer/id #uuid "e54c1709-79ec-5e74-9903-bf87dcc8ae95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{35 \\sqrt{14}}{13} + \\frac{405 \\sqrt{3}}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536234}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536231} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f6f04f0-a3b7-5983-aafe-e6b7a4a0ae76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4598c153-f2b3-5aa3-bf7d-421ba3e4749e" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{3 y + 3 z}{x}$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t,t^{2},\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\rangle$ for $t\\in [1,\\sqrt{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j+\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,k$, then along the curve we have $x=t$, $y=t^{2}$, $z=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(3 t)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^2+(2 t)^2+(3 t)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{3 y + 3 z}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(3)[(t^{2})+\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)]}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right)t^2}{(1)t} \\\\  & =\\frac{15 t}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\left(\\frac{15 t}{2}\\right)(\\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=13 t^{2} + 1$, $\\text{d}u=(26 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{15 t \\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 1}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{14}^{27}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{15}{52}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{52}{\\int }_{14}^{27}u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{52}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{14}^{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{26}[(27)^{3/2}-(14)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{26}[(81 \\sqrt{3})-(14 \\sqrt{14})] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{405 \\sqrt{3}}{26}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{35 \\sqrt{14}}{13}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{35 \\sqrt{14}}{13} + \\frac{405 \\sqrt{3}}{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08930374-f1d2-5c84-b19a-2683f1af6485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 100 \\sqrt{30} + \\frac{1120 \\sqrt{14}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536173} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5c7801-aeea-5abc-8505-a02245d7ee46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{30}}{13} + \\frac{10 \\sqrt{14}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536201} {:answer/id #uuid "af8bd43c-2eb0-5278-8be1-06e077eda466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{50 \\sqrt{30}}{13} + \\frac{560 \\sqrt{14}}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536217} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e839ff-7be3-5f5c-93ae-9e4bc0b08ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{50 \\sqrt{30}}{13} + \\frac{560 \\sqrt{14}}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536233} {:answer/id #uuid "e74cf37d-93ab-51d4-a5b3-0b2e116e7daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536217} :answer-field/id #uuid "d79b1b5d-3a71-51d9-b679-1016c4c4285b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4eee203b-5512-5c93-95fe-15ef6820bb5a" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{4 y + 4 z}{x}$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 2 t,\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2},t^{2}\\rangle$ for $t\\in [\\sqrt{2},2].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(2 t)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,j+(t^{2})\\,k$, then along the curve we have $x=2 t$, $y=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}$, $z=t^{2}$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(2)\\,i+(3 t)\\,j+(2 t)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+(3 t)^2+(2 t)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{4 y + 4 z}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4)[\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)+(t^{2})]}{2 t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(10)t^2}{(2)t} \\\\  & =5 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}(5 t)(\\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 4})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=13 t^{2} + 4$, $\\text{d}u=(26 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}5 t \\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 4}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{30}^{56}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{5}{26}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{26}{\\int }_{30}^{56}u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{26}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{30}^{56} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{39}[(56)^{3/2}-(30)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{39}[(112 \\sqrt{14})-(30 \\sqrt{30})] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{560 \\sqrt{14}}{39}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{50 \\sqrt{30}}{13}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{50 \\sqrt{30}}{13} + \\frac{560 \\sqrt{14}}{39}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a24a14e-7ef5-5ad1-a61b-3b0c903f1bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{25 \\sqrt{30}}{13} + \\frac{215 \\sqrt{43}}{78}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536178} {:answer/id #uuid "32658cc0-fb41-54b1-adeb-b587a3ed9bd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 50 \\sqrt{30} + \\frac{215 \\sqrt{43}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536183} {:answer/id #uuid "52633d9b-e8c6-534c-a7d5-036ac4ee235e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536190} {:answer/id #uuid "771125d9-54a3-59c0-aaa9-f0634bc33656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{5 \\sqrt{30}}{26} + \\frac{5 \\sqrt{43}}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536202} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a182f5-b696-5167-9387-544100c2c212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25 \\sqrt{30}}{13} + \\frac{215 \\sqrt{43}}{78}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536178} :answer-field/id #uuid "e67895d3-d4ec-5187-b82d-db3c871037c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "610b886f-b7bb-5ac0-b2cd-087760dc82c1" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{2 y + 2 z}{x}$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 2 t,t^{2},\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\rangle$ for $t\\in [\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(2 t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j+\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,k$, then along the curve we have $x=2 t$, $y=t^{2}$, $z=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =(2)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(3 t)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+(2 t)^2+(3 t)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{2 y + 2 z}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(2)[(t^{2})+\\left(\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right)]}{2 t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5)t^2}{(2)t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5 t}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{3}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\left(\\frac{5 t}{2}\\right)(\\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 4})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=13 t^{2} + 4$, $\\text{d}u=(26 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{5 t \\sqrt{13 t^{2} + 4}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{30}^{43}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{5}{52}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{52}{\\int }_{30}^{43}u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{52}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{30}^{43} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{78}[(43)^{3/2}-(30)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{78}[(43 \\sqrt{43})-(30 \\sqrt{30})] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{215 \\sqrt{43}}{78}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{25 \\sqrt{30}}{13}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{25 \\sqrt{30}}{13} + \\frac{215 \\sqrt{43}}{78}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c94afe4-cdcf-5f7f-ad7d-644f56ba78be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536181} {:answer/id #uuid "6bcf5482-d032-5cb5-832a-7e160c6f0b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{5} \\sin{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{6} \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536199} {:answer/id #uuid "7c80f3d4-abe2-593e-a0b6-9a8763440675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536203} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2b1f95-0ba2-57cf-818a-fede800a91f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{5} \\left(-1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536210} {:answer/id #uuid "d4abf743-e772-522d-adfb-5c4592283ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536226} :answer-field/id #uuid "87810930-e74a-5f50-8241-46263ccebad7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c2ce718e-45c4-5f03-8361-b5c8663f79aa" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ along the curve parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2},- 4 t^{2}\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{6}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(2 t^{2})\\,i+(- 4 t^{2})\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=2 t^{2}$, $y=- 4 t^{2}$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 4 t^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(4 t)\\,i+(- 8 t)\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4 t)^2+(- 8 t)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80 t^{2}} \\\\  & =4 \\sqrt{5}|t| \\\\  & =4 \\sqrt{5} t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ because $t\\geq0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3 \\cos{\\left(2 t^{2} \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{6}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{6}}(3 \\cos{\\left(2 t^{2} \\right)})(4 \\sqrt{5} t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2 t^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=(4 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\sqrt{\\pi}}{6}}12 \\sqrt{5} t \\cos{\\left(2 t^{2} \\right)}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}3 \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\cos u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3 \\sqrt{5}\\sin u\\big|_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\\\  & =3 \\sqrt{5}[\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)-\\sin(0)] \\\\  & =3 \\sqrt{5}[\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-(0)] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)-(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab988cf-5693-58a8-b278-e05c02100c50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n}\\frac{2}{n}(4-\\frac{4j}{n}+\\frac{4{j}^{2}}{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536323} {:answer/id #uuid "242d5a8d-320a-55dd-af45-50bb63662fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3j}{n}\\right)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536336} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf153cd-dbf3-5f7a-9e7a-e147bed59a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{1}\\frac{2}{n}(5-\\frac{4j}{n}+\\frac{4{j}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536353} {:answer/id #uuid "36b76477-1bee-5cfd-af35-4972e78ad218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{2}{n}(4+\\frac{4j}{n}-\\frac{4{j}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536370} {:answer/id #uuid "48caa462-32f1-501c-83c0-0379aaf59677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[2{\\left(\\frac{3j}{n}\\right)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536396} {:answer/id #uuid "638558e4-10ff-5afa-8bdf-9b786167c462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5\\sqrt{-2+\\frac{5i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536453} {:answer/id #uuid "6c04b536-8c47-5b60-84d0-ad4f649d58d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[{(3+\\frac{3j}{n})}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536465} {:answer/id #uuid "7d93b2a2-ea70-5d27-a06b-2eb1b0c174f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[2{(2+\\frac{3j}{n})}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536493} {:answer/id #uuid "83db7859-91ab-56dc-83ce-dbb89d1f2482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n}\\frac{2}{n}(5-\\frac{4{j}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536500} {:answer/id #uuid "8a534c45-0804-5cd0-ae1c-1bc24876ba0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{5}{(-4+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536507} {:answer/id #uuid "9b62e5a4-9e97-5400-851f-d6ea027be307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{2}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2j}{n}\\right)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536534} {:answer/id #uuid "a6839a44-5f1b-5130-ad49-d3732b17ae53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5\\sqrt{-4+\\frac{3i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536550} {:answer/id #uuid "b39b6574-f45a-534f-b0b8-98abbfe720be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{5}{(-2+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536571} {:answer/id #uuid "c308f5cd-baeb-5d55-a3e9-11c3b9fac238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{(-4+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536593} {:answer/id #uuid "cc660814-0552-58be-a5ff-bf43da8972f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[2{(2+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536607} {:answer/id #uuid "ecacd70a-0d41-5ace-a88e-64b07dd6c585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[2(2+\\frac{3j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536659} {:answer/id #uuid "f7fd2c5c-cf9d-5138-8ac5-87eba6bf55c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{2}{n}(\\frac{4j}{n}-\\frac{4{j}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c148ce-3d4b-5f82-be22-94b1b7a941d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{j\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536358} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc7096b-e4d0-5551-8b4c-698c994185de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{j\\pi }{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536410} {:answer/id #uuid "5479d04f-11d8-52ff-a10f-c5daf44d5b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{j\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536429} {:answer/id #uuid "79aff7b5-6b64-51d5-8c15-e972a08c75b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{j\\pi }{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536487} {:answer/id #uuid "d48e153e-cc47-599d-abb7-f430aae869da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{j\\pi }{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536429} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9b02b04-3990-5647-8a7e-f4a0b6db0f71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a6becce-61cc-5b53-a5ff-f772c9293a84" :question/math-academy-id "q-73069" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ between $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $4$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're given that $n=4$ subintervals.  The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0)}{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{4-1}f(0+j\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{3}f\\left(\\frac{j\\pi }{8}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{3}\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{j\\pi }{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{j\\pi }{8}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a41fa7c-ff54-5e79-94c5-8f781a6e77ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{15}{4}x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536300}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536300} :answer-field/id #uuid "36a65f47-1d59-5158-bad0-5ab25b1d5439" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "151db205-6c32-596b-ad29-fa17f10dd215" :question/math-academy-id "q-129772" :question/problem "Write the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ about $x=0.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{3}(x)=2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nand the Maclaurin series for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=1-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{4}-⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find the expansion of $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we multiply the expansions of $f(x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ as follows: \n\n$$\nf(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3})(1-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{4}-⋯)\n$$\n\nLet's work out each term individually:\n\n* There is a constant term, given by\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\n* There is a single $x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}x\\cdot 1=-\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\n* There are two   ${x}^{2}$  terms and their sum is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (-2{x}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot 1 & =-4{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}, \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{4}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll other terms contain powers of $x$ greater than $2.$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ about $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{15}{4}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12436895-69cb-503c-8085-217ca08ec281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536313} {:answer/id #uuid "43b8f5fb-30a4-58cc-b827-a3c6c0f1627e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{2i}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536386} {:answer/id #uuid "4eec80dc-bf63-5e26-a70a-c9df0a844d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{4i}{7}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536417} {:answer/id #uuid "af0d5166-e6c8-504d-975f-ece6f9efdfce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{4i}{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536568} {:answer/id #uuid "d6cf2865-18ea-59ba-9dab-3d90e49f0104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536568} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ee4b5b3-c121-55ee-96bd-54041a866832" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "21c1c6c9-f8f1-50f1-a9ef-3d13b0e5c684" :question/math-academy-id "q-116626" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+2}$ between $x=-2$ and $x=2$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $7$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is   \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n=7$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=-2$ and $b=2.$ So, the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-2)}{7}=\\frac{4}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}f(-2+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{7})\\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}f(-2+\\frac{4i}{7})\\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\,\\sqrt{(-2+\\frac{4i}{7})+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{4i}{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536823 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b5bd4c2-f489-5064-969b-dfe5f50bb313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3i/n+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536533}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536533} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d35b0bb-b5aa-56f8-b1ec-f3250b4039ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "328da786-31ee-5734-9605-611059a862b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-346178" :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\frac{2}{x+3},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=7.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{7-1}{n}=\\frac{6}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{7}\\frac{2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{6}{n})\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{6i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\frac{2}{(1+6i/n)+3}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\left(\\frac{2}{6i/n+4}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\\,\\frac{1}{3i/n+2}\\,)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "575e949f-a2b7-5302-ade7-234c593749f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{175}{288}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536436} {:answer/id #uuid "84f09ef6-af8c-5278-868c-878e38fd43f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{175}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536501} {:answer/id #uuid "94da350d-d600-5d09-a5e8-a507d6bea83a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{288}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536520} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8049a3-094e-56a7-a9bb-cd14ccc9b2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536540} {:answer/id #uuid "e4be4b9f-6f9d-51ae-b85e-355d75cde324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{243}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536436} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e082909-a6e2-55c2-a4a7-4878e06b14ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "390d417d-9926-5446-a159-d0e16ff521f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-138954" :question/problem "$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}{u}^{-3}\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}\\,{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{32}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{175}{288}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16912225-3f70-5541-b574-3061e2e7b38e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{j\\pi }{3n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536320} {:answer/id #uuid "242d5a8d-320a-55dd-af45-50bb63662fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3j}{n}\\right)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536336} {:answer/id #uuid "761dcd9a-706c-5db6-a6b8-883ea8047a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3j}{n}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536482} {:answer/id #uuid "9b62e5a4-9e97-5400-851f-d6ea027be307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{2}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2j}{n}\\right)]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536534} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d1bf4f-0539-5436-aeaf-4b61a267639b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{j}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536633}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536336} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a1a980a-9817-5cc2-b963-bee9f053f5b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3994f797-7a6e-5428-aacb-fe36a9051f76" :question/math-academy-id "q-49364" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ between $x=0$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(0+j\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f\\left(\\frac{3j}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3j}{n}\\right)].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536829 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5ab0f54-2397-5b21-9e48-6a667621b28e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\frac{3i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536621} :answer-field/id #uuid "b815cf32-0620-5ec8-861b-849fda16a0a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3c8de7d3-85f5-5987-bc43-0d9881c2dbaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-346205" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[8(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=8{x}^{4},$ the lower bound is $a=2$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}8{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(2+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(2+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[8{(\\,2+\\frac{3i}{n}\\,)}^{\\,4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "638558e4-10ff-5afa-8bdf-9b786167c462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5\\sqrt{-2+\\frac{5i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536453} {:answer/id #uuid "8a534c45-0804-5cd0-ae1c-1bc24876ba0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{5}{(-4+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536507} {:answer/id #uuid "a6839a44-5f1b-5130-ad49-d3732b17ae53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5\\sqrt{-4+\\frac{3i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536550} {:answer/id #uuid "b39b6574-f45a-534f-b0b8-98abbfe720be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{5}{(-2+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536571} {:answer/id #uuid "c308f5cd-baeb-5d55-a3e9-11c3b9fac238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{(-4+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536507} :answer-field/id #uuid "64a07a31-890d-5a37-87c6-b4aa072b0cdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4bc9664f-fb9e-55ce-8ee2-e0e93f2cfcb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-73181" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4}\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4},$ the lower bound is $a=-4$ and the upper bound is $b=-1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{-1-(-4)}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\sqrt{5}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(-4+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(-4+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{5}{(-4+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ada2e3d-0769-5b5a-85e6-218cbeec1c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536348} {:answer/id #uuid "4b33b710-8754-5317-ad8d-edc5c3f9305d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536402} {:answer/id #uuid "725bc856-b7c0-56dc-9518-bdc1f5428c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536473} {:answer/id #uuid "cc05201a-8232-5a04-8ce3-e3ac354a9f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536605} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d4553a-62e1-59c9-8854-5587cc1e9c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536680}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536473} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6fa46cf-d1f0-5fd4-a76c-c62db6438a9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50970995-9b99-52ed-ae6b-c20b930d578c" :question/math-academy-id "q-9537" :question/problem "![](images/50/5034bb902aadd151cc1a28764e054dbdd6a694ad653f50557e5fc208c8ec2342.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above).\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following identity: $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}.\n$$*" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nUsing $x=\\theta $ in the above identity, the integral can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={2\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea68bf8b-4deb-5b99-92c1-18ea456b7de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536652}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536652} :answer-field/id #uuid "c43acc61-3f4b-58f8-9442-6e3ef8f460f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "548006ed-ff60-5fe8-9b17-af5147ac7aae" :question/math-academy-id "q-347972" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{12n+1}{3n}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12n+1}{3n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $3n,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12n+1}{3n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{12n}{n}+\\frac{1}{n})}{\\frac{3n}{n}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{12+\\frac{1}{n}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12+0}{3} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges, and its limit is $4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea371c3a-ee29-5549-a033-ba24d614dda7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{3i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536649}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536649} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7e966e-716c-5603-9e5c-a619cf8a9b2c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5a9d714c-fb16-5bf1-a044-d2aaa53f540d" :question/math-academy-id "q-346169" :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[3(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=3{x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{n}=\\frac{3}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[3{(\\,1+\\frac{3i}{n}\\,)}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98919bb4-2785-53dd-9466-f9d0288a76cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536528}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536528} :answer-field/id #uuid "408af374-202e-561f-90a6-1910a956b51e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f16bf74-fd62-5389-9de5-031fc7e1da5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-352935" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we find the hyperbolic identities corresponding to the trigonometric identities \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identities to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\cdot {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/2)}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡(0)+\\frac{1}{2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{4}{({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1/2})^{2}}-\\frac{4}{({e}^{0}+{e}^{0})^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(1/2+2)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{50}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69f4e2a8-d94d-54bf-b805-7fcfd592c5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{43}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536462}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536462} :answer-field/id #uuid "33cb2526-ce60-5170-b71a-6d4b10c2625e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37425270-d6a5-558f-b94a-d16bcf4ad148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536372} :answer-field/id #uuid "63d4de25-9c00-54d0-adea-350732c1c21d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536745} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bc6b863-cf8b-5177-8c7c-cac0897e6dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536350}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536350} :answer-field/id #uuid "eca13d5a-0f01-5d47-ac43-fe2e58fa3861" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7baabd1b-d935-56aa-a7ed-5d322543486a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129773" :question/problem "Use the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ from part 2 to find the fourth-degree Taylor approximation of $g(x)$ about $x=0.$ Find $g(1)$ using this approximation.\n\n$g(x)\\,≈\\,2x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}$\n$+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{4}$\n\n$g(1)\\,≈\\,$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "We have that\n\n$$\nf(x)≈{P}_{3}(x)=2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate $$\n\\displaystyle g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ by integrating the above polynomial term-by-term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ≈{\\int }_{0}^{x}(2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[2t-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{24}{t}^{4}]}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =[2x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}]-0 \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $g(1)$ using the approximation above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ≈2(1)-\\frac{1}{4}(1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{12}(1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{24}(1)^{4} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{12}-\\frac{1}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036a64a0-313e-560e-a52e-dc6b8481224c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{104}{255}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536289} :answer-field/id #uuid "59c3d70b-5195-5774-847f-b546cbff6162" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7d070173-586a-5be7-bd5c-a4be4b18eeea" :question/math-academy-id "q-352966" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}2\\operatorname{csch}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{coth}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}2\\operatorname{csch}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{coth}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{coth}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡16$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{coth}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡16}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(u)\\operatorname{coth}⁡(u)\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(u){|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡16} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡16)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡16)}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{16-{16}^{-1}}{2})}+\\frac{1}{(\\frac{4-{4}^{-1}}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{255}{32})}+\\frac{1}{(\\frac{15}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{104}{255}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48caa462-32f1-501c-83c0-0379aaf59677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[2{\\left(\\frac{3j}{n}\\right)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536396} {:answer/id #uuid "6c04b536-8c47-5b60-84d0-ad4f649d58d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[{(3+\\frac{3j}{n})}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536465} {:answer/id #uuid "7d93b2a2-ea70-5d27-a06b-2eb1b0c174f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[2{(2+\\frac{3j}{n})}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536493} {:answer/id #uuid "cc660814-0552-58be-a5ff-bf43da8972f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[2{(2+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536607} {:answer/id #uuid "ecacd70a-0d41-5ace-a88e-64b07dd6c585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[2(2+\\frac{3j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536493} :answer-field/id #uuid "057a26a6-55af-55a3-897b-914138a1eb9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8aa630f2-bd34-5568-baf8-ea73abca85e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73155" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{5}2{x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}2{x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=2{x}^{1/3},$ the lower bound is $a=2,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{n}=\\frac{3}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}2{x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(2+j\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(2+\\frac{3j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[2{(2+\\frac{3j}{n})}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dda24c2-846e-5ae5-9e55-258b14393706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536494} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa265d47-40f3-5a3f-9bf0-25685f2be3fa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536769} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ee09fa0-6224-5178-b8f9-36c108594771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536470} :answer-field/id #uuid "cebc456f-ee3c-58cf-96e7-c237efa764f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e8a0eda-6609-511f-9732-1a6312007048" :question/math-academy-id "q-175581" :question/problem "$\\frac{2}{5}x+4y+\\frac{5}{2}x+3y=${{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y$" :question/worked-solution "We collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}x+4y+\\frac{5}{2}x+3y &= (\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{5}{2}x)+(4y+3y) \\\\ &= (\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{5}{2})x+(4+3)y \\\\ &= (\\frac{4}{10}+\\frac{25}{10})x+7y \\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{4+25}{10}\\right)x+7y \\\\ &= \\frac{29}{10}\\,x+7\\,y\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8e489ef-e019-5aa8-aaf8-81867e60a7e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9+\\frac{3i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536552}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536552} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c68f43-b8a4-56b8-87d9-4c3c430d2953" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "94a24590-9d5e-597a-b9fc-4e27a31e8bcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-346215" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+8)^{1/3},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{((1+\\frac{3i}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{(\\,9+\\frac{3i}{n}\\,)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fcdb4ad-14d5-5f0f-bf9e-41acc5a1d22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536329} :answer-field/id #uuid "241e61f4-57a8-5907-834c-7042fc78b039" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9fa8b923-c944-5ab9-8f5d-a280f219b3bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-352916" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}{2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}{2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\tanh }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\tanh }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba7e32a-043c-5931-9dab-fc2b20f12800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3y+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536305} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2e65ff-a8f3-5761-bf62-d366e3c46fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536408} {:answer/id #uuid "7567addf-e4b0-589e-a11d-3a3a13e6546a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536480} {:answer/id #uuid "c19039df-bdb3-5ee3-9b88-548c6a17e19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536587} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f817ac-4342-536d-890b-3ceed2d07931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3y-4{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536678} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fb6b47e-6d7c-543e-a3d2-2ef7185b7ec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "aac8b725-2415-58f2-86b7-ef6ffd39cf4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-83853" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=3y-4{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(\\frac{3}{4}-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=4,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f737d4-4208-55a3-aa88-7222aded4409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536340} :answer-field/id #uuid "c87e6d3b-23bd-5b1e-bec0-bba379741512" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b13f329b-704f-5b15-801d-b08d9d90b1ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-352678" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following  identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3-{3}^{-1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371ed2b5-3cfd-5f36-ad25-78277503bf11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536371} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6e5545-ad9f-555a-83ad-2208b3d8fb3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536405} {:answer/id #uuid "57ae105c-8d7c-5650-bab1-27e1f5494f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536437} {:answer/id #uuid "62f42da6-b448-5558-a669-efbc711b9a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536451} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7c2265-44f1-5e96-9940-d8c787667e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536491}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536405} :answer-field/id #uuid "4002135c-7d30-54e3-97cb-3c714ed2683f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b4fa5f6e-c8a3-5731-a2a2-6fc871183cc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-97309" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3/4}^{4/3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{3/4}^{4/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536877 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66c4c321-c3fc-50a9-83a8-59c121c7db0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536459}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536459} :answer-field/id #uuid "371769d0-a8d2-5429-b519-c67818b5ff8d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bd25bce0-e06f-5534-8414-1587d88ad138" :question/math-academy-id "q-352673" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identities corresponding to the trigonometric identities \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}-{e}^{-0}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-{2}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{1-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a24661b-9376-5478-ba22-345b80fbea58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536531} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a779e2-c85d-5324-8778-80dd2097d44f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c94b3bf4-462a-5a97-a5df-84bb2aaaa4fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-346474" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\coth }^{2}⁡x{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identities corresponding to the trigonometric identities \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\,\\text{ and }\\,\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\coth }^{2}⁡x{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}-{e}^{-0}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-{3}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{1-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{3})}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1084ac63-ef9b-56cc-bcb8-ef50cf2a751e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536310} {:answer/id #uuid "1a58b002-6268-59f9-8d62-d5780db00a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536322} {:answer/id #uuid "78d710b0-36b9-5af3-afee-6ec469874bb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536484} {:answer/id #uuid "975f8bf1-29e1-5e24-8470-e16a6bceb947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536527} {:answer/id #uuid "e2169256-da89-5b10-b1ae-084da0651fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536635}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536484} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d7987cd-9189-59ea-8be9-6b86123a7745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d26b4fec-f259-5757-943d-c92ed32d5a4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-10859" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(2{n}^{2}+3)(2{n}^{2}-3)}{2n(5{n}^{3}-1)}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2{n}^{2}+3)(2{n}^{2}-3)}{2n(5{n}^{3}-1)}=\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $10{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{10{n}^{4}-2n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{9}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{10{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{2n}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4-\\frac{9}{{n}^{4}})}{(10-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-0}{10-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $\\frac{2}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56ae9a87-b6bd-553c-894c-fb7b9c46e795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536434} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1b5624b-d189-5839-a384-dfb6d3457d12" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d4fc19ee-fc09-58f6-b456-25b6d7c144cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-352684" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x})}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2-{2}^{-1}}{2+{2}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{2}}{2+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{\\frac{5}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab988cf-5693-58a8-b278-e05c02100c50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n}\\frac{2}{n}(4-\\frac{4j}{n}+\\frac{4{j}^{2}}{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536323} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf153cd-dbf3-5f7a-9e7a-e147bed59a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{1}\\frac{2}{n}(5-\\frac{4j}{n}+\\frac{4{j}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536353} {:answer/id #uuid "36b76477-1bee-5cfd-af35-4972e78ad218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{2}{n}(4+\\frac{4j}{n}-\\frac{4{j}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536370} {:answer/id #uuid "83db7859-91ab-56dc-83ce-dbb89d1f2482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n}\\frac{2}{n}(5-\\frac{4{j}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536500} {:answer/id #uuid "f7fd2c5c-cf9d-5138-8ac5-87eba6bf55c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{2}{n}(\\frac{4j}{n}-\\frac{4{j}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536370} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4c4f8a5-a724-5fb9-9af0-afee36d1582d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d9fc617a-f5be-5a74-8545-9128626b78b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-49383" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=5-{x}^{2}$ between $x=-1$ and $x=1$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of subintervals, ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$, and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nThe endpoints indicate that $a=-1$ and $b=1,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-1)}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(-1+j\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(-1+\\frac{2j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[5-{(-1+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{2}]\\frac{2}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{2}{n}(4+\\frac{4j}{n}-\\frac{4{j}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36b11b91-37c1-568c-9d1a-8a39694f67a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536369} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eb4c32a-3d20-50c8-8f9e-4bb89fd9ad7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ee29c156-d06d-572b-b229-09cda1e3a6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-352915" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}{2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\coth }^{2}⁡x}{2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\coth }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\coth }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca0fb74-d0d3-5f86-be12-a821fb96b41e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536445} {:answer/id #uuid "973d5ba1-9f1d-5d54-a5a6-681dae8dfc0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536526} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2648e8-3c45-5b47-8bbc-61b0f969add5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536578} {:answer/id #uuid "ddcaa7ca-0c0c-5d03-adf8-e0d7f6832969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536628} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7ed841-cc61-56c4-b2ed-0e53c44c292c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536628} :answer-field/id #uuid "0759db57-b8ce-5f50-b46e-8b442e52ee62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f593ac8e-7a29-5f43-82ff-aea84a5ba69c" :question/math-academy-id "q-123332" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(3n-2)(3n+2)}{n(1-9n)}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(3n-2)(3n+2)}{n(1-9n)}=\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-9{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{n}^{2}-4}{n-9{n}^{2}} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{n}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{9{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9-\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{n}-9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-0}{0-9} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2b669f-2579-5335-819c-e77eab944787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536346} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af736d1-f000-5f15-b738-443b7520d5c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f7758d7e-a5ba-50ee-a45d-ff68b3cc6226" :question/math-academy-id "q-352968" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{csch} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot {\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5/3}u\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{5/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{25}{9}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f0daa64-ab0d-5c7d-bc74-4d0c2bf2920a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6j}{n}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536418}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536418} :answer-field/id #uuid "da4b8589-0630-5be5-bada-40a46902ce0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fe245032-d155-5a02-8475-f1836177bbab" :question/math-academy-id "q-315710" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+1)$ between $x=-1$ and $x=2$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{R}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\n$$\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=-1$ and $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-1)}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(-1+j\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-1+\\frac{3j}{n})+1)] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6j}{n}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1898e89d-7f9b-5658-8c51-d493fe024120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537087} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea7e0b2-048d-501b-8ce8-6a30e5846097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y + 3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537101} {:answer/id #uuid "b85cdbbd-f0a1-5380-b88e-05b490cd1b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537119} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f676ce-6f87-51f1-a05e-777980795d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x + 3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537123} {:answer/id #uuid "e5db7b26-9071-561e-8c30-3a00b00da0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x + y + 3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537087} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa7f8d9d-bf5e-5054-b7fc-6882417c0d5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fa107303-abd7-5cc9-bd95-cc8524938adb" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2}=1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-3,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-3,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-3,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{2 x}{9}\\right)\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-3,0) & =\\left(\\frac{2 (-3)}{9}\\right)\\,i+(2 (0))\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\,i+(0)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{2}{3},0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-3,0)=⟨- \\frac{2}{3},0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-3,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-(-3))(0)-(y-(0))\\left(- \\frac{2}{3}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2 y}{3} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "1d1902df-1e1a-57b7-92b9-f031a1d54cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=2{\\log }_{3}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537289} {:answer/id #uuid "47e95c0b-938e-5763-8319-8c104318e174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡14x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537379} {:answer/id #uuid "a31c6409-3a2a-541f-a937-5134697763fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡14x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537537} {:answer/id #uuid "b867ae05-4768-52a1-a7ab-9ac578fa106a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={\\log }_{3}⁡(4x+\\frac{1}{2})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537587} {:answer/id #uuid "b95fcc5a-b97c-570a-b6aa-9be9f9c37ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=2{\\log }_{3}⁡(4x+\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537589} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ef0cbc-d0f2-5d87-96ee-056aed052a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={\\log }_{3}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4655caa9-d5c5-51c5-8bd4-8475855ddad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4({3}^{4x-4})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537372} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d74a14-bc3d-5d29-8730-61d8c4d650f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "04376890-0a44-56a8-b428-8110db81b255" :question/math-academy-id "q-250505" :question/problem "The inverse of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1\n$$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 4x-4 & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4x-4 \\\\ {3}^{{\\log }_{3}⁡(y/4-1/2)} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & ={3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4})+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=4({3}^{4x-4})+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc2ab74-5abc-5136-83a2-898739e322fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537248} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2e8257-bfe6-5e3e-9807-69a66ed6f4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537313} {:answer/id #uuid "31ae5f0f-b924-5d5b-a2bd-1c8a5115b5af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537337} {:answer/id #uuid "6961d82f-9d68-5c51-9119-5d2172d51b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537435} {:answer/id #uuid "b6be647a-f32e-58b3-b595-e16d7a2bc472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537583}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537583} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2943f3-7b0e-5a8d-9f07-ea94989cfcbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12bc09cc-29a3-5e9b-995b-e577844956a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-97085" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-16{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-16{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[1-16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(9-8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(9x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =[9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)]-[9\\cdot 0-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0] \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-4\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2({2}^{2}-{2}^{-2}) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dba3b70-c074-5458-a323-b19ef1d35432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537522} :answer-field/id #uuid "28a1cb41-1b5b-5d2e-ab6e-6d8b55c026eb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537772} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f2feab-92a8-538c-8fa0-8749a77b4343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537236} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5630480-4fe9-588e-bdf0-1ee399688150" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537844} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d3e5918-3dfe-5112-97f6-219db70ddf3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537390}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537390} :answer-field/id #uuid "db14b8e4-522b-5e2d-8024-a70ac890758f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12efb8aa-cbcc-5328-91fe-5d769b12ac7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-351902" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+3)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n {{field-2}}\n$)\\,+\\,$\n {{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =x$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({\\cosh }^{2}⁡x+3)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+\\frac{7}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\frac{7}{2}x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "128c44ff-5002-57ad-a45f-4e742d10c24e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+2{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537263} {:answer/id #uuid "14056c1d-1503-5c16-903f-a42353bc7679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+2{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537266} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2f4d68-d3cb-5e06-9684-ade0a7155990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537439} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcf0690-e390-5dbd-b7f0-70fd10a011b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{5}+{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537630} {:answer/id #uuid "d825bf38-2203-5280-977c-5f960b7c5773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537439} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dacd865-577a-5c58-b803-da35ca44d6b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1c959d7c-d68c-591a-a3b7-ea71063de12e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96466" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{x}^{3}-2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{x}^{3}-2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2[{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})]+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\cdot \\text{d}x+\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x+{x}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a24b83cf-35df-57fb-bfe2-9bb91087aee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537535} {:answer/id #uuid "b063029c-31ed-5fb8-bd08-c53673fc0531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537564} {:answer/id #uuid "b605189f-b236-5c14-b566-17cbfac34bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x+5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537580} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf14566-40b7-51bb-9c49-37e8d525189a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537673} {:answer/id #uuid "f920fbe8-305f-58a8-bcd2-bef18daf9ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537741}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537673} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1ca330f-ffdf-51b2-9a3c-b44ad29a0d13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1ddb9340-9af2-51a6-8581-bb4c21f42fab" :question/math-academy-id "q-97073" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (4{\\sinh }^{2}⁡2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =1+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta -1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sinh }^{2}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{\\sinh }^{2}⁡2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x-1)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2359ac3e-05f6-59cf-b866-9b382c762ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537304}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537304} :answer-field/id #uuid "72f2aba8-3ad3-5527-af94-606d696f3adf" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537806} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7db4dc2-4afa-5530-8d81-ba6a2b0a54c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537585}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537585} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc429605-87f5-544d-978a-0285dffc3203" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537850} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de0c8485-931d-5e14-9d0b-1416b5ec8693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537676}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537676} :answer-field/id #uuid "df5421c1-33c8-52c3-a934-b3f293d407a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ebb4c16-c258-518a-a825-ce276a761a9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-351898" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{3x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+1)+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{2}x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c78e8f45-6241-530a-8e86-768b85aa828e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537618}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537618} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e7444c8-c4e7-5947-a2ec-fcf6d2b56070" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "21325d3f-ea32-5619-afd8-5e491ef497e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-270354" :question/problem "Solve for $b$ where $\\frac{b+5}{b}+\\frac{b-7}{b}=6.$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $b=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $b,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b+5}{b}+\\frac{b-7}{b} & =6 \\\\ b\\cdot (\\frac{b+5}{b}+\\frac{b-7}{b}) & =b\\cdot 6 \\\\ b\\cdot \\frac{b+5}{b}+b\\cdot \\frac{b-7}{b} & =b\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\cancel{b}\\cdot \\frac{b+5}{\\cancel{b}}+\\cancel{b}\\cdot \\frac{b-7}{\\cancel{b}} & =b\\cdot 6 \\\\ b+5+b-7 & =6b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $b,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b+5+b-7 & =6b \\\\ 2b-2 & =6b \\\\ -4b & =2 \\\\ b & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $b=0$ as a solution. However, since $b=-\\frac{1}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $b=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5d1032-d8df-5131-9315-d27ce3d12daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537247} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8a5c91-67b1-5ae9-83b6-a14d25c55b45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537296} {:answer/id #uuid "46824a5c-5d89-5cc0-92ba-1b68ec6d9595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537373} {:answer/id #uuid "9e873ca7-af38-5f44-9341-1fb3e1ea8712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537525} {:answer/id #uuid "eb337268-12ae-51d7-ad27-55abc1397eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537525} :answer-field/id #uuid "600ccac0-481d-5135-af9b-c1f28719ac01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "37e413b5-1ec6-5915-aa78-dfcf790b6b22" :question/math-academy-id "q-126519" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x-{x}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{3}\\,dx-{x}^{3}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(-3{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -3{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}-3{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{-3{r}^{3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "143eeb18-acb6-58b9-9d2a-0e8621cf072a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537268} {:answer/id #uuid "3afaeacd-5ea5-5eab-9723-41fa0b9df340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537350} {:answer/id #uuid "805ee25c-bc46-5a2b-bb0b-a7850c3300e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537478} {:answer/id #uuid "a17a715e-bff4-5662-80e2-9bdfe2c285ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537533} {:answer/id #uuid "f92e502d-c078-59ac-9d95-4be2a12cda17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537350} :answer-field/id #uuid "a172fe47-3553-5b48-9c9c-d02e78168ac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "40abd682-d4fa-55d0-8dea-db00bcc211a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-109131" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(1,1)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+(xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/56/5673a3eadfa3c01dbb9e2c8bf497b17f34a834d950621afa7d480af625ce1e18.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+(xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=y.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28fa7678-f13a-5850-9ebd-3577ac1a0379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537311} {:answer/id #uuid "ae79307e-0a76-5408-ab09-a497ed81c568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537560} {:answer/id #uuid "b591f2b8-be38-5e17-9192-b6351f2a138c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537579} {:answer/id #uuid "d60c91b7-6822-585c-88c2-65de5a82f0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537654} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc29b15-264e-5331-b92c-70ab6eedaf01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537669} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b6833fc-e197-5043-abac-1f89f7ffca8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "46a23183-289b-59ee-a9e5-bbe0aa79406a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162603" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=4,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=y\\sqrt{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(z-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\sqrt{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[\\sqrt{x}]_{x=1}^{x=4}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a853eb43-35ad-5318-b3ea-fd258865a714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537546}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537546} :answer-field/id #uuid "98cc11c0-aee2-5fda-a252-3022cc314d32" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537825} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ab22a23-f9cc-56d5-95ac-279199c70b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537436}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537436} :answer-field/id #uuid "b45263fa-64ca-5fca-a711-c9b1f2ec9306" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537842} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0395874-ffc8-59ad-a520-d723c8af1b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537532}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537532} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ee657f-8a91-5462-a83e-e961791c1d5e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4b85bc81-6104-5328-b444-83c8973655d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-352792" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-4)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)-\\,$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =1+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta -1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-4)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1)-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(1)-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57dc0f26-61e8-54f6-8765-f4f9dd4d0d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537408} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2113a2-dd4d-539c-984a-b4939769ed41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537521} {:answer/id #uuid "c491e7f1-cd3a-54c5-a9fb-12f4835abac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537606} {:answer/id #uuid "e023c4cd-5ab4-51c0-8881-2596a66eaf02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537680} {:answer/id #uuid "e98c24e5-1c22-5064-93fa-bfdbc05fdb81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537701}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537521} :answer-field/id #uuid "69fd017c-8fd8-594c-96db-bb6def0c9f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "56f4d79a-05af-516b-82f5-f4f41e31d95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162598" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=\\pi ,$ $z=2,$ $z=4.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}={z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xz\\operatorname{cos}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xz\\operatorname{cos}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}y)+0 \\\\  & =-2xz\\operatorname{sin}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}y)\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{2}^{4}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{cos}y]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=2}^{z=4} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}0)\\cdot (\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "840a654a-86fc-5489-8324-6801e6feeda4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537482}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537482} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3fdce0b-aebb-517e-8390-5a6aaec5bb75" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537860} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70b18141-2bee-5ae8-b9c2-69d17c5a68b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537448}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537448} :answer-field/id #uuid "e84af63e-7ff1-5d27-8af7-764ee61799ab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6b367b19-8f35-5f68-a044-eb7b9764c38d" :question/math-academy-id "q-351903" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-3{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,-\\,\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =x$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-3{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =[\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\sqrt{2})]-[0-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1734e68b-49b7-5657-a8ab-e7fd14ec92c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537276} {:answer/id #uuid "6de2d048-caee-5e8a-baf6-c37fde229e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537442} {:answer/id #uuid "b0bcfa39-281d-566a-8e63-d65c56907c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537568} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a99a7f-7e6c-5818-a83d-a13f8c260bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537692} {:answer/id #uuid "faef0ce8-c4e4-53a3-9255-f04524119f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537746}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537276} :answer-field/id #uuid "215a951f-a6c0-593d-8a1c-917970be4ccd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6bf4783e-de85-5ad3-95bc-1eadaee336f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-96307" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (1-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x-{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0035feb3-cb79-5047-8afd-04eee047fe8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csch}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537224} {:answer/id #uuid "37efae25-c6d0-510f-8a84-02b69180da3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537343} {:answer/id #uuid "71d70417-7ef2-5fec-908e-11a78c840b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537451} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f2dbd6-7b66-5d4d-b301-a9bd61a52a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537542} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fc32da-9081-513c-b08e-78ad9623f9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537451} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7f36f1c-baeb-5d0c-a838-31d70af0c742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6c61796b-b24c-5403-8a9e-cd8bda63e7c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96405" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x={\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡(1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "620344d8-2da9-55c3-b8ec-d1845745c666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537423} :answer-field/id #uuid "52fbaaa5-e6e2-563a-8fc8-919869cd64cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7461b1f3-1aea-5d09-ab06-c9f25cb75e4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-348024" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡x={\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x={\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot (1-{\\tanh }^{2}⁡x)}{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot {\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}{{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡0) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}+{e}^{-(0)}}{2}) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{2+{2}^{-1}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{5}{4}-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51e15788-2fff-568a-a3d7-e43b6c609b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537400} {:answer/id #uuid "53242edd-a396-563b-8aa5-73886a38edb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537401} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb51c3e-f35f-58f3-8931-5422fe5d6f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537629} {:answer/id #uuid "edd19e76-dcf2-5721-a377-64323522bc6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537718} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3563a9-4100-54a7-b530-a404469d2003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537718} :answer-field/id #uuid "93fd6742-7170-5522-9954-f5c8bd0f65a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7b5373c3-faa7-5442-9a9c-2d5aebc96c49" :question/math-academy-id "q-165874" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡3x+2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta ={\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sinh }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x={\\cosh }^{2}⁡3x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡3x+2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2({\\cosh }^{2}⁡3x+{\\sinh }^{2}⁡3x)-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x-x){|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-\\frac{1}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df39945-f17e-5051-9124-626d55bf8a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537293} {:answer/id #uuid "64742e1b-bec8-5682-b5ee-105f5d4f7a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537429} {:answer/id #uuid "7532c4d0-18e0-53bb-a6b7-c9f7c5d650eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537461} {:answer/id #uuid "abfebc27-8159-524d-9a6e-cdfd59eb0187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537553} {:answer/id #uuid "befbf0c7-7d19-5ffd-943e-139e02963d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537599}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537293} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8e3c1c2-59cd-57ee-a592-7d15bb5c605d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "86933fa6-f8f7-5354-b325-40dd23c73f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-75921" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-2}{2x}+\\frac{x-4}{2x}=\\frac{5x-6}{2x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $2x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{2x}+\\frac{x-4}{2x} & =\\frac{5x-6}{2x} \\\\ 2x\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{2x}+\\frac{x-4}{2x}) & =n\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5x-6}{2x}\\right) \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{2x}+2x\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{2x} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{5x-6}{2x} \\\\ \\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{2x}}+\\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{\\cancel{2x}} & =\\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\frac{5x-6}{\\cancel{2x}} \\\\ x-2+x-4 & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2+x-4 & =5x-6 \\\\ 2x-6 & =5x-6 \\\\ 5x-2x & =-6+6 \\\\ 3x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Therefore, we conclude that there is no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027489bb-28e3-5dfe-a2b6-367b579e0221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537229} {:answer/id #uuid "2ceb2f6f-5d34-5be3-a06c-024dd0503854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537323} {:answer/id #uuid "2e591bdc-0ce1-5740-b9d1-67306bc53ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537327} {:answer/id #uuid "8090692a-b362-571b-91a3-0212386b7f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537479} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8501be-4fcb-577e-8819-117055fdb0d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537327} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e75216d-bcd6-569a-b621-c249dcbf26bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a581df95-2d51-59ad-a71c-d2138e307e87" :question/math-academy-id "q-3518" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{3-x}{3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nThe equation has the denominators $x$ and $3x.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,3x)=3x.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3x,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{x}) & =3x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3-x}{3x}\\right) \\\\ 3x\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} & =3x\\cdot \\frac{3-x}{3x} \\\\ \\cancel{3}x\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}}+3\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{x}} & =\\cancel{3x}\\cdot \\frac{3-x}{\\cancel{3x}} \\\\ x\\cdot (2)+3\\cdot (1) & =3-x \\\\ 2x+3 & =3-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =3-x \\\\ 2x+x & =3-3 \\\\ 3x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=0,$ we conclude that there is no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e31ec7e-c4a0-5af1-a007-a5f374623cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537294} {:answer/id #uuid "307eac12-8ffa-5b27-b7eb-9fa2f8ffbd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537332} {:answer/id #uuid "4697b16a-b954-5ce9-9fdc-2eb3d67c270a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537374} {:answer/id #uuid "4a79f07c-c8a1-549a-81cf-2a1164ba3d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537386} {:answer/id #uuid "656fd776-3d31-5e32-af9a-81ea6f3a0abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{3}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537294} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf819579-d6fd-53c1-af84-bcfb66bf0607" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "af0d631e-6f1f-57fd-bd84-ccd6cde384ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-126546" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}x},\\hspace{0.222em}x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/0c/0cb340ef33d4cc38081e3796b4c8e076f1499760c0a3ffeecb8123a41fefeec8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03b1168a-ec72-53fd-aa75-2d246631fb21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537232} {:answer/id #uuid "0d190ad0-5d86-58ee-99e2-6b1dcbd8e20d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537250} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9a665e-71e9-5ec8-bf10-4366e938cdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537477} {:answer/id #uuid "b06e5be7-dbe2-5334-99a2-daeab5470c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537565} {:answer/id #uuid "c764df4f-a1d7-5e04-a973-c2800addafd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537616}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537250} :answer-field/id #uuid "5596b996-55c7-584d-a670-904b75102451" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "affed96b-3c3d-54a9-a279-f8432cb1a012" :question/math-academy-id "q-114073" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can write this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43d13c05-6da4-5912-9d1f-919a238ff2db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{45}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537368} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1d0a7db-40e9-524e-a61b-ec49a93196d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bc295d1d-76d5-5f67-a844-a3b5be954577" :question/math-academy-id "q-352419" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(6+6{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(6+6{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{2}^{2}-{2}^{-2}}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{45}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd13cf7-7215-5f4f-8a7c-38c0559334ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537244} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb969dc-7431-591a-8333-b0ef3b0cc412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537500} {:answer/id #uuid "ae62e6b6-9922-57f6-be87-76d55ca80e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537559} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a7b6ab-39e1-5f20-9b17-720ce6944c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537573} {:answer/id #uuid "f890beb3-8209-55e8-a5fa-269d219fee48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537244} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fdc00ed-8996-53bf-b0b6-f7702bef817b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cd69e9f7-e929-5fe2-b3bb-4bb36c948590" :question/math-academy-id "q-345553" :question/problem "$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2\\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the third principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the third principal component is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6a86c9-5f1e-5b55-a779-0b27d17b9cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537241} {:answer/id #uuid "133a3fb8-a470-51e3-9aa8-75064677269e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537264} {:answer/id #uuid "48807227-d099-5e79-866e-1ce8d84016ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537382} {:answer/id #uuid "5c34cd29-1831-563e-a117-a4a9c3fbd985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537416} {:answer/id #uuid "858ba711-5287-57e4-a23a-9253523a5ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537382} :answer-field/id #uuid "345e42f3-499f-5ae0-9f6c-526a1f93142b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf4092b0-8e64-55b4-94ba-2a8ea4d2ac81" :question/math-academy-id "q-165889" :question/problem "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\cosh }^{2}⁡3x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2{\\cosh }^{2}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(2-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(2x-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x)|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =[2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})]-[2\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75c584e3-4715-56f3-957f-04a6776e7cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\ln(3)-\\frac{40}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537464} :answer-field/id #uuid "b73b1863-d6f0-5b10-b3fa-815bfcf7b139" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d0857a55-78b2-5e38-b682-a21c51974c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-352425" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2-12{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2-12{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[2-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(8-6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(8x-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =[8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)]-[0-0] \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\left(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\left(\\frac{{3}^{2}-{3}^{-2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{40}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47e95c0b-938e-5763-8319-8c104318e174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡14x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537379} {:answer/id #uuid "a31c6409-3a2a-541f-a937-5134697763fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡14x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537537} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d92fab-f086-5ced-9eef-34e5e4a830aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537660} {:answer/id #uuid "df8dd24b-0de1-55dc-806e-c2876017394b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537679} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc85635-96f4-5594-ba0d-743b2cf746b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡14x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537751}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537379} :answer-field/id #uuid "0174772c-e601-55be-94f4-65f166c6c8a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2739611-7ef4-5493-8191-67c702a22c88" :question/math-academy-id "q-97004" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x(2-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the double-angle formula for the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cosh}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =7x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡14x=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x\\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡14x=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x(2-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡14x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡14x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡14x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{28}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡14x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡14x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b599b3-e5fb-55be-8b4a-64cf19160660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537238} {:answer/id #uuid "89ae6ffb-c4b7-5567-8681-07205f0f211d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{2}{v}_{2}^{T}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537491} {:answer/id #uuid "c54575f9-512e-55ef-a0c7-e74be9d25000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{2}^{T}{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537610} {:answer/id #uuid "c6427590-8b6b-5345-bd73-92d79f31db5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537613} {:answer/id #uuid "feb49a45-3299-531f-989b-c646c0d9773a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537752}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537752} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a0ee814-fca1-58c8-8cc1-dbfe10fc9460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dcb3950f-e511-5b2b-8452-0fa93d40b2ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-345562" :question/problem "$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}| &  & | \\\\ {u}_{1} & ⋯ & {u}_{n} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}| &  & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & ⋯ & {v}_{m} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be an $n\\times m$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the second principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $m\\times m$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $m\\times m$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the second principal component is ${v}_{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "173e1c1a-757c-5fcf-be6a-91e7ca4875db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537278} {:answer/id #uuid "637488d7-0f63-5fe0-8698-1d6414fe8f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537427} {:answer/id #uuid "b33107cc-21e0-55e3-a66c-7183491e915e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537576} {:answer/id #uuid "f109c179-6843-5d73-a8d7-5f145c4e38c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537726} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7a5d0d-e5eb-5ce1-ad1a-caa3023a2a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537726} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b11927f-a39d-501b-8f1e-1dbd9842d17d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f095dc29-06a2-59bf-8997-6e524951d317" :question/math-academy-id "q-75936" :question/problem "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4m+1}{3m}-\\frac{m-1}{6m}=\\frac{1}{9m}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3m,6m,$ and $9m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3m,6m,9m)=18m.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $18m,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4m+1}{3m}-\\frac{m-1}{6m} & =\\frac{1}{9m} \\\\ 18m\\cdot (\\frac{4m+1}{3m}-\\frac{m-1}{6m}) & =18m\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{9m}\\right) \\\\ 18m\\cdot \\left(\\frac{4m+1}{3m}\\right)-18m\\cdot \\left(\\frac{m-1}{6m}\\right) & =18m\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{9m}\\right) \\\\ 6\\cdot 3m\\cdot \\left(\\frac{4m+1}{3m}\\right)-3\\cdot 6m\\cdot \\left(\\frac{m-1}{6m}\\right) & =2\\cdot 9m\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{9m}\\right) \\\\ 6\\cdot \\cancel{3m}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{4m+1}{\\cancel{3m}}\\right)-3\\cdot \\cancel{6m}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{m-1}{\\cancel{6m}}\\right) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{9m}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{\\cancel{9m}}\\right) \\\\ 6(4m+1)-3(m-1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(4m+1)-3(m-1) & =2 \\\\ 24m+6-3m+3 & =2 \\\\ 21m+9 & =2 \\\\ 21m & =-7 \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=-\\frac{1}{3}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa0176e-c098-5a31-a679-f78c82cde774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537242} {:answer/id #uuid "5c08b8f2-6ec1-5600-93e6-9f66c69f245a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537414} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8d62c6-d563-54dc-adcf-ee55a3efdfc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537471} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0efb8b-9b26-5242-b8a3-0bb76a38d966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537526} {:answer/id #uuid "e08be13a-a49a-561a-b2a2-30db269c341d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537526} :answer-field/id #uuid "48bde9ee-22aa-5b03-8cfa-0dd37dae0e93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f63f0268-1410-54a1-bbf2-06e90b1cc575" :question/math-academy-id "q-96997" :question/problem "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(2+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the double-angle formula for the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\n$$\n\nRearranging the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(2+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7a2e99-60fa-5845-b485-7f1ecf41d0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537243} {:answer/id #uuid "2db04f8a-deb3-5e11-91d5-eea128cb749d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537326} {:answer/id #uuid "b61f0de4-08b9-5e89-9aaa-a012a63e7f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537581} {:answer/id #uuid "c71bf5dc-237a-5908-9987-a1df036cb19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537615} {:answer/id #uuid "cf71e830-8356-5e8d-bce8-f9b93f8cc125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537635}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537326} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc35caa-71e9-53b2-85d0-1e3c73992105" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7541a79-898f-52f6-9741-385d5ef8e2c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75955" :question/problem "Which value of $n$ satisfies the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2n}-\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}=\\frac{7}{3n}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $n=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $2n,$ ${n}^{2},$ and $3n.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2n,{n}^{2},3n)=6{n}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6{n}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2n}-\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}} & =\\frac{7}{3n} \\\\ 6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2n}-\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}) & =6{n}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{3n}\\right) \\\\ 6{n}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2n}\\right)-6{n}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}\\right) & =6{n}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{3n}\\right) \\\\ 3n\\cdot 2n\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2n}\\right)-6\\cdot {n}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}\\right) & =2n\\cdot 3n\\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{3n}\\right) \\\\ 3n\\cdot \\cancel{2n}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{\\cancel{2n}}\\right)-6\\cdot \\cancel{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1-n}{\\cancel{{n}^{2}}}\\right) & =2n\\cdot \\cancel{3n}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{\\cancel{3n}}\\right) \\\\ 3n\\cdot (3)-6\\cdot (1-n) & =2n\\cdot (7) \\\\ 9n-6(1-n) & =14n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $n,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9n-6(1-n) & =14n \\\\ 15n-6 & =14n \\\\ n & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $n=0$ as a solution. But since $n=6\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $n=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ce934f1-749e-5c82-9be2-eb4043940d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537322} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb68e51-5ee4-5bd9-86b7-657995c28427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537360} {:answer/id #uuid "6248e345-a77f-5d0b-a6d9-29b049db316b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537425} {:answer/id #uuid "b2172526-cf2e-5a91-b2a6-d166613ff60b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537574} {:answer/id #uuid "dc07d439-b7d4-549a-97f7-0b5dc5b32343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537670}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537425} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f7254ba-8516-55b4-929d-3effa926b080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fa991cba-7607-5ad7-99b3-e6c6f2993b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115795" :question/problem "Solve for $q$ where $\\frac{2q-1}{4q}=\\frac{q+1}{3q}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $q=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3q$ and $4q.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3q,4q)=12q.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12q,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2q-1}{4q} & =\\frac{q+1}{3q}-1 \\\\ 12q\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2q-1}{4q}\\right) & =12q\\cdot (\\frac{q+1}{3q}-1) \\\\ 12q\\cdot \\frac{2q-1}{4q} & =12q\\cdot \\frac{q+1}{3q}-12q\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\cancel{4q}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2q-1}{\\cancel{4q}}\\right) & =4\\cdot \\cancel{3q}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{q+1}{\\cancel{3q}}\\right)-12q \\\\ 3(2q-1) & =4(q+1)-12q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $q,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2q-1) & =4(q+1)-12q \\\\ 6q-3 & =4q+4-12q \\\\ 6q-3 & =-8q+4 \\\\ 6q+8q & =4+3 \\\\ 14q & =7 \\\\ q & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $q=0$ as a solution. But since $q=\\frac{1}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $q=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036eaa53-7479-5120-8bab-05396fca46ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537230} {:answer/id #uuid "56203f17-53a8-5839-bf69-62f170218fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537406} {:answer/id #uuid "be5dfe54-d2c6-51bc-ba60-5b77415cb27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537597} {:answer/id #uuid "c7815962-506a-58a7-a401-291b446f0170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537617} {:answer/id #uuid "ead0b0b9-c829-5ec7-932a-5da8c8741b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537708}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537406} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce6d141-0aa1-5d3d-a0d1-784268e63420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fcede9b9-3aa7-543c-ba0b-831f64ff8671" :question/math-academy-id "q-148370" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky} & =x-2 \\\\ {3}^{ky} & =\\frac{5x-10}{2} \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{ky}) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ ky & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{k}{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{k}{\\log }_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).\n$$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38875fd2-c051-51ae-9cd4-a572e31fc61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537344} :answer-field/id #uuid "787aa0da-04c6-5397-99c2-28c45379391c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ff75b330-ecd4-5fa0-b36d-564d6c66e864" :question/math-academy-id "q-276869" :question/problem "Solve for $v$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v}=\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n$v=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We note that $v=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $6v,\\,{v}^{2},$ and $2{v}^{2}.$ First, we find the denominators' least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$). We have that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(6v,{v}^{2},2{v}^{2})=6{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6{v}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v} & =\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6{v}^{2}(\\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v}) & =6{v}^{2}\\left(\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}}\\right) \\\\ 6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{6v} & =6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6\\cdot {v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-6v\\cdot v\\cdot \\frac{7}{6v} & =3\\cdot 2{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6\\cdot \\cancel{{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{\\cancel{{v}^{2}}}-\\cancel{6v}\\cdot v\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\cancel{6v}} & =3\\cdot \\cancel{2{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{2{v}^{2}}} \\\\ 6(v+4)-v\\cdot (7) & =3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 6(v+4)-7v & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $v,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(v+4)-7v & =15 \\\\ 6v+24-7v & =15 \\\\ -v+24 & =15 \\\\ -v & =-9 \\\\ v & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $v=0$ as a solution. But since $v=9\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $v=9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36c72f44-8cad-59b9-b978-9486a3fb8e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{51\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538159} {:answer/id #uuid "5035e854-d3a5-5790-9eb6-eb2c68c9a904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538167} {:answer/id #uuid "617c9ac0-970c-54d5-982f-e31ce5878129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{57\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538172} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdec5d1-edeb-5afe-bba5-e721dfcadba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538176} {:answer/id #uuid "a844d8b0-bab6-5d45-822f-ff3c2b83d953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{51\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538191}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538191} :answer-field/id #uuid "8546cdef-82e7-5512-88cc-d73209e88c5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0093dc0f-6d13-5136-8a41-4ec719538cf8" :question/problem "![](images/57/573013be1dc56a1ae4d0765b81b8e94006b8402a6da9706b118d43e286fb8291.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}6y\\,dx+9x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6y$ and $Q(x,y)=9x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}6y\\,dx+9x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(9-6)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\frac{\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{17\\pi}{2}  \\\\  & =\\frac{51\\pi}{2} .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538156 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d51d6ac-4c27-54cd-b01f-880b60999800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "492\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538165} {:answer/id #uuid "55780946-8d44-5da2-bf53-437f89466c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "108\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538169} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba0634d-fd89-51ca-bf2d-52c72119346e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "192\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538185} {:answer/id #uuid "a36d97e0-3b01-5109-9dd3-61dfaf2cc216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "300\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538189} {:answer/id #uuid "b5bd23ab-0096-5f66-92c3-71109a692c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-108\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538197}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538169} :answer-field/id #uuid "1574ac62-0771-511a-8354-4c35bed7f7ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "28489039-fdfa-503c-aed0-769a751abddb" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1e97d150acbe82bf31d0a0e371122ae567d62279b66a5db2a3164180248a5e71.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-13dx+12x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13$ and $Q(x,y)=12x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13dx+12x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {5}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {4}^{2}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (25\\pi -16\\pi ) \\\\  & =108\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "338fccf2-f549-585f-af57-a920eb07c34e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-560" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538158} {:answer/id #uuid "4f0eacde-8684-5144-862a-ff9207abd200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-704" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538166} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9d1961-35d2-57dc-9b39-45b4e2119f1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-624" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538194} {:answer/id #uuid "b541b542-9c57-5006-bc4a-4c3a995d3adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "560" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538196} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f897dc-a433-57f6-aaa4-ca8f6e3515d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-976" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538158} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b981ed-6379-5393-ba8b-72c70879308f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3c303577-d49b-5f6d-ae6a-7c78499f5f3d" :question/problem "![](images/24/245fb5eea54d303236a038f3d1b8253bc6c2e70426bcf0e5f48a40704b47d669.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}9y\\,dx+5x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=9y$ and $Q(x,y)=5x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}9y\\,dx+5x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-9)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-4{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (16\\cdot 12-{4}^{2}-{6}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (192-16-36) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 140 \\\\  & =-560.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a326d88-270c-56a5-934b-0ecf38037461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2016" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538155} {:answer/id #uuid "8ead2f2d-0fbf-581f-a47f-ce631f9f623c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2520" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538182} {:answer/id #uuid "bb86ad3f-4859-534b-a0ba-424359b6630f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2184" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538199} {:answer/id #uuid "c22625c0-8ffa-57ca-b447-c4331e833add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538201} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b5dba7-939f-52a8-bc6c-dd82a8901707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538204}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538201} :answer-field/id #uuid "aca3e355-2de5-5a67-a052-93dfcc4e2cf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5c5f644e-f3a9-5505-9151-ceb09a68b40b" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1b2d83da42a1a95525ce3359e947800af884cd29f1ead6d9dbe4cee1c8dfb40.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx-6x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=8y$ and $Q(x,y)=-6x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=8,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx-6x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6-8)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-14\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-14{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-14\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =-14\\cdot (16\\cdot 10-{4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =-14\\cdot (160-16-4) \\\\  & =-14\\cdot 140 \\\\  & =-1960.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f16cfc2-f619-5e86-9b3b-77f85371764c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-240\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538175} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d7754c-2a5d-5712-9b09-74a2ab844f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538198} {:answer/id #uuid "d9350129-4bd1-5631-8b78-17fb3a4316d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "250\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538205} {:answer/id #uuid "e2985cfb-93fe-5a5d-a26a-0e73d7c15c3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "245\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538208} {:answer/id #uuid "f2669bc2-2483-5613-be28-070c44b1ac89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "240\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538213}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538213} :answer-field/id #uuid "58154caa-247c-59c1-b74c-77e6b506e7df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "874f8d64-a3fc-5a12-89b8-9d65bb0715ce" :question/problem "![](images/48/483ce10aae9978fbc4c1b5a71173ab10b52bca0b15c84f01d7adb7247e82e436.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-2y\\,dx+3x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-2y$ and $Q(x,y)=3x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-2y\\,dx+3x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-(-2))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {7}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 48\\pi  \\\\  & =240\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45b042c5-5ebd-5d31-a6cc-baf65a7e1e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538163} {:answer/id #uuid "7f99a411-d07d-5e1d-8fc7-8e4bebe92bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538178} {:answer/id #uuid "a27bcbcc-285b-5d7f-9591-69f818793844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538187} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d2bb25-05b6-5674-817e-51dd14f03467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1848" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538190} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b8573b-d9b9-5a64-bb38-a1d9124d5ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1344" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538212}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538178} :answer-field/id #uuid "c84981b5-b8c1-55f7-8be1-ae3f5a997562" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9687dcec-a46d-5800-a061-342d9450fae3" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a1b8a23b4fb0d081f9d88748735e02b3a173843e5c4a1a40e70e6ba4e7335ad.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-6y\\,dx+8x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-6y$ and $Q(x,y)=8x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-6,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-6y\\,dx+8x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8-(-6))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}14\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =14{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =14\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =14\\cdot (14\\cdot 8-{4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =14\\cdot (112-16-4) \\\\  & =14\\cdot 92 \\\\  & =1288.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047f2b36-8964-5e1c-ba40-c92c874a8db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2350\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538152} {:answer/id #uuid "433dda26-4bb9-5114-b487-b99e5b406903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1925\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538162} {:answer/id #uuid "461804c5-46bb-5d96-af8e-d970cc58a5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1800\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538164} {:answer/id #uuid "550a93f5-da6d-5738-b24c-952715ec8a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1700\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538168} {:answer/id #uuid "da4d1cc7-9202-56d5-99b4-8e92451ba91c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1700\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538168} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3960239-93b4-5860-9265-ba5ce587e842" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "aa14acb3-297f-5aab-a401-2c7b97eae563" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2039c49121a2da30efa96b7d456a043aa3860bc19399e67385c3f3268f6c0f8.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+12x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13y$ and $Q(x,y)=12x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-13,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+12x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12-(-13))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}25\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =25{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =25\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {9}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =25\\cdot (81\\pi-9\\pi-4\\pi) \\\\  & =25\\cdot 68\\pi \\\\  & =1700\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a0efce4-e40a-5e17-b861-06b7b2120749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "216\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538160} {:answer/id #uuid "66452399-e90b-503f-8285-f8443359f389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "510\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538173} {:answer/id #uuid "70f313eb-7bf3-576e-aabf-93fe0ff7e116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "78\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538177} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a4a1df-b5f1-5dd4-9b8a-e7cd9b8d7028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-78\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538188} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf58509-b20b-5513-9bd9-8e1fec479d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "294\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538210}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538177} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b9efb0e-1e55-59e1-a247-642db1fe26b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bf10ccdd-2f45-595c-a4ea-08a81d57feab" :question/problem "![](images/83/83357e8db109cd09ddcc51043caff588225edfd1b8f7b98dbb640a230ea7843d.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-13dx+6x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13$ and $Q(x,y)=6x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13dx+6x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {7}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {6}^{2}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (49\\pi -36\\pi ) \\\\  & =78\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12575b41-8b98-5c1d-826e-16aacaf6e698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "760\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538153} {:answer/id #uuid "32f53203-099b-5874-889b-f83efd77d8a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-684\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538157} {:answer/id #uuid "861fadab-9825-5be8-8460-de34cdfe93f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "684\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538180} {:answer/id #uuid "92135252-c7a2-5a2e-b8bb-edf11e6375d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "855\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538183} {:answer/id #uuid "ef813cc5-c174-5a71-87e1-585aabb2bfa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1178\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538211}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538180} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eaf85fa-58f3-5f10-8de7-57b73f7c609c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e22aa2a8-a144-5ad0-98b1-7417fbf28999" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c92f6dbb417fb4ff7bff5442d50d57777c74a9072c13bbc614f333f76e04d7f2.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+6x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above.\n\nThe outer boundary is counterclockwise and each hole boundary is clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-13y$ and $Q(x,y)=6x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-13,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-13y\\,dx+6x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6-(-13))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}19\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =19{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =19\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =19\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {7}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}) \\\\  & =19\\cdot (49\\pi-4\\pi-9\\pi) \\\\  & =19\\cdot 36\\pi \\\\  & =684\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "4f21857b-1659-5d21-9156-55464bc5827c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538401} {:answer/id #uuid "5a53a977-08c6-5df9-a860-2371d7ca93a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538428} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbbfe99-40b4-5a39-acb5-22411fdbd5b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538432} {:answer/id #uuid "7b11f975-7008-532f-86a6-34c7ddbed852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538514} {:answer/id #uuid "a541d1ea-9725-5c0d-81a6-8717209ba023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538627} {:answer/id #uuid "ac30b210-151b-52ec-9f43-9946b5b6bd5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538637} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e11cb3-9047-5ff4-947e-2cd0cb49d427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538755} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe3d04d-8a52-5a06-8112-4f61eaf69eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538771} {:answer/id #uuid "f5fa1e27-e6e7-5ee2-8cfd-06752c595b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538790} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8e96b0-33c1-525d-a438-9b2176a1d06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16da06c8-5818-5d83-b103-468e96d457f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538281} {:answer/id #uuid "28af7c9a-9d9e-5376-9c5b-6d7fa5e689d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$ and $(1,2,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538317} {:answer/id #uuid "84475504-c2ca-5622-a840-6d61ac41fdbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538534} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9f2d3f-0ff9-53c5-b3cd-a63b7c043f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,2,9)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538607} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7c6f53-ab06-5a01-be5d-aa83ddab04f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,2,9)$ and $(1,1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538317} :answer-field/id #uuid "560ffd1b-5a84-5953-b5a7-cc2f4832a155" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "278532bd-d5b2-5391-ac62-5dd37f8bae10" :question/math-academy-id "q-129553" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-12y \\\\  & =12(2{x}^{2}-y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =-12x+3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3({y}^{2}-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨12(2{x}^{2}-y),\\,3({y}^{2}-4x),\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}12(2{x}^{2}-y)=0 \\\\ 3({y}^{2}-4x)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=2{x}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({(2{x}^{2})}^{2}-4x) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{4}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of the last equation are $x=0,1.$ So, we have two cases.\n\n- For $x=0,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({0}^{3})-12(0)(0)+{0}^{3}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our first point is $(0,0,1).$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({1}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({1}^{3})-12(1)(2)+{2}^{3}+1=-7.\n$$\n\nSo, our second point is $(1,2,-7).$\n\nTherefore, the points where the tangent plane is horizontal are $(0,0,1)$ and $(1,2,-7).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2840fb0c-b24c-5ace-8c60-e5f1d39630ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538315} {:answer/id #uuid "5278ff3e-cb2e-5401-af52-b131b740deb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538410} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa711cb-bd92-5be9-85c2-c50e7b5fcea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.5\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538512} {:answer/id #uuid "9b96d177-b725-5a00-a09c-c5f9e4a3f622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14.7\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538600} {:answer/id #uuid "f476b44e-8168-549a-91d7-1e7d0fc87e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15.9\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538785}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538315} :answer-field/id #uuid "269b10db-2af0-5890-bf3b-16469d2146ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "371ac971-2ba8-5a04-9b8d-bbb2a2347b46" :question/math-academy-id "q-121678" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b18b1e3a686f70a4c583324ddc253e8d334f63f017639bd47f6f483d42582801.png)\n\nAn inverted conical dispenser has a diameter of $100\\,\\text{in}$ and a depth of $110\\,\\text{in}.$ If a liquid is flowing out of the vertex of container at a rate of $9\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/h}$, how fast is the height of the liquid dropping when the liquid is $60\\,\\text{in}$ deep?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=110\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the container, and $R=\\frac{100}{2}=50\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=60\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{50}{110} \\\\ r & =\\frac{5h}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {\\left(\\frac{5h}{11}\\right)}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{363}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{25}{363}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{25}{121}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9\\,000\\pi  & =\\frac{25}{121}\\pi (60)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -9\\,000\\pi  & =\\frac{90\\,000}{121}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-12.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water height is decreasing at a rate of $12.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371b1475-55c5-532f-b34a-de18250ba6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538346} {:answer/id #uuid "63b5b7ee-a0bf-5cce-8cd4-ef0e8b697d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538454} {:answer/id #uuid "792df15e-6b0d-5e4d-9e48-244de04a293b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538507} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4beebd-bb27-5b86-b030-66ca40a0d99e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538707} {:answer/id #uuid "dd22f80c-0bb5-52dd-af6a-2e0500564142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)+\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538346} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9bc71c7-9b59-5c65-af5a-72086d309520" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "41f4ba90-bf6f-5f13-ae98-77405268cba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-27725" :question/problem "Solve $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-1)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x+9)$ for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-1) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x+9) \\\\ 2x-1 & =6x+9 \\\\ 4x & =-10 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(6\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)+9) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-6) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\operatorname{log}⁡(-6)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1855bada-2725-59d8-a436-9eabac0d269a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-3}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538287} {:answer/id #uuid "a36db184-0ba8-51a6-a6e1-f3d0031f8eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538620} {:answer/id #uuid "a53549e9-4d6a-5b8a-9f0f-45fd33da51c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-3}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538625} {:answer/id #uuid "cd63ed22-de30-53b6-8fe7-0ebc4520cbe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-3}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538708} {:answer/id #uuid "d187b48a-c1be-5c6a-8137-357a4d0e0d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538625} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8996f52-0a1f-58ab-9d5b-d752f553fcb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42211531-3fd4-52dd-adb0-73d677948d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97907" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{6x-{x}^{2}-5}\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-{x}^{2}-5 & =-({x}^{2}-6x+5) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+5) \\\\  & =-((x-3)^{2}-4) \\\\  & =4-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator of our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{6x-{x}^{2}-5}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{4-(x-3)^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{2}(x-3))}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{1}{2}(x-3).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{2}(x-3))}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x-3))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-3}{2}\\right)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x-3}{2}|>1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d7c4290-9353-557a-a78e-c9968ff65543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x-2}{3}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538326} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce2b7d3-6d59-59f5-8ebe-05b2e387253e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(3x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538433} {:answer/id #uuid "88bb5d4b-0ce6-53d9-8497-039fb7fe2e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x-2}{3}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538551} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f32036-6948-5f43-a566-222721d70200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x-2}{3}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538681} {:answer/id #uuid "d37f6b69-2bf6-5e33-a159-45bc4b373ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(3x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538681} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe2698ca-7e20-5c2e-95df-9f69c77c58a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "46c80522-dca7-526c-a225-6cc3d9755240" :question/math-academy-id "q-97313" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{(6x-4)\\sqrt{9-(3x-2)^{2}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{(6x-4)\\sqrt{9-(3x-2)^{2}}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{6(x-\\frac{2}{3})\\sqrt{9-9{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{18}\\int \\frac{1}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})\\sqrt{1-{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=x-\\frac{2}{3}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18}\\int \\frac{1}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})\\sqrt{1-{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{18}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x-\\frac{2}{3})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡\\left(\\frac{3x-2}{3}\\right)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4659c64c-5190-52e8-8db5-eb121e204831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538377} {:answer/id #uuid "47e6eaaf-0488-5e0a-b6b7-4666b416074b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538383} {:answer/id #uuid "6e14df0f-9ce2-5965-bb91-1fbe22d21aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538479} {:answer/id #uuid "965fd1e1-14aa-51e2-add1-57ed1e1880f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538586} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3ee915-a0f5-5e10-b5c6-cadf0c09fd1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538672}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538672} :answer-field/id #uuid "f81fd95e-548c-5041-a689-fc8dd1a4f6b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "629a07aa-1bdb-505b-9cdf-8118c6579d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-136955" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+2}{8},\\,-2\\le x\\le 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{32}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{16}{32}+\\frac{8}{12}]-[\\frac{16}{32}-\\frac{8}{12}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e1043c4-9142-52b2-8992-91ad1d63e682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538327} {:answer/id #uuid "536a338a-3b08-561d-b437-b32195834bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{4}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538411} {:answer/id #uuid "61d84e0f-46af-534f-97ed-8a837fc6090c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{343}{8}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538449} {:answer/id #uuid "66f75452-5479-556a-a5d0-d9bcd562286f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{12}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538462} {:answer/id #uuid "868508cb-90da-5e04-b5e6-299a168b5301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{49\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538543}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538449} :answer-field/id #uuid "54f0d882-e045-51e5-805a-be34d10addf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "62d14dcd-2286-5ac0-9da8-8fd4144e472b" :question/math-academy-id "q-118657" :question/problem "Each side of a cubic room has length $\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{yd}.$ What is the volume of the room?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the volume of a cube.\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the side length of the cube.\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{yd}$ into the formula, we get \n\n$$\nV={(\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{yd})}^{3}=\\frac{343}{8}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the room is $\\frac{343}{8}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3225ce6-163f-5ab0-848d-eac7a030fd95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538780}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538780} :answer-field/id #uuid "380c73ef-343d-548e-9c09-eeac68deca83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75da158e-460b-5ea3-876d-2ea3f7a43576" :question/math-academy-id "q-346635" :question/problem "![](images/47/479e2f20fea6b5e22b11fa8f8e5015ad81a57a10ed43a53d5669b0363d33b185.png)\n\nA conical grain container has a height of $450\\,\\text{cm}$ and the radius at the bottom of the container is $90\\,\\text{cm}.$ The container is being emptied so that the distance $h$ from the top of the container to the grain surface increases at a rate of $\\frac{25}{3h}\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ Find the rate at which the volume of the grain is decreasing when the grain level is $180\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe volume of grain is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=450\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=90\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the bottom. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the grain level. We call $h$ the distance from the top of the container to the surface of the grain, $r$ the radius of the cone at the grain surface, and $v$ the volume of the empty space in the container. All of these quantities change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{25}{3h}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nwhich is positive because the distance from the top of the container to the grain surface increases as the level of the grain in the container decreases. \n\nWe want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when the level of the grain is $180\\,\\text{cm}.$ At this moment, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =H-180 \\\\  & =450-180 \\\\  & =270\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the empty space located between the container's top and the grain surface must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{90}{450} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {\\left(\\frac{h}{5}\\right)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{75}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{75}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}\\left(\\frac{25}{3h}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (270) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =90\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of grain is decreasing at a rate of $90\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f21857b-1659-5d21-9156-55464bc5827c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538401} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbbfe99-40b4-5a39-acb5-22411fdbd5b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538432} {:answer/id #uuid "7b11f975-7008-532f-86a6-34c7ddbed852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538514} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe3d04d-8a52-5a06-8112-4f61eaf69eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538771} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8e96b0-33c1-525d-a438-9b2176a1d06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538799} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e035c3a-1daf-5a2e-a5be-43c121a2a65e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87c6c320-8ad0-5a7a-8ea7-78e763a7875c" :question/math-academy-id "q-97781" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3}{|3x+1|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}+6x+10}}\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+6x+10 & =9({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =9({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}x+{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =9[{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{|3x+1|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}+6x+10}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{|x+\\frac{1}{3}|\\sqrt{9[1+{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}]}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|x+\\frac{1}{3}|\\sqrt{1+{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x+\\frac{1}{3}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|x+\\frac{1}{3}|\\sqrt{1+{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98179f33-36bb-5786-aba0-2e8a4ef467fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538592}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538592} :answer-field/id #uuid "377f3da4-4d13-5ed9-a0c5-00b4eaa5cff5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8ad57966-24c0-50f4-b632-7291950e6a6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-353118" :question/problem "Find the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of the geometric series \n\n$$\n-10-2-\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{25}-⋯.\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term ${a}_{1}=-10$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-2)}{(-10)}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bbc5aca-b66b-5326-80f6-72047f13c7d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{4}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538389} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd91ee3-171f-5ecd-a04d-ece67ed21978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{4}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538403} {:answer/id #uuid "50e631f8-7a68-5ed6-948d-c6cd8d569be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{4}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538406} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d77791-2d19-57bb-83b2-1fc061951977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538657} {:answer/id #uuid "babf514f-5d4d-5f68-a29b-d4e542505f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538670}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538406} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ba0e725-7b1f-5872-b603-123fc8faa2d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8dc0f52e-b98b-54ec-bcf4-1fa4702113db" :question/math-academy-id "q-98005" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{12+4x-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12+4x-{x}^{2} & =-({x}^{2}-4x-12) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}-12) \\\\  & =-((x-2)^{2}-16) \\\\  & =16-(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator of our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{12+4x-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{16-(x-2)^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{16}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{4}(x-2))}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{1}{4}(x-2).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{4}(x-2))}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{16}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x-2))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{4}\\right)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x-2}{4}|>1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05258157-23e2-526c-899d-ec024306a2de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538244} {:answer/id #uuid "08fd375d-6272-539b-937a-1c9b075b15f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{45\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538256} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf036d6-1a5e-5945-b9ba-cc1297978d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538294} {:answer/id #uuid "9f237ac4-89bf-501b-8ab3-6322efde53b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538610} {:answer/id #uuid "faf91b57-eac5-5ead-9e6c-cdc5db530bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{45}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538256} :answer-field/id #uuid "f86e5ed2-3fa8-525a-9f5d-79abb731828e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9d29b6dd-70c7-586b-8075-827c46bfd599" :question/math-academy-id "q-121589" :question/problem "![](images/ad/ad22dcd0793dbd4f7135598f94d3cfce514b8966bb18ea7edbe34058c208404e.png)\n\nA child is tearing the border of a paper party cone-shaped hat in such a way that the volume inside the hat decreases at $10\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The height of the cone is always two times its base radius. At what rate is the radius of the hat decreasing when the radius is $15\\,\\text{in}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the hat and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=15\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always two times its base diameter. So, we have $h=2r.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}(2r) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =2\\pi (15)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -10 & =450\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{45\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the hat is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{45\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3de78098-2f30-5690-a297-c93784039e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538358} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb7824d-c0c6-5aa8-963a-415d8fba6461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538443} {:answer/id #uuid "b514ce4c-c0a9-56cf-b77b-d76239980c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538653} {:answer/id #uuid "dce2f00a-8e3b-5f21-85eb-ac015d859b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538739} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb12994-1f15-5905-ac9f-a9912f0659fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.25\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538770}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538653} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3b0ce1e-2140-5aca-87cb-414958166a8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a327d128-352b-50f9-99e7-ff67c5f44d4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-31996" :question/problem "![](images/13/135ce8e277c0895d36972e12fb7df68447af79765c4acaaba6fb0e63fc498d73.png)\n\nThe height of an ice-cream cone is $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and the radius at the top of the cone is $1.5\\,\\text{cm}.$ Some ice-cream is leaking from a small hole at the bottom at a rate of $\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$ If the ice-cream level is $4\\,\\text{cm}$ at a particular moment, how fast is the ice-cream level decreasing at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cone. Let's call $H=6\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the cone, and $R=1.5\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the ice cream level. We call $h$ the height of the ice cream in the cone and $r$ the radius of the ice cream top surface. Both of these quantities, and the ice cream volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of the ice cream:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cone and the ice cream part of the cone must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{1.5}{6} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {\\left(\\frac{h}{4}\\right)}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level in the cone when $h=4\\,\\text{cm}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi ({4}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-1\\,\\text{cm/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the ice cream is decreasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{cm/min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7957cff2-b385-535f-994d-5aa194c21c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538508} :answer-field/id #uuid "77b9569b-ea54-5309-956e-f4f9379e5a45" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a403c1d3-ab4a-51b5-846b-0a043ce7d6fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-359420" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ffdfbbfcbc2a01e79fd9490f05744b2d0bf1d24dd616a0b318ee95acfa26e9ba.png)\n\nA cone-shaped pile of salt is being depleted so that the volume of the pile decreases at $5\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{hr}.$ The height of the pile is always one-half of its base diameter. Find the rate of the pile’s height decrease when the height is $5\\,\\text{m}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe height is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m}/\\text{hr}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the pile and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-5\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{hr},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=5\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always one-half of its base diameter. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{1}{2}d \\\\ h & =\\frac{1}{2}(2r) \\\\ r & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (h)^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\pi  & =\\pi (5)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -5\\pi  & =25\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{hr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the pile is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{hr}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ee4abf-f8a1-5118-9471-a54db96f45a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}\\times {\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538378} {:answer/id #uuid "69e93b7b-b1d1-5cb1-973f-7ccaf1e39a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{4}^{5}}{7\\times 5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538469} {:answer/id #uuid "cb92c9ab-11a4-5e5d-bb15-e3eb6dedabbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\right)}^{5}\\times \\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538706} {:answer/id #uuid "d0058a23-5040-59e0-a92d-9b29b1549ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\right)}^{5}\\times {\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538715} {:answer/id #uuid "d6feceb1-2fa4-5151-ba55-921a2c546541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{7}^{5}\\times {5}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538729}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538715} :answer-field/id #uuid "544faafb-45af-51f7-9886-1c44cb2fbaea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c32986e7-9010-5588-b701-72ad7388c062" :question/math-academy-id "q-68615" :question/problem "${(\\frac{4}{7}\\times \\frac{1}{5})}^{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise the product of two numbers to the power of $5$, we can raise each number to the power of $5,$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{4}{7}\\times \\frac{1}{5})}^{4}={\\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\right)}^{5}\\times {\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{5}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "477b4b85-509c-50c5-94e2-30b9c718e7a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-6}{3}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538382} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb6bcbe-491f-5c80-b732-790eda522c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-6}{3}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538476} {:answer/id #uuid "97f3f503-2f88-57dd-99bb-2fd2911f3dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-6}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538591} {:answer/id #uuid "a267b645-2e3e-5684-b73a-ee1553ef4a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-6}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538618} {:answer/id #uuid "d701ac5e-f7fc-59a7-8172-9671e49711d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-6}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538730}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538730} :answer-field/id #uuid "0edb72e4-359e-521e-a337-ecd9f317a1a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dc07885c-59ca-5761-a1b0-43a8e8cb6e2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98008" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{12x-{x}^{2}-33}\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-{x}^{2}-33 & =-({x}^{2}-12x+33) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}-{6}^{2}+33) \\\\  & =-((x-6)^{2}-3) \\\\  & =3-(x-6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator of our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{12x-{x}^{2}-33}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{3-(x-6)^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-6))}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-6).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-6))}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-6))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-6}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x-6}{\\sqrt{3}}|>1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539294 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1090fa4f-2de9-5a71-b762-f9c4b206e1c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539289} {:answer/id #uuid "640d0292-c344-5d5e-9a14-b447496b8f72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539311} {:answer/id #uuid "a376fd42-0744-52dd-a934-28272c20671d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539327} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e93479-2c09-5768-8f64-ed59388e0706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539348} {:answer/id #uuid "f27ea39a-86a0-5aeb-ab5d-b1302b3e4a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539351}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539348} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccb83526-61fd-5cdc-9d18-5c81f0f664bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "261bbf3c-0c9b-595d-8e0e-d4b85f4e6d85" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):1\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 2\\le x + 3 y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 2\\le x + 3 y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + 3 y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\le u\\le 3,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u}{3} + \\frac{v}{3}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} [\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left[\\frac{e^{2 v}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)-(\\left(\\frac{e^{4}}{2}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\int_{1}^{3} u - 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2} - u\\right]_{1}^{3} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)+\\left(-3\\right)-(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(-1\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{e^{4}}{3} + \\frac{e^{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d4f8852-8c2d-52c3-9bfe-2c07f2a7d249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{4}}{4} + \\frac{e^{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539288} {:answer/id #uuid "10c1298d-2a3f-5806-a08e-4abe4508710b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 e^{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539290} {:answer/id #uuid "62e518e1-7f15-50d7-b3d9-6f2e7e0ec706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{e^{4}}{4} + \\frac{e^{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539309} {:answer/id #uuid "b0895210-4fe6-59d4-81bd-4a7b6f99774c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539334} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ee4ba4-229e-5664-895e-136f3ef23945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539309} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c3a5904-bf08-5994-9567-c34370267b6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2d2b5012-62ca-5818-b5dd-c30729dfd6d5" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):2\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 2\\le x + 3 y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n2\\le x\\le 3,\\quad 2\\le x + 3 y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x + 3 y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\le u\\le 3,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\\\1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u,\\ y=- \\frac{u}{3} + \\frac{v}{3}$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D e^{2 x + 6 y} \\left(x - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} [\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} e^{2 v}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left[\\frac{e^{2 v}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{2}^{3} \\left(u - 1\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)-(\\left(\\frac{e^{4}}{2}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\int_{2}^{3} u - 1\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2} - u\\right]_{2}^{3} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)+\\left(-3\\right)-(\\left(2\\right)+\\left(-2\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{4}}{2} + \\frac{e^{6}}{2}\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{e^{4}}{4} + \\frac{e^{6}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4279263b-c0d7-5a75-9245-547ed9b0ca6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6209}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539300} {:answer/id #uuid "6e820050-7944-5473-b303-2ec464e7466f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539314} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a9ff0e-0926-5538-8866-cacb621fc637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6209}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539329} {:answer/id #uuid "ce456d12-162a-5dbd-9b9e-a859e73acc05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4435}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539343} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7f8432-2a60-57db-9845-37922668dac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15071}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539355}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539329} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4e2778-b9c5-5559-851a-c35614d178b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c0c650a1-6d32-58bd-afa1-47278ef42758" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_D x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y):2\\le \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}\\le 5,\\quad 2\\le y\\le 3\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n2\\le \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}\\le 5,\\quad 2\\le y\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\le u\\le 5,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x}&\\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left|\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}} & \\frac{x y}{\\left(16 - y^{2}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\\\0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right|\\\\&=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\sqrt{16 - y^{2}}=\\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\ne0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving the transformation equations gives $x=u \\sqrt{16 - v^{2}},\\ y=v$, so the transformation is one-to-one on this domain.\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2} \\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta \\left(u v^{2} \\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\sqrt{16 - v^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\iint_\\Delta u v^2(16-v^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ & =\\int_{2}^{5} \\left(u\\right)[\\int_{2}^{3} - v^{4} + 16 v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v]\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =1\\int_{2}^{5} \\left(u\\right)\\left[- \\frac{v^{5}}{5} + \\frac{16 v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{2}^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =1\\int_{2}^{5} \\left(u\\right)\\left(\\left(- \\frac{243}{5}\\right)+\\left(144\\right)-(\\left(- \\frac{32}{5}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{128}{3}\\right))\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right)\\int_{2}^{5} u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right)\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{5} \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right)\\left(\\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right)-(\\left(2\\right))\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{21}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{887}{15}\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{6209}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4498327b-6560-51a2-b3e5-e74c8480729b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539363} {:answer/id #uuid "4a95c0e4-a164-5d09-9222-9c149c45ab7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{122 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539365} {:answer/id #uuid "6e41dde2-dfa2-5092-b54e-8a8248dcf4a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{123 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539374} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c5a825-f639-5ea1-9df7-8def67861623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{122 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539382} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb39b70-4ea5-52bd-a660-df7df68c411f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{122 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539388}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539382} :answer-field/id #uuid "74e20894-f26f-5e9b-8de6-fcc616806ae9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4ddd94b2-cc71-504e-a7a5-1df422f9b408" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4{x}^{2},\\,4\\le xy\\le 5\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,4\\le v\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{4}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}{\\int }_{4}^{5}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{4}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}\\cdot (125-64) \\\\  & =\\frac{122\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49693d90-a88f-5cc1-8d9c-4cdbfd34c316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{203 \\ln(4)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539364} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce3c7dc-caf4-59c6-bc90-66911b31b855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 26 \\ln(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539370} {:answer/id #uuid "66c4bff9-f18e-5a0a-ae8c-565ea3df5f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 \\ln(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539372} {:answer/id #uuid "8b1f244f-0b28-5817-86a7-3ca216955106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539378} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dbc465-6dd3-5c31-801d-8e21abfc3c96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "78 \\ln(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539390}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539378} :answer-field/id #uuid "189e4d5c-7d67-5000-8ba2-67a9c86a5254" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5fc233a3-016d-5c4e-a242-9f02a02751f2" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 16{x}^{2},\\,2\\le xy\\le 5\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{b